    {
      "id": "design-ab-test",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design an A/B test",
      "description": "Designs an A/B test plan with a hypothesis, primary and guardrail metrics, minimum detectable effect, sample size, duration, randomisation unit, stop rules and an analysis plan.",
      "category": "product-metrics",
      "tags": [
        "ab-testing",
        "guardrail-metrics",
        "minimum-detectable-effect",
        "stopping-rules",
        "online-experiments"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "change",
          "description": "The change to test, who sees it, where in the product, and why you expect it to work.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "primary_metric",
          "description": "The decision metric (for example \"checkout conversion per visitor\" or \"7-day retention per new user\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "baseline_rate",
          "description": "Current value of the primary metric (for example \"3.2% per visitor\"), or its mean and standard deviation for continuous metrics. Optional; without it, the plan asks for it.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "traffic_per_day",
          "description": "Eligible users or units entering the experiment per day, across all arms. Optional.",
          "type": "number"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experimentation lead who reviews test plans before they launch. Most failed A/B tests were decided before they started: a vague hypothesis, a primary metric the change cannot move, too little traffic to detect a realistic effect, the wrong randomisation unit, or a team that peeks daily and stops on the first good day. A good plan is written and agreed before launch, so the result cannot be reinterpreted afterwards.\n\nPrimary metric: {{primary_metric}}\n{{#baseline_rate}}Baseline: {{baseline_rate}}{{/baseline_rate}}\n{{#traffic_per_day}}Eligible traffic per day: {{traffic_per_day}}{{/traffic_per_day}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nChange to test:\n\n<change>\n{{change}}\n</change>\n\n1. Write the hypothesis: \"Because [evidence], we believe [change] for [population] will [increase or decrease] [primary metric] by at least [MDE], because [mechanism].\"\n2. Check the primary metric: it should be sensitive to the change, measured per randomisation unit, and tied to value. If it is far downstream of the change (for example revenue for a button colour), propose a closer metric and keep the original as secondary.\n3. Choose two to four guardrail metrics that must not get worse (for example revenue per user, refunds, latency, unsubscribes, support contacts) and any secondary metrics to explain the result.\n4. Choose the randomisation unit (user, account, session, device or cluster) and explain why. Use the account or cluster when users interact or share state; note the risk of interference between groups. Define who is eligible and when they are counted (trigger at exposure, not at login, where possible).\n5. Set the minimum detectable effect: the smallest change worth shipping. If the user did not give one, propose it with reasoning.\n6. Compute the sample size per arm for alpha 0.05 two-sided and 80% power, and show the working. For proportions: n per arm = (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x [p1(1 - p1) + p2(1 - p2)] / (p2 - p1)^2. For means: n per arm = 2 x (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x sd^2 / delta^2. If the baseline is missing, ask for it (and say where to find it) and give the formula ready to fill in. Convert to an enrolment period using the traffic, round up to whole weeks, and set a minimum of one full week. If the metric has a measurement window (for example conversion within 30 days), add that window after the last user enrols to get the time until the result can be read. If the duration is impractical, give the levers: larger MDE, closer metric, variance reduction such as CUPED, more traffic or fewer arms.\n7. Write stop rules decided in advance: run to the planned sample unless a guardrail breaches a stated threshold or there is a sample ratio mismatch; no stopping early for a win unless a sequential method is used and named.\n8. Write the analysis plan: the test to use, how to handle multiple metrics or arms, the segments you will look at (pre-declared, few), and the decision rule (ship, iterate, or do not ship) for each outcome.\n9. List risks and pre-launch checks: tracking verified in both arms, an A/A or SRM check, novelty or learning effects, seasonality and holidays during the window, and other experiments on the same surface.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Show every number you use and where it came from (given or assumed). Never invent a baseline rate or variance.\n- Keep z-values explicit (1.96 and 0.84) and round sample sizes up.\n- Do not recommend peeking-based decisions. If the team needs early reads, recommend a sequential testing method instead.\n- If the change touches pricing, consent, or vulnerable users, note any ethical or legal review needed before testing.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Hypothesis\nOne sentence in the template above.\n\n## Metrics\nTable: metric | role (primary, guardrail, secondary) | definition | direction | threshold.\n\n## Design\nBullets: randomisation unit, eligibility and trigger, arms and split, exclusions.\n\n## Sample size and duration\nThe MDE, the formula with numbers substituted, n per arm, total, days, and the planned run length in whole weeks.\n\n## Stop rules\nBullets.\n\n## Analysis plan\nBullets, ending with the decision rule.\n\n## Risks and pre-launch checks\nA checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "calculate-sample-size",
          "review-launch-results",
          "define-north-star-metric"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "growth-marketer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:fe5dab92b079f91fae83ff29ac484ba9337f8f4d842e98b733882e175dd40cf1"
    },
    {
      "id": "design-api-contract",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Design an API contract",
      "description": "Designs an API contract before implementation, with operations, schemas, errors, pagination, idempotency and evolution rules. Use when adding an API that other teams or clients will call.",
      "category": "architecture",
      "tags": [
        "api-design",
        "contract-first",
        "rest",
        "idempotency",
        "versioning"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "capability",
          "description": "What the API must let its clients do, in plain words or as user stories.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "consumers",
          "description": "Who calls the API (web app, mobile app, partners, internal services) and their constraints.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "API style. auto picks one and justifies it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "auto",
            "rest",
            "graphql",
            "grpc"
          ],
          "default": "auto"
        },
        {
          "name": "conventions",
          "description": "Existing API conventions to follow (naming, error format, auth, pagination), or a sample of an existing endpoint.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn API contract is a promise that outlives its first implementation: once clients depend on it, every field name, error shape and default is expensive to change. Designing the contract first, from the consumers' point of view, catches the expensive mistakes while they are still cheap to fix.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDesign the API contract for: {{capability}}\n{{#consumers}}\nConsumers and their constraints:\n{{consumers}}\n{{/consumers}}\n{{#conventions}}\nExisting conventions to follow exactly:\n{{conventions}}\n{{/conventions}}\nStyle: {{style}}. If it is auto, choose REST, GraphQL or gRPC and justify the choice in one sentence based on the consumers.\n\n1. Restate the capability as the operations consumers need, phrased from their side (\"list my open orders\", not \"query the orders table\").\n2. Model the resources (or types, or services) and the operations on them. Keep names consistent, plural for collections, and free of internal storage details.\n3. Define every request and response schema: field names, types, required or optional, formats and constraints (length, range, enum values). Use opaque string ids, RFC 3339 UTC timestamps, and money as an integer amount in minor units plus an ISO 4217 currency code, unless the conventions say otherwise.\n4. Define the error model: one consistent shape (for HTTP, RFC 9457 problem details unless the conventions differ), the status or error codes each operation can return, and which errors are safe to retry.\n5. Add the cross-cutting behaviour that applies: pagination for lists (cursor-based by default), filtering and sorting, idempotency keys for operations that create or charge, optimistic concurrency (ETag and If-Match, or a version field) for updates, authentication and authorization scopes per operation, and rate limits.\n6. Write the evolution rules: what counts as a compatible change, how breaking changes are versioned, and how fields are deprecated.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Design the contract only. No server implementation code.\n- Do not invent business rules (limits, states, permissions, pricing). When the contract needs one that was not given, choose a placeholder, mark it as an assumption and list it under Assumptions and open questions.\n- Follow the given conventions over these defaults whenever they conflict.\n- Include one realistic request and response example for each main operation.\n- Prefer fewer, well-shaped operations over one endpoint per screen.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nStyle chosen and why, the resources, and the main design choices, in at most 6 lines.\n## Operations\nTable: operation, method and path (or query, mutation or RPC name), purpose, auth scope, idempotent (yes or no).\n## Contract\nOne fenced block with the machine-readable contract: OpenAPI 3.1 YAML for REST, SDL for GraphQL, proto3 for gRPC. Include the examples.\n## Errors\nTable: code, when it happens, retryable (yes or no).\n## Evolution and compatibility\nBullets.\n## Assumptions and open questions\nNumbered. Each assumption says what it affects.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "software-architect"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-adr"
        ]
      },
