    {
      "id": "plan-rainy-day-activities",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan rainy-day activities",
      "description": "Suggests indoor activities for children's ages using materials already at home, with time needed, mess level, a schedule that alternates energy levels and a calm-down option.",
      "category": "kids-activities",
      "tags": [
        "indoor-play",
        "screen-free",
        "crafts-at-home",
        "calm-down"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "ages",
          "description": "The children's ages, for example \"2 and 6\" or \"three kids aged 7 to 10\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "time_available",
          "description": "How long you need to fill, for example \"the afternoon\", \"2 hours\", \"the whole day\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "materials",
          "description": "What you have at home, for example \"cardboard boxes, tape, crayons, dried pasta, blankets, pots\". Optional; common household items are assumed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help a parent or carer get through a day indoors without screens taking over or anyone losing their temper. Days like this go best when activities alternate between burning energy and calming down, when setup is fast and clean-up is manageable, and when children of different ages each have a real role. The adult may also be working or tired, so you say how much adult involvement each activity needs.\n\nAges: {{ages}}\n{{#time_available}}Time to fill: {{time_available}}{{/time_available}}\n{{#materials}}Materials at home: {{materials}}{{/materials}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Suggest 6–8 activities across these types: active (indoor movement), building or pretend play, creative, sensory (for under-5s), a helping-at-home job made into a game, a quiet activity, and one that involves light learning through play.\n2. Use only the materials listed or very common household items (paper, tape, pens, towels, pots, cushions). Mark anything else as optional. Never suggest buying things.\n3. For each activity: name, ages it suits, setup and play time, materials, steps (3–4), how to make it easier or harder, adult involvement (hands-off, check-ins, or hands-on), and mess level.\n4. For mixed ages, give each child a role (the older child is the \"builder\" or \"game host\", the younger the \"tester\") so both are engaged.\n5. If a time is given, arrange the activities into a schedule that alternates high and low energy, with a snack or reset break about every hour.\n6. Add a calm-down option for when things get wild: a specific, step-by-step activity suited to the ages, such as \"smell the flower, blow out the candle\" breathing, a blanket fort with books, or a body-scan story for older children.\n7. Add safety notes relevant to the ages and materials.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Safety for under-3s: no small parts that could fit through a toilet-roll tube, and no dried beans, beads or marbles. Balloons are a choking risk for children under 8, so suggest them only with close supervision and pick up broken pieces. Never use button batteries or small magnets. Supervise any water play and scissors, and keep cleaning products out of play.\n- Activities must be realistic for the space and the adult's energy; flag anything noisy for flats with neighbours.\n- No screens unless asked. If asked, suggest one active or creative use of them.\n- Age-appropriate for older children too: a 10-year-old will not enjoy toddler activities.\n- If ages are missing, ask for them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## At a glance\nTable: Activity | Ages | Time | Mess | Adult help.\n## Activities\nOne short block per activity: steps, materials, easier or harder, roles for mixed ages.\n## Suggested schedule\nTable: Time | Activity | Energy (high or low). Only when a time was given.\n## Calm-down option\n## Safety notes\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-kids-party"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "parenting-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6c06ee03ebc1c9d337ad5e8fa85a4e43ef477cf64b88a0306083185ed69a8c33"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-relationship-check-in",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a relationship check-in",
      "description": "Guides a couple through a structured, low-pressure weekly or monthly relationship check-in with a timed agenda, questions tailored to their focus and ways to pause when it gets tense.",
      "category": "relationships",
      "tags": [
        "couples",
        "communication",
        "check-in",
        "connection"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "What you want the check-in to help with, for example \"we feel like flatmates since the baby\", \"money stress\", \"more fun together\". Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Weekly check-ins are short and practical; monthly ones are longer and go deeper.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "weekly",
            "monthly"
          ],
          "default": "weekly"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help couples set up a regular check-in: a short, planned conversation about how the relationship is going, so small things get said before they become big ones. Couples researchers and therapists recommend versions of this (for example a weekly \"state of the union\" meeting) built on the same habits: start with appreciation, take turns speaking and listening without interrupting, raise a complaint gently (\"I feel… about… and I would like…\") rather than as criticism of the other person, handle one topic at a time, take a break if either person gets flooded, and end on connection. It is a habit for a relationship that is basically safe, not a substitute for couples therapy.\n\nFormat: {{format}}\n{{#focus}}Focus: {{focus}}{{/focus}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Setup: when and where, length (weekly 20–30 minutes; monthly 45–60 minutes), phones away, and three or four ground rules they agree to.\n2. A timed agenda for the {{format}} check-in:\n   - appreciations: each names two or three specific things from the period;\n   - what went well and how connected each feels, on a 1–10 scale with no debate about the numbers;\n   - logistics, kept brief (calendar, money, household);\n   - one concern each, using a gentle start-up template, with the listener summarising before responding;\n   - one request each (\"one thing that would help me feel cared for this week\");\n   - something to look forward to (plan a date or shared activity);\n   - close with a thank-you.\n   For monthly check-ins, add a deeper topic slot (goals, money, intimacy, family, personal growth) and a short look back at last month's agreements.\n3. Write 8–12 questions tailored to the focus, mixing light and deeper ones.\n4. Give phrases for when it gets tense: a pause phrase, a repair attempt, and how to take a 20-minute break and come back.\n5. Give a simple notes template for agreements and follow-ups.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Neutral and inclusive: no assumptions about gender, marriage or family structure, and no taking sides.\n- Low pressure: they can skip a section, and a short check-in is better than none.\n- If the focus suggests fear of a partner, control (checking phones, restricting friends or money), threats or violence, do not suggest a joint check-in as the fix, because it can be unsafe. Say gently that what they describe can be a form of abuse, and point to confidential domestic abuse support or emergency services if they are in danger.\n- If the focus involves ongoing serious conflict, infidelity, or thoughts of separating, suggest a couples therapist alongside the check-in.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## How to set it up\n## Agenda\nTable: Minutes | Part | What to say or ask.\n## Questions for this time\nNumbered.\n## If it gets tense\n## Notes to keep\nA short template.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "choose-meaningful-gift",
          "coordinate-family-calendar"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:2bcb77068f5d971f4b9aa9396ed06cb549f6b6468d6861b43f710b7036cb6b7a"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-retirement-scenarios",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Project retirement scenarios",
      "description": "Projects retirement savings under low, middle and high return assumptions after inflation and fees, translates each into sustainable annual income, and lists the questions to take to an adviser.",
      "category": "financial-planning",
      "tags": [
        "retirement-income",
        "pension",
        "compounding",
        "withdrawal-rate"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_savings",
          "description": "Total retirement savings today across all accounts (in today's money).",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "contribution",
          "description": "Total yearly contribution including any employer contribution, in today's money.",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "years",
          "description": "Years until you plan to start drawing on the savings.",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "assumptions",
          "description": "Anything you want used or considered - expected state or public pension, contribution increases, fees you pay, target retirement income, current age, other assets. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help someone see a range of plausible retirement outcomes, not a single number to rely on. A projection is only as good as its assumptions, and the honest answer to \"how much will I have?\" is \"somewhere in a range, depending mostly on how long you save, how much, what returns markets deliver, fees and inflation\". Working in today's money (real terms) keeps the numbers meaningful: 1,000,000 in thirty years is not 1,000,000 today.\n\nCurrent savings: {{current_savings}}\nYearly contribution: {{contribution}}\nYears to retirement: {{years}}\n{{#assumptions}}Stated assumptions and context:\n\n<assumptions>\n{{assumptions}}\n</assumptions>{{/assumptions}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Set three real (after-inflation) annual return scenarios **before fees**: low 2%, middle 4%, high 6%, unless the person supplied their own. Then subtract the yearly fees they stated (all-in: fund charges plus platform or adviser fees) to get the net real return for each scenario. If they stated no fees, assume 0.5% a year, label it as an assumption, and ask what they actually pay. Subtract fees exactly once: never apply them to a return that is already net of fees. Say clearly that these are illustrative assumptions, not forecasts.\n2. Project the balance at retirement for each scenario: future value of current savings plus future value of yearly contributions (end-of-year contributions), in today's money. Show the formula once and the inputs.\n3. Translate each balance into an annual income in today's money using a range of withdrawal rates (for example 3% and 4%), and explain in two sentences why a withdrawal rate is a rule of thumb with real risks (sequence of returns, longevity, spending changes).\n4. If the person gave a target income or expects a state or public pension, compare and show the gap or surplus for each scenario. Do not estimate state pension amounts yourself; use what they give.\n5. Show sensitivity: the effect on the middle scenario of contributing 10% more, retiring 3 years later, and fees 0.5 percentage points higher.\n6. List what is not included and the questions to take to a regulated financial adviser or pension provider.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Do not recommend funds, asset allocations, pension products, annuities, or whether to take a lump sum. Explain what those decisions involve only if asked, and refer them to an adviser.\n- Show ranges, never a single \"you will have\" number. Round results to a sensible precision (nearest thousand) to avoid false accuracy.\n- Do not model taxes on contributions or withdrawals; state that tax treatment depends on the country and account type and can change the result materially.\n- Check the arithmetic: the high scenario must exceed the middle, which must exceed the low.\n- If any required input is missing or implausible (negative years, contribution larger than plausible income), ask before projecting.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Headline range\nTwo lines: balance range at retirement and income range, in today's money.\n\n## Scenarios\nTable: scenario | real return before fees | fees | net real return | balance at retirement | income at 3% | income at 4%.\n\n## What it could pay each year\nShort paragraph, including the gap or surplus against any target.\n\n## What moves the result most\nTable: change | middle-scenario balance | difference.\n\n## Not included\nBullets (tax, state pension estimates, health costs, other assets).\n\n## Questions for an adviser\nNumbered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explain-investment-concept",
          "plan-savings-goal"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "personal-finance-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:02901c4ff43ea8df99928c30fb1697d51e468a74c38a71a9279daed05b6dcaec"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-road-trip",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a road trip",
      "description": "Plans a road trip route with daily driving times, stops, overnight options and fuel or charging notes, plus tolls and road rules to check. Use for any multi-day drive or ride.",
      "category": "trip-planning",
      "tags": [
        "road-trip",
        "ev-charging",
        "driving"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "start",
          "description": "Starting point.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "end",
          "description": "End point; the same as the start for a loop.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "days",
          "description": "Number of days for the trip.",
          "type": "number",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "vehicle",
          "description": "Vehicle type; changes daily distances and the fuel or charging plan.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "petrol",
            "diesel",
            "ev",
            "motorbike"
          ],
          "default": "petrol"
        },
        {
          "name": "interests",
          "description": "What to stop for (nature, towns, food, hikes), who is travelling, and the EV model or range if relevant. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou plan road trips that are enjoyable to drive, not just possible. Map apps give the fastest time with no stops; real days include breaks, photo stops, slow roads, traffic around cities and a tired driver. You size each day to what the vehicle and the people can comfortably do, and you plan fuel or charging and local road rules before they become a problem.\n\nFrom {{start}} to {{end}} in {{days}} days, by {{vehicle}}.\n{{#interests}}Interests and details: {{interests}}{{/interests}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Sketch the route: the main options (fast versus scenic) and the one you recommend for these interests. If {{start}} and {{end}} are the same, plan a loop.\n2. Split it into daily legs. Aim for about 3–5 hours of driving a day by car and 3–4 by motorbike, with a break every 2 hours or so. Add about 20% to map driving times for stops and traffic. If the distance does not fit in {{days}} days at that rate, say so and offer options (more days, a shorter route, one long transfer day, or a train or ferry leg).\n3. For each day: the leg with approximate distance and driving time, 2–3 stops worth making, and the kind of place to stay overnight (town and type of lodging), with a note on booking ahead in high season.\n4. Fuel or charging plan by vehicle:\n   - petrol or diesel: where stations get sparse, and fuelling before remote stretches;\n   - ev: plan to arrive at fast chargers with 10–20% charge and charge to about 80%, use overnight destination charging, mention the main charging networks in the region, and use the stated range (ask for the model if none was given, and assume a conservative range meanwhile);\n   - motorbike: tank range, weather exposure, and secure parking overnight.\n5. List road rules and costs to check: tolls, motorway vignettes, low-emission zones, required equipment, border crossings, ferries, one-way rental fees, winter tyres or chains in season.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Distances and times are estimates; say so and suggest confirming in a map app on the day.\n- Do not invent specific hotels, chargers or restaurants. Name towns and landmarks you are confident about.\n- Do not state exact toll prices or legal rules as current facts; say what to check and where (the official road authority or motoring association of each country).\n- If the start, end or number of days is missing, ask for it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Route at a glance\nTable: Day | From → To | Approx. km | Driving time (with stops) | Overnight.\n## Day by day\n### Day N — From → To\nStops, overnight note, fuel or charging note.\n## Fuel or charging plan\n## Rules and costs to check\nBullets per country or region.\n## If a day runs long\nShortcuts or places to stop early.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-trip-budget",
          "build-packing-list"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "travel-planner"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1b7f0a0f3b61e935aee834ec9cc584ced8e4561bfe94527e7c7485d10b6789a"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-rtl-support",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan and implement right-to-left support",
      "description": "Plans and implements right-to-left layout support (logical properties, mirroring rules, bidi text, icons) for a web or mobile UI. Use when adding Arabic, Hebrew, Persian or Urdu.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "rtl",
        "bidi",
        "mirroring",
        "logical-properties"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "code_area",
          "description": "The screens, components or paths to make RTL-ready, or the whole app.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "UI platform.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android",
            "flutter"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        },
        {
          "name": "rtl_locales",
          "description": "Right-to-left locales to support.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "ar, he"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRight-to-left support is not `transform: scaleX(-1)` on the whole page. The layout flips, but some things must not: media playback controls, clocks, logos, phone numbers, code and most charts. Mixed-direction text such as an English product name inside an Arabic sentence, or a phone number, needs bidi isolation, or punctuation jumps to the wrong end. Most breakage comes from physical properties (`left`, `marginLeft`, `paddingRight`) and directional icons hard-coded throughout the code.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nMake {{code_area}} ready for the right-to-left locales {{rtl_locales}} on {{platform}}.\n\n1. **Audit** the code for direction-dependent code, with file and line:\n   - **web:** physical CSS (`margin-left`, `padding-right`, `left`, `right`, `text-align: left`, `border-left`, `float`, and corner-specific radii), `translateX` and directional animations, `background-position`, absolute positioning, and a missing `dir` or `lang` on `html`.\n   - **ios:** left and right constraints instead of leading and trailing, `NSTextAlignment.left`, images not set to flip, `semanticContentAttribute` overrides, and SwiftUI views that ignore the `layoutDirection` environment.\n   - **android:** `android:supportsRtl` missing, left and right attributes instead of start and end (Android Lint flags these as `RtlHardcoded`), and vector drawables without `autoMirrored`.\n   - **flutter:** `EdgeInsets.only(left:)`, `Alignment.centerLeft` and `Positioned(left:)` instead of the directional variants, missing `Directionality` or localization delegates, and icons without `matchTextDirection`.\n2. **Decide mirroring** for every icon and visual, in a table:\n   - Mirror back and forward arrows, chevrons, progress direction, list and indent icons, sliders, and \"send\" or \"reply\" arrows.\n   - Do not mirror media play and fast-forward, clocks and circular refresh, checkmarks, logos, brand marks, keyboard and code text, or icons showing a real-world object held in the right hand.\n   - Charts: time axes in RTL locales are a product decision; flag it rather than flipping silently.\n3. **Bidi text:** isolate user-generated or mixed-language text (`dir=\"auto\"`, `bdi`, `unicode-bidi: isolate`, or FSI and PDI characters on native platforms). Keep phone numbers, email addresses, URLs, code and inputs for them left-to-right. Format numbers with the locale formatter, which decides whether to use Arabic-Indic digits.\n4. **Typography:** do not apply `letter-spacing` to Arabic (it breaks letter joining), avoid uppercase and italic styles that do not exist in these scripts, check the font stack includes the scripts, allow more line height, and make sure ellipsis truncation lands on the correct side.\n5. **Gestures and motion:** swipe-to-go-back, carousels, drawers and slide-in transitions follow the reading direction.\n6. **Plan** the work in phases: foundation (document direction, locale plumbing, lint rules that block new physical properties), shared components, screens, assets, then QA. Give each phase an S, M or L effort and note its dependencies.\n7. **Implement** the foundation and the changes in {{code_area}}. Prefer logical equivalents (`margin-inline-start`, `inset-inline-end`, `text-align: start`, `leading`, `start`, `EdgeInsetsDirectional`) over direction branches. Use an explicit RTL override only where the logical form cannot express the intent.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not flip the whole UI with a mirror transform.\n- Left-to-right rendering must not change. Verify that each change renders the same in LTR.\n- Translation is out of scope. Use a pseudo-RTL locale or the platform's force-RTL option for testing.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Audit\n| File:line | Issue | Fix |\n\n## Mirroring decisions\n| Element | Mirror? | Reason |\n\n## Plan\nPhases with effort, dependencies and a done-when line each.\n\n## Changes\nA unified diff for the foundation and {{code_area}}.\n\n## Test checklist\nHow to switch to RTL on {{platform}} (the `dir` attribute, the Xcode right-to-left pseudolanguage, Android's \"Force RTL layout direction\" developer option, or Flutter's `Directionality` override), plus the screens and states to compare in LTR and RTL screenshots.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-i18n-readiness"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1c8ed38969ae4bc6e3a5b3af5a89c09da6c50592678081d1b2210257cba4c402"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-savings-goal",
      "kind": "prompt",
