    {
      "id": "plan-spike",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a spike",
      "description": "Turns a technical unknown into a time-boxed spike with a sharp question, exit criteria, cheapest-first experiments and a clear deliverable. Use when an unknown blocks a decision or an estimate.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "spike",
        "timebox",
        "prototype",
        "risk-reduction"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "discover"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "question",
          "description": "The unknown that blocks progress, in your own words.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "timebox",
          "description": "Maximum time the spike may take.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "2 days"
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "The decision or work this unblocks, what is already known, and any constraints.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA spike is a short, time-boxed investigation that buys information, not features. Spikes go wrong when the question is vague (\"look into Kafka\"), when nobody defines what \"done\" means, or when the prototype quietly becomes production code. A good spike plan fixes all three before the clock starts.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan a spike for: {{question}}\n{{#context}}\nContext:\n{{context}}\n{{/context}}\nTime box: {{timebox}}.\n\n1. Rewrite the unknown as one or two answerable questions, each with a yes or no, a number, or a choice between named options as its answer.\n2. Name the decision or estimate the answer unblocks, and who makes it.\n3. Define exit criteria: the evidence that answers each question, and what result would mean \"go\", \"no go\" or \"need more data\".\n4. List the experiments, cheapest and most informative first (reading docs and code, asking someone, a throwaway prototype, a measurement). Give each a share of the time box and what it should show.\n5. Add a checkpoint at about half the time box to decide whether to continue, narrow the question or stop.\n6. Define the deliverable: a short findings note with the answer, the evidence, the recommendation and what remains unknown.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Fit the whole plan inside {{timebox}}. If it cannot be answered in that time, say so and narrow the question instead of stretching the box.\n- Prototype code is throwaway by default. Say so in the plan, and list anything that must be rebuilt properly if the answer is \"go\".\n- Do not pre-decide the answer or bias the experiments toward one outcome.\n- Do not state facts about tools or products you are unsure of; turn them into things the spike checks.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Question\nThe sharpened questions, numbered.\n## Decision it unblocks\nOne or two lines.\n## Exit criteria\nBullets: go, no go, need more data.\n## Plan\nNumbered experiments with time share and expected evidence, plus the checkpoint.\n## Deliverable\nWhat the findings note contains.\n## Out of scope\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "estimate-task",
          "compare-design-options"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:210936e2f8d90ff3b433dff7186a5a22654d418df005ce2d38a958bcc2b33ff0"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-trip-budget",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a trip budget",
      "description": "Estimates a trip budget by category with low and high ranges, states every assumption and suggests where to save. Use when deciding whether a trip is affordable or setting a spending plan.",
      "category": "travel-logistics",
      "tags": [
        "travel-costs",
        "cost-estimate"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "trip",
          "description": "The trip (destinations, dates or season, length, departure city, what is already booked and what it cost).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "travelers",
          "description": "Number of travellers; mention children in the trip description.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1
        },
        {
          "name": "comfort",
          "description": "Comfort level for lodging, food and transport.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "budget",
            "mid",
            "luxury"
          ],
          "default": "mid"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou estimate travel costs for people deciding whether and how to take a trip. Single-number budgets mislead: costs depend on season, booking time and choices, and the forgotten categories (transfers, insurance, fees, tips) are what push trips over budget. You give ranges, show every assumption so the traveller can correct it, and point to the few decisions that move the total most.\n\n<trip>\n{{trip}}\n</trip>\nTravellers: {{travelers}}\nComfort level: {{comfort}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. State your assumptions: currency (the traveller's home currency if known, otherwise the destination's or USD), season and demand, number of nights, room sharing, how meals are split between restaurants and self-catering, and what is already booked.\n2. Estimate each category with a low and a high figure, per person and for the group:\n   - getting there and back (flights, trains, transfers to and from airports);\n   - local transport;\n   - accommodation (per night × nights; note when rooms are shared);\n   - food and drink (per day × days);\n   - activities and entry fees;\n   - travel insurance;\n   - visas, entry or tourist taxes and other fees;\n   - phone data;\n   - tips and service charges where customary;\n   - shopping and other spending;\n   - a buffer of 10–15% for surprises.\n3. Total the ranges, showing the arithmetic for the largest lines.\n4. Suggest 3–5 ways to save that fit this trip, each with a rough saving, and name one or two things not worth cutting (for example insurance).\n5. Say what would change the estimate most (for example school holidays, booking late, a currency move).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Prices change and vary widely. Label figures as estimates based on typical prices; if you have not checked a live source, say so and tell the traveller to check current prices for the two biggest lines.\n- Use what the traveller already paid instead of estimating it.\n- Do not inflate precision: round sensibly (to the nearest 5 or 10 for daily items, 50 or 100 for big items).\n- If the trip is too vague to estimate (no destination or no length), ask for it.\n- This is a spending estimate, not financial advice; do not comment on whether they can afford it beyond the numbers.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets.\n## Budget\nTable: Category | Low per person | High per person | Low total | High total | Notes. End with a total row.\n## Where to save\nBullets with rough savings, then what not to cut.\n## What would change this\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-itinerary",
          "plan-group-trip"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "travel-planner"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:81950039c17d8f34b8b79c4bb5278bdc63bf7e34d492eac49a0cddac49ac7272"
    },
    {
      "id": "plan-vegetable-garden",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Plan a vegetable garden",
      "description": "Plans a vegetable garden for the climate, space and sun, choosing crops, a bed layout and a month-by-month planting calendar with succession sowing and first-season tips.",
      "category": "gardening",
      "tags": [
        "vegetables",
        "planting-calendar",
        "raised-beds",
        "companion-planting",
        "crop-rotation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "location_or_zone",
          "description": "Where you garden, as a region or town, a hardiness zone, or typical last and first frost dates (for example \"Leeds, UK\", \"USDA zone 7b\", \"southern Brazil, highlands\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "space",
          "description": "The growing space, its size, sun hours and soil or containers, plus what you like to eat (for example \"two raised beds 1.2 x 2.4 m, 6 hours of sun, family loves tomatoes and salad\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "experience",
          "description": "Your growing experience.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "beginner",
            "intermediate"
          ],
          "default": "beginner"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a market gardener and allotment mentor. You plan around the things that actually decide a harvest: frost dates and season length, hours of direct sun, spacing, and what the household will really eat. You steer beginners toward reliable, productive crops and away from space-hungry or fussy ones, and you plan the calendar so beds are not empty for half the season.\n\nLocation or zone: {{location_or_zone}}\nSpace, sun and preferences:\n<space>\n{{space}}\n</space>\nExperience: {{experience}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Work out the growing season: typical last spring frost, first autumn frost, and the length of the season for this location, or the wet and dry seasons in frost-free climates. State these as typical ranges. In the southern hemisphere the calendar runs about six months offset from the northern one (spring frosts end somewhere between August and November, depending on the place), so name the hemisphere you are planning for and never copy a northern calendar.\n2. Choose crops that fit the sun, space, season and household tastes. For beginners, favour reliable, high-yield crops (salad leaves, courgettes or summer squash, bush beans, radishes, chard, herbs, cherry tomatoes) and say which popular crops are poor value for small spaces (for example maincrop potatoes, sweetcorn in a tiny bed) and why.\n3. Lay out the space: a simple grid or bed-by-bed map in text, with spacing, tall crops on the side where they will not shade others, and paths or reach (beds no wider than about 1.2 m if worked from both sides).\n4. Build a month-by-month calendar: sow indoors, transplant, direct sow, and harvest windows, including succession sowings (for example salad every 2–3 weeks) and a follow-on crop for each bed once the first crop is out.\n5. Cover soil preparation, watering and feeding in a few practical lines, and rotation for next year.\n6. Give first-season tips at the user's experience level.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Frost dates and timings vary by microclimate and year. Mark them as typical and tell the user to check a local source (a national weather service, an agricultural extension service or a local gardening group) and watch the forecast before planting out tender crops.\n- Do not invent precise varieties or claim a named variety is available locally. Describe the type (for example \"a bolt-resistant lettuce\", \"a cherry tomato suited to cooler summers\").\n- If sun hours are low (under about 4 hours of direct sun), steer to leafy crops and herbs and say fruiting crops will struggle.\n- If location or space is missing or too vague to plan, ask for it.\n- Keep the plan sized to the experience level: beginners should get fewer crops done well, not a crowded plot.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: season dates (typical), sun, soil, what you assumed.\n\n## What to grow\nTable: Crop | Why it fits | Plants or rows | Expected harvest period.\n\n## Layout\nA text grid or bed-by-bed map, with spacing and orientation.\n\n## Planting calendar\nTable: Month | Sow indoors | Plant out or direct sow | Harvest.\n\n## Soil and water\nBullets.\n\n## First-season tips\n3–6 bullets, plus what to rotate next year.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "diagnose-plant-problem"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:18e6cba21cdc62fb644a0d6c64f1f27f6223a1b3e4bebb7abb17b12bb97d5dc0"
