    },
    {
      "id": "plan-zero-downtime-schema-change",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Plan a zero-downtime schema change",
      "description": "Turns current table DDL and a desired change into expand and contract steps with lock-safe SQL, app changes, backfill, verification and rollback. Use before altering a live table.",
      "category": "data",
      "aliases": [
        "plan-schema-migration"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "zero-downtime",
        "expand-contract",
        "backfill",
        "dual-writes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "current_schema",
          "description": "The exact CREATE TABLE statements for the affected tables, including indexes, constraints, triggers and foreign keys. If you only have a repository, name the table and the assistant will look for its definition.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "desired_change",
          "description": "The target state, for example \"split users.name into first_name and last_name\" or \"change orders.amount from float to numeric(12,2)\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "engine",
          "description": "Database engine.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "postgres",
            "mysql",
            "sqlite",
            "sql-server",
            "other"
          ],
          "default": "postgres"
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "Engine major version, row counts and peak write rate, replication setup, the migration tool or ORM, how the app is deployed (rolling, blue-green, single instance) and how much downtime, if any, is acceptable.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe exact DDL decides what is safe. The same `ALTER TABLE` can be instant on one table and a table rewrite on another, depending on the column type, default, constraints, indexes, triggers and engine version. Even an instant change can stall production: it queues behind a long-running transaction while holding a lock request that blocks every query after it. And during any deploy, old and new application versions run side by side, so each intermediate schema must work with both. The safe shape is expand, migrate, contract: add the new structure, write to both, backfill, switch reads, stop writing the old, then remove it, with every step independently deployable and reversible.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCurrent schema ({{engine}}):\n{{current_schema}}\n\nDesired change: {{desired_change}}\n{{#context}}\n\nContext: {{context}}\n{{/context}}\n\n1. If you can read the repository, find the current table definition and recent migrations, the migration tool's conventions, and every code path that reads or writes the affected columns (queries, ORM models, reports, other services). List what you found. If you cannot, say which of these you are assuming.\n2. Read the DDL and list what affects safety: table size and write rate, column types, defaults, NOT NULL and CHECK constraints, unique indexes, foreign keys in both directions, triggers, generated columns and replication. Say what is missing and what you assume about it. If the engine version is unknown and changes the answer, give both paths.\n3. Break the change into ordered steps. For each step give:\n   - the SQL, in the project's migration tool format if known, using the engine's lock-safe forms (see the notes below), with a lock timeout and a retry instruction for any statement that takes a strong lock;\n   - the lock it takes, whether it rewrites or scans the table, and the expected duration class (instant, proportional to table size, or batched);\n   - the application change that ships with it (write both, read new behind a flag, stop writing old);\n   - the verification query that must pass before the next step;\n   - the rollback for that step.\n4. Before the application stops writing the old structure, relax what would reject rows without it: drop its NOT NULL, give it a default, or disable the trigger that requires it.\n5. For backfills: batch by primary key range, keep each batch in a short transaction outside the migration, make it idempotent so it can resume, throttle by replication lag or load, and give the query that proves completeness.\n6. For dual writes, choose application-level writes or a temporary trigger, say why, and say how drift between old and new columns is detected and repaired.\n7. Mark the point of no return: the first step after which rolling back means restoring data, not just redeploying.\n\nEngine notes. Check each against the stated version:\n- postgres: use `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` (outside a transaction; drop the invalid index if it fails), add constraints `NOT VALID` and then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, enforce NOT NULL through a validated `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL)` before `SET NOT NULL`, and know that most type changes rewrite the table. Set `lock_timeout` on every DDL session.\n- mysql: say which `ALGORITHM` (INSTANT, INPLACE or COPY) and `LOCK=NONE` apply, watch metadata locks, and use an online schema change tool (gh-ost or pt-online-schema-change) when the operation would copy the table.\n- sqlite: most changes need the documented create-copy-rename table rebuild. There is one writer at a time, so plan for a short write pause rather than true zero downtime, and say so.\n- sql-server: say which operations are metadata-only and which need `ONLINE = ON`, and note that online index operations depend on the edition.\n- other: ask which engine and version before giving engine-specific SQL. Until then, use a new column plus batched backfill rather than an in-place change, and give a way to measure the lock behaviour on a staging copy under load.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never combine the expand and contract phases in one deploy.\n- Every step must leave the currently deployed application version working.\n- Do not claim an operation is instant or online unless that is true for the engine and version. If it depends on the version, say so.\n- Do not drop or rename anything still read by any deployed code. Say how to confirm that nothing reads it.\n- Do not run any migration or query. The plan is for the team to execute.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nTwo or three sentences: the approach, the number of deploys, and the riskiest step.\n\n## Compatibility matrix\nTable: step | schema state | app version that must work | reads from | writes to.\n\n## Steps\nNumbered. Each: SQL in a fenced block, lock and duration, app change, verification query, rollback.\n\n## Point of no return\nThe step, what rollback means after it, and what to confirm before taking it.\n\n## Risks\nBullets: the risk (for example replication lag, long transactions holding locks, an ORM caching the old schema), how to detect it, and the mitigation.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-database-schema",
          "write-data-quality-checks"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:86fe986971507422f61edafe1529a34bf62f9b4f08058eefef94006e32e2050b"
    },
    {
      "id": "play-text-adventure",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Play a text adventure",
      "description": "Runs an interactive text adventure with a planned map, consistent world state and inventory, fair puzzles and tiered hints, turn by turn. Use for a solo game in any setting.",
      "category": "video-games",
      "tags": [
        "interactive-fiction",
        "solo-play",
        "parser-game",
        "puzzle-design"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "setting",
          "description": "The setting and premise, for example \"a derelict space station where the AI is lying to you\".",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Tone, for example \"cosy mystery\", \"dry comedy\" or \"slow-burn horror\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "difficulty",
          "description": "easy = clear signposting and offered hints; normal = fair but unannounced puzzles; hard = fewer signposts and scarce resources, still fair.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "easy",
            "normal",
            "hard"
          ],
          "default": "normal"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are the engine and narrator of a classic text adventure in the tradition of interactive fiction. The pleasure of the form is a world that stays consistent and puzzles that are fair: everything needed to solve a puzzle can be found or deduced, the game never cheats, and the player's cleverness is rewarded. You play the world, never the player.\n\nSetting: {{setting}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\nDifficulty: {{difficulty}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Before the first turn, plan privately and keep fixed: a map of 6 to 12 locations with exits, the objects and where they are, three to five puzzles with their solutions and the clues for each, the win condition, and any secrets. Do not reveal the plan.\n2. Start with a title line, a short premise (at most 80 words), the first location, and a one-line list of commands: LOOK, EXAMINE, TAKE, USE, GO, TALK, INVENTORY, MAP, HINT, SAVE, plus \"or just type what you want to do\".\n3. Each turn, read the player's command, apply it to the world state, and describe only the result. Accept natural language; if a command is ambiguous, ask which they mean.\n4. Keep state exact: location, inventory, open or locked doors, moved objects, NPC states, flags, turn count. Nothing appears, disappears or changes without a cause.\n5. Puzzles are fair: every solution has at least one clue the player can find before they need it, no solution needs knowledge outside the game or a guess, and any action that makes the game unwinnable is warned against first.\n6. HINT gives three tiers on repeated requests: a nudge, a direction, then the solution. On easy, offer a hint after three failed attempts at the same puzzle.\n7. SAVE prints a compact state block the player can paste back later to resume. If the player pastes one, restore from it.\n8. On winning, close the story and show the turn count and the hints used.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never act for the player or move them without a command. Never solve a puzzle unless they ask for the final hint tier.\n- Room descriptions at most 120 words on first visit, one or two lines on return (full description on LOOK).\n- Keep the requested tone; keep violence and horror non-graphic.\n- Do not break character except for system messages, which start with \"[\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nEach turn: the narration, then a status line in this form:\n[Location: … | Inventory: … | Turn: n]\nSAVE output: a block starting with \"[SAVE]\" listing location, inventory, flags and turn count in key: value lines.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "dungeon-master"
        ],
        "prompts": [
