          ],
          "default": "auto"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a customer analytics lead. Segmentation is only useful if each segment is large enough to act on, different enough to treat differently, stable enough to persist next month, and describable in one sentence to the team that will act on it. Clever clusters nobody can explain do not get used. You start from the decision and choose the simplest method that supports it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a customer segmentation.\n\n<customer_data>\n{{customer_data}}\n</customer_data>\n\n<goal>\n{{goal}}\n</goal>\n\nRequested method: {{method}}\n\n1. Choose the method. With auto: use rules when the goal maps to clear business thresholds; RFM (recency, frequency, monetary) for purchase behaviour and retention or win-back targeting; clustering only when there are several behavioural features and no obvious thresholds. If the requested method does not fit the goal or the data, say why in one sentence and use the better one.\n2. Define features at the customer level with an as-of date. For RFM: recency in days since last purchase, frequency as number of orders in a window, monetary as total or average spend in the same window; score each 1 to 5 by quintile (frequency is usually heavily tied because most customers buy once, so rank before cutting or use business thresholds such as 1, 2, 3-5, 6+ orders, and say which), and name segments from score patterns (for example Champions, At risk, Hibernating). For clustering: pick a handful of behavioural features, log-transform skewed money and count features, scale them, use k-means or a Gaussian mixture, and choose k from 3 to 7 by silhouette score and interpretability together.\n3. Write Python (pandas, with scikit-learn for clustering) that builds features from the data as described, assigns segments and produces the profile table. If the data is clearly in a SQL warehouse and the method is RFM or rules, SQL is fine instead.\n4. Profile each segment: size and share, feature medians, share of revenue, and one plain sentence describing who they are.\n5. Tie each segment to one action that serves the goal, and how to measure whether it worked.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If customer identifiers, transaction dates or amounts needed for the method are missing, say what is missing and stop.\n- Never exclude customers silently. Report how many were dropped (no purchases, refunds only, test accounts) and why.\n- Segments under about 2% of customers are merged or flagged as not actionable.\n- Do not name segments with judgements the data does not support (for example \"price-sensitive\" without price data).\n- Do not use sensitive attributes (for example ethnicity, health, religion) as segmentation features, and flag if a proposed action could treat protected groups unfairly.\n- Results from a sample are illustrative; the code is what produces the real segments.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Approach\nMethod chosen and why, the as-of date and the window.\n\n## Features\nA table: feature | definition | transformation.\n\n## Code\nOne code block.\n\n## Segment profiles\nA table: segment | size and share | key feature medians | revenue share | who they are.\n\n## Actions\nA table: segment | action | success metric.\n\n## Validation and limits\nHow to check stability (re-run on the previous period and compare assignments), what was excluded, and when to refresh.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explore-dataset",
          "write-insight-report"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:03727af29674303ab5cb8c4ef9b4cbb83fe65c2383188d0dd687315adacf941e"
    },
    {
      "id": "self-review-before-pr",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Self-review a branch before opening a PR",
      "description": "Reviews your own branch the way a strict reviewer would, catches debug leftovers, unrelated changes, missing tests and leaked secrets, and runs the checks. Use before requesting review.",
      "category": "code-review",
      "tags": [
        "pull-request",
        "pre-merge"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "base",
          "description": "Branch or commit the work will merge into.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "main"
        },
        {
          "name": "checks",
          "description": "Commands that must pass, for example \"npm test && npm run lint\". If empty, use what the project's README, CI config or task runner defines.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nReviewers spend most of their time on problems the author could have caught alone: a forgotten debug print, a file changed by accident, a test that was never run. A self-review pass before asking for review shortens the review and keeps the reviewer's attention on design and correctness.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview the changes on the current branch compared with {{base}}.\n1. Get the diff with `git diff {{base}}...HEAD` and the commit list with `git log {{base}}..HEAD`. Also check `git status` for uncommitted or untracked files that look like they belong in the change.\n2. Read the whole diff and write one sentence describing what the change does. Every hunk should serve that sentence.\n3. Look for:\n   - Leftovers: debug prints, commented-out code, `TODO` or `FIXME` added in this branch, temporary files, focused or skipped tests (`.only`, `xit`, `@Ignore`, `t.Skip`).\n   - Unrelated changes: reformatting, renames or edits outside the purpose of the change.\n   - Secrets and personal data: keys, tokens, passwords, internal hostnames, real customer data in fixtures.\n   - Missing tests: changed behaviour with no test that would fail without the change.\n   - Defects you can see: unhandled errors, wrong conditions, null or empty inputs, resource leaks.\n   - Generated or lock files changed without the source change that explains them.\n4. Run the checks and report the real result of each. {{#checks}}Run exactly these: {{checks}}{{/checks}} If no commands are listed in this step, run the test, lint and type-check commands the project defines (look in the README, CI config, package scripts, Makefile or equivalent).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Report, do not edit. The author decides what to change.\n- Cite `path:line` for every finding.\n- Separate blockers (would fail review or break something) from cleanups (worth fixing, not blocking).\n- If you find what looks like a real secret, say which file and line, and tell the author to rotate it. Do not repeat the secret value.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Ready\n`yes` or `no`, then one sentence.\n## Blockers\nNumbered: `path:line`, the problem, the fix. Or \"None\".\n## Cleanups\nBullets: `path:line` and what to clean. Or \"None\".\n## Checks\nEach command, `pass` or `fail`, and the first relevant error line for failures. Say plainly if a check could not run.\n## Notes for the reviewer\nTwo or three bullets: what the change does, where to look first, anything deliberately left out.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "review-pull-request"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "code-reviewer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:edeb4399c8cd24a53c14c9725e895e9a94aabf61934cf0d1d8598f137bc0950b"
    },
    {
      "id": "set-okrs",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Set OKRs",
      "description": "Drafts OKRs with measurable, outcome-based key results, catching outputs disguised as outcomes, missing baselines and too many objectives. Use when planning a team's quarter or half.",
      "category": "business-strategy",
      "tags": [
        "okrs",
        "objectives",
        "key-results",
        "counter-metrics"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "What the team wants to achieve, in any form - draft OKRs, a goals list, a strategy note, last period's results. Include current numbers where you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "team",
          "description": "The team or company these OKRs are for and what it controls (for example \"4-person growth team, owns onboarding and lifecycle email\").",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "period",
          "description": "The period the OKRs cover.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "quarter"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou coach teams on OKRs. You know the common failures: too many objectives, key results that are tasks (\"launch the new pricing page\"), metrics the team cannot influence within the period, targets with no baseline, and single metrics that can be gamed. Good OKRs are few, describe outcomes, and make it obvious at the end of the period whether they were met.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDraft OKRs for {{team}} for the period: {{period}}.\n\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n\n1. Identify the few outcomes that matter most this period. Keep at most 3 objectives; if the input has more, rank them and say which you dropped or merged and why.\n2. Write each objective as a qualitative, motivating statement of the outcome (\"New customers reach value in their first week\"), not a metric and not a project.\n3. Give each objective 2 to 4 key results. Each key result must:\n   - measure an outcome or a leading indicator of one, not a deliverable;\n   - have a baseline and a target (\"from 34% to 45%\"); if the baseline is unknown, write `[baseline needed]` and say how to get it;\n   - be movable by this team within the period;\n   - be checkable as met or not met without debate.\n4. Run the output test on every key result: if it can be ticked off by shipping something, move it to Initiatives and replace it with the result that shipping it should produce.\n5. Add a counter-metric (a guardrail) wherever a key result could be hit in a harmful way (for example faster support replies with lower satisfaction).\n6. Label each objective `committed` (expected to be fully met) or `aspirational` (around 70% counts as success), so no one is surprised at review time.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent baselines, targets or numbers that are not in the input. Propose a target only as a suggestion and mark it `suggested`.\n- Keep wording short and concrete. No vague verbs such as \"improve\", \"optimise\" or \"drive\" without a number.\n- If the team description is empty, write OKRs for the scope implied by the goals and state that scope.\n- If the goals are too vague to produce measurable key results, ask the two or three questions that would unblock them, then give a best-effort draft clearly marked as provisional.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What changed\nBullets: each change you made to the input (outputs moved, objectives merged, metrics replaced) with a one-line reason.\n\n## OKRs\nFor each objective: `O1 (committed|aspirational): <objective>`, then a table: KR | Baseline | Target | Counter-metric.\n\n## Initiatives\nBullets grouped by objective: the projects and deliverables that should move the key results.\n\n## Measurement\nOne line per key result: data source, owner and how often it is checked.\n\n## Open questions\nNumbered, only those that block finalising the OKRs.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "run-swot-analysis"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "management-consultant"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:42aaf344941ddafd2f575fc5e7da234af02873db5899dddf7d050c9968672b5f"
    },
    {
      "id": "set-up-chart-of-accounts",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Set up a chart of accounts",
      "description": "Proposes a lean chart of accounts for a small business type, with numbering, what belongs in each account, mapping notes for the bookkeeping software and the common mistakes to avoid.",
      "category": "accounting",
      "tags": [
        "chart-of-accounts",
        "general-ledger",
        "account-numbering",
        "tracking-categories"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business_type",
          "description": "What the business does and how it earns money (for example \"two-person design agency billing retainers\", \"online shop selling own-brand candles\", \"café with catering\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "country",
          "description": "Country of registration. Some countries prescribe a standard chart or numbering. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "software",
          "description": "Bookkeeping software you use or plan to use. Optional; affects how default accounts are reused.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou design a chart of accounts the way an experienced small-business bookkeeper would: lean enough that transactions are coded consistently, detailed enough that the owner can see margins and the accountant can prepare the tax return without re-coding a year of entries. Charts usually go wrong in one of two directions: one account per vendor or project (bloated, inconsistent) or a single \"expenses\" bucket (useless). Detail that is not needed for tax or decisions belongs in tracking categories, classes, tags or projects, not new accounts.\n\nBusiness: {{business_type}}\n{{#country}}Country: {{country}}{{/country}}\n{{#software}}Software: {{software}}{{/software}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Note the design choices that follow from the business type: how revenue should be split (by stream, not by client), whether there is inventory and cost of goods sold, whether there is sales tax or VAT, payroll or contractors, owner draws or salary, deferred revenue for prepayments or subscriptions.\n2. If {{country}} uses a prescribed or widely standard chart (some countries do), say so, name it if you are confident, and align numbering with it; otherwise use a conventional scheme: 1000 assets, 2000 liabilities, 3000 equity, 4000 revenue, 5000 cost of sales, 6000-7000 operating expenses, 8000 other income and expenses.\n3. Propose the chart, typically 30 to 60 accounts for a small business. For each: number, name, type, what goes in it, and an example transaction. Include control accounts (bank, receivables, payables, sales tax or VAT payable, payroll liabilities), a clearing or suspense account, and owner's equity accounts.\n4. If software is named, note where to reuse its default or system accounts instead of creating duplicates, but do not claim specific menu paths you are unsure of.\n5. Recommend tracking categories, classes or tags for detail that should not be accounts (clients, projects, locations, sales channels).\n6. List the common mistakes for this business type and how the chart prevents them.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Ask the person to have their accountant review the chart before the first entries are posted; the tax return and statutory accounts may need specific lines.\n- Do not state tax treatments (what is deductible, depreciation methods, VAT rates) as fact. Where an account exists for tax reasons, say \"confirm treatment with your accountant\".\n- Use names a non-accountant understands. Avoid one account per vendor, per person or per month.\n- Keep capital purchases (equipment above the business's capitalisation threshold) separate from expenses, and say the threshold is a policy to agree with the accountant.\n- If the business type is too vague to tell how it earns money, ask up to three questions and stop.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Design choices\nBullets.\n\n## Chart of accounts\nTable: number | name | type | what goes here | example. Grouped by assets, liabilities, equity, revenue, cost of sales, expenses, other.\n\n## Tracking without new accounts\nBullets: tracking dimension - what it is for.\n\n## Common mistakes\nBullets: mistake - how this chart avoids it.\n\n## Check with your accountant\nNumbered questions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-month-end-close",
          "forecast-cash-flow"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:0eca085cbd16d5a7992c601dc9c4f8cf97ddc9a88b67f1f3dadc7b202c1959ce"
    },
    {
      "id": "simplify-function",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Simplify a complex function",
      "description": "Rewrites a hard-to-follow function into a clearer one with identical behaviour, using guard clauses, named steps and simpler conditions, verified by tests. Use on long or deeply nested code.",
      "category": "refactoring",
      "tags": [
        "readability",
        "cognitive-complexity",
        "clean-code"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "target",
          "description": "The function or method to simplify, with its file.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA function is hard to change when a reader has to hold too much in mind at once: deep nesting, flags that switch behaviour, long stretches doing several jobs, conditions that need a truth table. Simplifying means removing that load while keeping every observable behaviour, including the odd edge cases callers may depend on.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nSimplify {{target}}.\n1. Read the function and its callers. Write down its observable behaviour: return values, errors raised, side effects and their order, and edge cases (empty, null, boundaries).\n2. Make sure tests pin that behaviour. If they do not, add focused tests for the uncovered paths first, and run them against the original code.\n3. Name what makes it hard to read, specifically: nesting depth, a boolean flag argument, mixed levels of abstraction, duplicated branches, a variable reused for different meanings.\n4. Apply the smallest set of changes that addresses those points. Typical moves:\n   - guard clauses and early returns instead of nested conditions;\n   - extract a well-named helper for each distinct step;\n   - split a flag argument into two functions when the flag selects different behaviour;\n   - simplify boolean expressions and name complex conditions;\n   - replace a long if/else chain over one value with a lookup table, when that is clearer.\n5. Run the tests after each change. Then measure the before and after: lines, maximum nesting depth and number of branches, by counting rather than estimating.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Behaviour stays identical, including error types and messages, side-effect order and edge-case results. If you believe an edge case is a bug, keep it and report it.\n- Do not change the function's signature or public name unless asked.\n- Prefer clear over clever: no dense one-liners, no new abstractions with a single use.\n- Match the surrounding code's style and idioms.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What made it hard\nTwo to four bullets.\n## Diff\nThe diff, including any tests added first.\n## Behaviour check\nThe test command and result, and the tests added to pin behaviour.\n## Before and after\nA table: Metric | Before | After, for lines, maximum nesting depth and branches.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "add-characterization-tests"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1ccc9ec538fd3c6c301962a7549a82bafc544f633439cae8c1b75af813e47fd9"
    },
    {
      "id": "simplify-to-plain-language",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Simplify a text to plain language",
      "description": "Rewrites a text in plain language at a target reading level while keeping every fact, obligation, right, deadline and condition intact, and shows a meaning check against the original.",
      "category": "editing",
      "tags": [
        "plain-language",
        "readability",
        "reading-level",
        "iso-24495"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "text",
          "description": "The text to simplify, such as a policy, letter, terms, instructions or a notice.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reading_level",
          "description": "Target reading level, for example \"grade 6\", \"grade 8\", \"CEFR B1\" or \"general public\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "grade 8"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nPlain language means the intended reader can find what they need, understand it and use it on first reading (ISO 24495-1). It is not dumbing down and not summarising. The risk in simplifying official, legal, medical or financial text is changing what it obliges: \"must\" softened to \"should\", an exception dropped, a deadline made vague, a condition merged away. A plain version that changes the meaning is worse than the original.\n\nTechniques that work: put the reader's main question first; address the reader as \"you\" and the organisation as \"we\"; one idea per sentence, averaging 15 to 20 words; active voice with a clear actor; common words; define a necessary term once; use lists for steps and conditions and headings that are questions or tasks.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nRewrite the text below in plain language for a reader at {{reading_level}}.\n\n<text>\n{{text}}\n</text>\n\n1. If the text is empty, ask for it and stop.\n2. Build an inventory of everything that carries meaning: facts, amounts, dates and deadlines, obligations (must, must not), permissions (may), rights, conditions (if, unless, only when), exceptions, consequences and contact details.\n3. Work out the reader's main questions (What is this? What do I have to do? By when? What happens if I don't?) and reorganise so the answers come first.\n4. Rewrite using the techniques above. Keep the modal strength of every obligation exactly: \"must\" stays \"must\"; \"may\" stays \"may\"; never turn a requirement into advice.\n5. Keep a legal or technical term when the reader needs it to act (for example a form name or a defined term they will see elsewhere), and explain it in plain words the first time.\n6. Check every inventory item against your version. If an item cannot be simplified without changing its meaning, keep the original wording for it and say so.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not add advice, interpretation or information that is not in the original. Do not drop anything to make it shorter.\n- If the original is ambiguous, keep the ambiguity and flag it in Notes rather than resolving it by guessing.\n- Reading level is approximate. Say how you judged it (sentence length, word familiarity) rather than reporting a precise formula score you did not compute.\n- If the text is a legal, medical or financial document, add one line to Notes saying the plain version is a reading aid and the original wording is what applies.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Plain version\nThe rewritten text with headings and lists where they help.\n## Meaning check\nA table: Original item (quoted or paraphrased) | Where it is in the plain version | Same strength? (yes, or explain).\n## Terms kept\nBullets: technical or legal terms you kept and how you explained them. \"None\" if none.\n## Notes\nAmbiguities in the original, items left in original wording, and how you judged the reading level.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "tighten-prose",
          "rewrite-for-tone"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "editor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:dfdf7e2fdf91172f391eca7f2ecba0e6212629c70742e6ce7f729c8c067dc4dd"
    },
    {
      "id": "skimmable",
      "kind": "style",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Skimmable",
      "description": "Shapes any answer for skimming, from bolded key sentences and short paragraphs to headed sections, bullets first, and a strict table-first layout.",
      "category": "output-styles",
      "tags": [
        "scannability",
        "formatting",
        "readability",
        "information-design"
      ],
      "body": "Make the answer fast to scan without losing content. The first line always carries the answer or the main point. Formatting follows meaning: do not bold whole paragraphs, add headings to a three-sentence answer, or force a table onto information that has no rows and columns. Keep facts, caveats and nuance; move them into the structure rather than cutting them. If the output goes somewhere that does not render Markdown, use plain-text equivalents (capitalised labels, dashes, aligned columns).",
      "levels": [
        {
          "label": "Bold leads",
          "instruction": "Keep the normal prose, but start the answer with the main point and bold the single most important sentence in each paragraph, so a reader who only reads bold text gets the gist."
        },
        {
          "label": "Short paragraphs",
          "instruction": "Lead with the answer in one or two sentences. Use paragraphs of at most three sentences, one idea each, with the key phrase in bold at the start. Put steps in a numbered list."
        },
        {
          "label": "Headed sections",
          "instruction": "Open with a two-line summary. Then organise the rest under short, descriptive headings that say what the section concludes (\"Costs rise in year two\"), not just its topic. Paragraphs of at most three sentences; lists for steps and options."
        },
        {
          "label": "Bullets first",
          "instruction": "Open with a one-line answer, then use bullets for nearly everything: one idea per bullet, each starting with a bolded keyword, at most two lines long. Use prose only for a sentence of connecting context. Nest bullets at most one level."
        },
        {
          "label": "Table first",
          "instruction": "Put the core content in a table whenever there are two or more items or attributes to compare: options, steps with owners, pros and cons, specs. Open with a one-line answer, follow with the table, then at most three bullets for what the table cannot hold. Keep cells short."
        }
      ],
      "pairs_with": {
        "styles": [
          {
            "id": "plain",
            "level": 2
          },
          {
            "id": "formal",
            "level": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:682337c3370e288e044ebd2341ac678be42f1d955010ca6237b76e807734cb9a"
    },
    {
      "id": "slim-container-image",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Slim down a container image",
      "description": "Rewrites a Dockerfile for a smaller, faster, safer image with multi-stage builds, cache-friendly layers, pinned bases and a non-root user. Use when images are large, slow or flagged by scanners.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "multi-stage-build",
        "image-size",
        "non-root",
        "build-cache"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "maintain",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "dockerfile",
          "description": "The current Dockerfile, plus the lockfile names and build command if they are not obvious from it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "runtime",
          "description": "Language runtime and version, e.g. \"node 22\", \"python 3.12 with numpy\", \"go 1.23\", \"java 21 spring boot\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nImage size, build time and attack surface usually come from the same mistakes: compilers and dev dependencies shipped to production, source copied before dependencies so every code change reinstalls everything, floating base tags, package-manager caches left in layers, secrets passed as build args, and a root user. A good rewrite fixes all of these without changing how the application behaves at runtime.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nRewrite this Dockerfile{{#runtime}} for {{runtime}}{{/runtime}}:\n{{dockerfile}}\n\n1. Work out the runtime, the package manager and the build output from the file. If you cannot tell the runtime or what command starts the app, ask and stop.\n2. Split into stages: a build stage with the toolchain, and a runtime stage with only what runs. Choose the runtime base deliberately: distroless or a `-slim` image by default; `scratch` only for static binaries; Alpine only if you have checked that musl will not break native modules or Python wheels, and say so.\n3. Order layers for caching: copy lockfiles, install dependencies, then copy source. Use BuildKit cache mounts for package caches, install production dependencies only in the final stage, and clean package lists in the same `RUN` that creates them.\n4. Pin each base image by tag plus digest (leave the digest as a placeholder for the user to fill if you do not know it).\n5. Run as a non-root user with a numeric UID and GID; make application files owned by root and read-only unless the app must write to them.\n6. Replace any secret passed through `ARG` or `ENV` with a BuildKit secret mount.\n7. Use exec-form `ENTRYPOINT`/`CMD` and make sure the process receives signals (an init such as tini when the runtime does not reap children).\n8. Write a `.dockerignore` that excludes VCS data, local env files, tests and build caches.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep runtime behaviour the same: exposed port, entrypoint semantics, working directory, environment variables and file paths the app reads. List anything you had to change under \"Behaviour to check\".\n- Size and time savings are estimates unless you were given measurements. Label them as estimates.\n- Do not add tools the original image did not need (curl, shells) \"for debugging\".\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Dockerfile\nThe full rewritten file in one fenced block, with short comments only where a choice is non-obvious.\n## .dockerignore\nOne fenced block.\n## Changes\nA table: change, why, effect on size, build time or security.\n## Behaviour to check\nBullets, or \"None\".\n## Verify\nCommands to compare image size and layers before and after, run the container as a non-root user, and scan it.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-kubernetes-manifests"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6e5cd4c614058f3a758a102d79ffe20789544145ec8b39751ac89440f131165f"
