    {
      "id": "vet-dependency",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Vet a dependency before adding it",
      "description": "Checks a third-party package for supply-chain risk, maintenance health, license fit and real need before it is added or upgraded. Use when a PR adds a new dependency or bumps one.",
      "category": "security",
      "tags": [
        "supply-chain",
        "dependencies",
        "licensing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "package",
          "description": "Package name with its ecosystem and version, for example \"npm:left-pad@1.3.0\" or \"pypi:requests==2.32.3\". A diff of a manifest or lockfile also works.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "purpose",
          "description": "What the project needs the package for.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "license_policy",
          "description": "Licenses the project accepts or rejects, for example \"permissive only, no GPL or AGPL\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEvery dependency runs with the project's privileges and brings its own dependencies along. Typosquats, hijacked maintainer accounts, malicious install scripts and abandoned packages with open vulnerabilities are common ways into a codebase. A short check before adding a package is far cheaper than removing it after an incident.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nVet {{package}}.\n{{#purpose}}The project needs it for: {{purpose}}{{/purpose}}\n{{#license_policy}}License policy: {{license_policy}}{{/license_policy}}\n\n1. Identity: confirm the exact name against the registry and the source repository it links to. Flag names one edit away from a popular package, a registry entry with no source link, or a source repo that does not match the published package.\n2. Install-time behaviour: check for install, preinstall or postinstall scripts, native builds, binary downloads, and any network or file system access at import time.\n3. Maintenance: latest release date, release cadence, number of active maintainers, recent ownership or maintainer changes, open security advisories, and whether known vulnerabilities are fixed in the requested version.\n4. Footprint: number of transitive dependencies it adds and anything risky among them. In a repo, compare against the lockfile to see what is new.\n5. License: the package's license and any transitive license that conflicts with the policy.\n6. Need: whether the project already has a dependency or standard library feature that does the job, and how much code the package saves.\nUse your tools to look things up. For every fact, say where it came from (registry page, advisory database, repository). If you cannot reach a source, write \"not checked\" for that item instead of guessing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never state download counts, dates, versions, advisories or maintainer facts from memory. Only report what you looked up in this session, with its source.\n- Do not install, import or run the package to test it.\n- Judge the specific version requested, not the package in general.\n- A verdict of `reject` needs at least one concrete reason from the evidence.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Verdict\nOne line: `adopt`, `adopt-with-conditions` (state them, for example \"pin to 2.3.1\"), or `reject`, plus the main reason.\n\n## Evidence\n| Check | Finding | Source |\nOne row each for identity, install scripts, maintenance, advisories, footprint, license and need. Use \"not checked\" where you could not verify.\n\n## Risks\nBullets, most serious first. \"None found\" if empty.\n\n## Alternatives\nUp to three: a standard library feature, an existing dependency, or a better-maintained package, each with one line on the trade-off. \"None needed\" if the package is a good fit.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "security-auditor"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "review-pr-for-security"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a6f6100ac72a4efcc60460a259a7425256c8229066e885bc5c606fe4b0e6c593"
    },
    {
      "id": "video-production-track",
      "kind": "workflow",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Video production track",
      "description": "Takes a video from idea to hook, script, title and thumbnail, and description, pausing for approval between steps. Use when producing a YouTube video end to end.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "audience-retention",
        "hooks",
        "packaging",
        "video-description"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The video idea or subject, with any notes, sources or constraints you already have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        }
      ],
      "body": "Produces a video about \"{{topic}}\" one approved step at a time: a sharpened idea with a clear promise to the viewer, then the opening hook, then the full script, then the title and thumbnail package, then the description. Each step produces one artifact and stops for the creator's approval or edits; later steps build on the approved versions and never re-open settled decisions without asking. The promise approved in step 1 is the contract for every later step: the hook sets it up, the script pays it off, and the packaging advertises it honestly. The creator owns every creative decision; the assistant drafts, checks consistency between steps and flags gaps it cannot fill without inventing facts. If the creator asks to skip the approvals, confirm once that later steps will then build on unreviewed choices; if they agree, run the remaining steps in one reply, state the choice made at each skipped gate, and keep every placeholder visible.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "idea",
          "stage": "plan",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 1: Idea and promise\n\nTurn \"{{topic}}\" into a video idea that a specific viewer would click and finish.\n\n1. Ask the creator, in one message, for anything not already given: the channel and its usual audience, the target length, what they personally know or have done that makes them credible on this topic, any footage or examples they can show, and what the video should achieve (views, subscribers, leads, teaching).\n2. When you have the answers, write:\n   - **Viewer:** who it is for, in one sentence, and what they already know.\n   - **Promise:** one sentence: by the end, the viewer will know, be able to do, or have seen what.\n   - **Angles:** three distinct angles on the topic (for example a tutorial, a mistake-driven list, a test or experiment, a story), each with a one-line pitch and why it would get clicked. Recommend one.\n   - **Proof points:** the examples, demonstrations or facts the video will rest on, marked as either supplied by the creator or still needed.\n   - **Risks:** anything that makes the idea hard to deliver (missing footage, unverifiable claims, a crowded topic).\n\nStop and wait for the creator to approve or edit the angle and promise. Do not write hooks yet."
        },
        {
          "id": "hook",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 2: Hook\n\nWrite the first 15 to 30 seconds for the approved angle of \"{{topic}}\".\n\n1. Write five hook options, each using a different technique (for example result first, bold claim, the viewer's problem as a question, a mistake and its cost, a story that starts mid-action). For each, give the spoken lines, what is on screen at 0:00, and the open loop it creates.\n2. Every option must set up the approved promise and nothing the video will not deliver.\n3. No greeting, channel intro or \"in this video\" before the hook lands.\n4. Recommend one and say why in one sentence.\n\nStop and wait for the creator to choose or edit a hook. Do not write the script yet."
        },
        {
          "id": "script",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 3: Script\n\nWrite the full script for \"{{topic}}\", starting from the approved hook.\n\n1. Use the approved hook verbatim as the opening, then order the body so value arrives early and escalates. One point per segment, each with a concrete example or demonstration from the approved proof points.\n2. Add a pattern interrupt every 45 to 90 seconds (a shot change, B-roll, an on-screen graphic, a question, a quick story) and mark it `[INTERRUPT: …]`. Mark editor cues as `[ON SCREEN: …]` and `[B-ROLL: …]`.\n3. Place one soft call to action after a high-value moment and one end call to action pointing to a specific next video or action. Avoid lines that signal the ending before the last 20 seconds.\n4. Budget about 150 spoken words per minute of the approved length.\n5. Where a fact, number or story is needed but was not supplied, insert a bracketed placeholder instead of inventing it, and list all placeholders at the end with the word count.\n\nStop and wait for approval or edits. Do not write titles or thumbnails yet."
        },
        {
          "id": "packaging",
          "stage": "build",
          "gate": "approve",
          "text": "# Step 4: Title and thumbnail\n\nPackage the approved script for \"{{topic}}\" so the right viewers click and are not disappointed.\n\n1. Write six title and thumbnail pairs. The thumbnail shows and the title tells: they work together and do not repeat the same words.\n   - Title: under 60 characters, the most important words first.\n   - Thumbnail: one focal subject, at most four words of text, high contrast, readable at phone size. Describe the composition, the subject's expression or the key object, and the text.\n2. For each pair, name the curiosity mechanism (result, contrast, mystery, stakes, before and after) and check it against the script: the payoff it implies must arrive in the video, ideally in the first minute.\n3. Recommend two pairs to test against each other and say what each tests.\n\nStop and wait for the creator to choose a package. Do not write the description yet."
        },
        {
          "id": "description",
          "stage": "ship",
          "gate": "none",
          "text": "# Step 5: Description\n\nWrite the description for the approved video about \"{{topic}}\".\n\n1. First two lines: the promise in plain language with the main search phrase used naturally. These lines show before \"more\", so they must stand alone.\n2. A short paragraph on what the video covers and who it is for.\n3. Chapters from the approved script's timestamps, starting at `0:00`, at least three, in ascending order, each at least 10 seconds long and named for what the viewer gets. Tell the creator to adjust the times to the final edit.\n4. Links the creator supplied, labelled. Never invent a URL; use `[LINK: …]` placeholders for anything mentioned but not supplied.\n5. The end call to action from the script, in one line.\n6. Finish with a pre-publish checklist: placeholders still open, chapter times to confirm against the edit, and any claim in the packaging that the final cut must still deliver."
        }
      ],
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-video-hooks",
          "write-youtube-script",
          "package-video-title-thumbnail",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "content-strategist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ab128ff083d7f7cc12b0e7fa745f9bf90671462730547a933625e18c9b384ad0"
    },
    {
      "id": "warm",
      "kind": "style",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Warm",
      "description": "Makes any answer warmer and more personal, from simply friendly to deeply empathetic, while keeping the substance, accuracy and honesty of the answer intact.",
      "category": "output-styles",
      "tags": [
        "empathy",
        "friendly-voice",
        "kindness",
        "emotional-support-tone"
      ],
      "body": "Warmth changes how things are said, never what is true. Keep facts, warnings, bad news and disagreement intact; deliver them kindly rather than dropping or blurring them. Do not use flattery, gushing, pet names or stacked exclamation marks, and do not claim feelings or experiences you do not have. Match warmth to the situation: a technical question at a high level still gets a precise answer first.",
      "levels": [
        {
          "label": "Friendly",
          "instruction": "Use a relaxed, approachable voice: contractions, a friendly opening clause when it fits naturally, and plain, upbeat wording. Keep it as concise as it would otherwise be."
        },
        {
          "label": "Kind",
          "instruction": "Be friendly and considerate: soften criticism with what is working first, use \"we\" and \"you\" naturally, and phrase instructions as suggestions where the stakes allow. Add one short encouraging line where it helps."
        },
        {
          "label": "Warm",
          "instruction": "Speak as a supportive person who cares how this lands: acknowledge the person's situation or effort in a sentence, use their name if given, and close with genuine encouragement or an offer of next steps. Keep advice clear and specific."
        },
        {
          "label": "Caring",
          "instruction": "Lead with acknowledgement of how the person might be feeling before any advice, validate that the situation is hard or frustrating when it is, use gentle phrasing, and check in on what they need (information, options, or just to be heard) before giving long advice."
        },
        {
          "label": "Deeply empathetic",
          "instruction": "Prioritise the person: reflect back what they said in their own terms, name and normalise the feelings they express without assuming ones they did not, go slowly, and offer support before solutions. Keep every fact accurate, and if anything suggests distress or danger, gently point to people and services who can help."
        }
      ],
      "pairs_with": {
        "styles": [
          {
            "id": "plain",
            "level": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:79bd10195ff8187a8aa3cb76e82d2c88688f4fc71ef0aee041cc45605d95e252"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-abstract",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a paper abstract",
      "description": "Writes a structured or unstructured abstract within a word limit from a manuscript, keeping every number faithful to the source and showing where each appears. Use before submission.",
      "category": "scientific-writing",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "word-limit",
        "reporting-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript",
          "description": "The manuscript text, or at least its methods and results sections with the main tables.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Maximum number of words in the abstract.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 250
        },
        {
          "name": "structure",
          "description": "Structured abstracts use labelled sections (Background, Methods, Results, Conclusions); unstructured ones are one paragraph.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "structured",
            "unstructured"
          ],
          "default": "structured"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe abstract is the only part of a paper most readers, indexers and reviewers read, so it must stand alone and be exact. Common errors are numbers that differ from the results section, conclusions stronger than the data, a missing primary outcome, undefined abbreviations and going over the limit. Reporting guidelines (for example CONSORT for trials, STROBE for observational studies, PRISMA for systematic reviews) each have abstract checklists, and journals often name the headings they require.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{structure}} abstract of at most {{word_limit}} words from this manuscript:\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript}}\n</manuscript>\n\n1. Identify the study type, the objective, the design, setting and participants, the primary outcome and its main result, the key secondary results and the authors' conclusion.\n2. If the manuscript names a reporting guideline or a target journal's required headings, follow them; otherwise use Background, Methods, Results and Conclusions for a structured abstract.\n3. In Results, lead with the primary outcome, giving the effect size with its confidence interval (and p-value if the manuscript reports one) and the number analysed.\n4. Write a conclusion that says only what the results support, with the main limitation if it changes how the result should be read.\n5. Count the words and cut until you are within the limit: remove background before results, and secondary results before the primary one.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Copy every number, unit and interval exactly as it appears in the manuscript. Never round, recompute or combine numbers. If the results section and a table disagree, use neither, flag the conflict in Notes and leave a placeholder such as \"[CHECK: n analysed]\".\n- Include nothing that is not in the manuscript: no new claims, no citations, no references to figures or tables.\n- Define each abbreviation at first use, and use at most three abbreviations.\n- Match the strength of language to the design: observational studies report associations, not effects.\n- If the manuscript has no results (for example only an introduction), say so and ask for the results instead of drafting them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Abstract\nThe abstract, with bold section labels if structured. Then the line \"Word count: N / {{word_limit}}\".\n## Number check\nA table: number in the abstract | where it appears in the manuscript (section, table or quoted phrase).\n## Notes\nAny conflicts, placeholders, or content cut to fit the limit. \"None\" if clean.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "explain-research-to-public",
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:bfb2aed618f32d26251e6864fba63fc7c944cb4c20a9ceb296613a481e96ecbe"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-acceptance-criteria",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write acceptance criteria",
      "description": "Writes testable acceptance criteria for a user story or ticket, covering the main path, alternatives, validation, boundaries, permissions and empty states. Use before a story enters development.",
      "category": "product",
      "tags": [
        "gherkin",
        "bdd",
        "given-when-then",
        "definition-of-done"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "The user story or ticket text.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "Business rules, limits, roles and screens that apply.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "criteria_style",
          "description": "gherkin gives Given/When/Then scenarios; checklist gives short verifiable statements.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "gherkin",
            "checklist"
          ],
          "default": "gherkin"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAcceptance criteria are the shared definition of done between product, engineering and testing. Good criteria describe observable behaviour with concrete values, so two people reading them would test the same thing. Most production bugs in new features sit in the cases the criteria never mentioned: boundaries, permissions, errors and empty states.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite acceptance criteria for this story: {{story}}\n{{#context}}\nRules and context that apply:\n{{context}}\n{{/context}}\nStyle: {{criteria_style}}.\n\n1. Identify the main path and write it first.\n2. Add the cases that apply to this story: alternative paths, input validation, exact boundaries (at, just below and just above each limit), permissions for each role, empty and first-use states, errors from dependencies, and repeated or concurrent actions.\n3. Use concrete example values in every criterion (amounts, dates, names, counts), not \"valid input\".\n4. Check every criterion: could a tester verify it with no further explanation? Rewrite any that fail.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Describe behaviour the user or a system can observe, not implementation or UI layout, unless the story is about the layout.\n- Each scenario stands alone and tests one behaviour.\n- At most 12 criteria. If the story needs more, say that it should be split and suggest where.\n- Do not invent business rules. If a criterion needs a rule that was not given, write it with your best guess, mark it \"Assumption:\", and repeat it under Questions for the product owner.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Acceptance criteria\nFor gherkin: numbered scenarios, each with a `Scenario:` title and Given, When, Then lines (And where needed).\nFor checklist: numbered \"- [ ]\" items, one verifiable statement each.\n## Assumptions\nBullets, or \"None\".\n## Questions for the product owner\nNumbered, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "product-manager"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-user-stories"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:845fb3be41066ff567a852c8872c9c76b8dc0e2777d36c5637dc7f9e9dc107bf"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-adr",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an architecture decision record",
      "description": "Writes an architecture decision record that states one decision, the forces behind it, the options weighed and the honest consequences. Use when a significant technical choice is made or proposed.",
      "category": "architecture",
      "tags": [
        "decision-record",
        "trade-offs",
        "madr"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "decision",
          "description": "The decision taken or proposed, or the question to decide, in a sentence or two.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "Notes, constraints, discussion excerpts, links or numbers behind the decision.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "options",
          "description": "Options that were considered. Leave empty to draw them from the context.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "status",
          "description": "Status to record in the ADR.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "proposed",
            "accepted",
            "superseded"
          ],
          "default": "proposed"
        },
        {
          "name": "template",
          "description": "Layout to use when the project has no existing ADRs to copy.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "madr",
            "nygard"
          ],
          "default": "madr"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn architecture decision record (ADR) captures one architecturally significant decision so that someone joining the team in two years can see what was decided, why, and what it cost. Its value is honesty about the forces and the consequences. An ADR that lists only upsides, or quotes a benchmark nobody ran, is worse than no ADR, because readers trust it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an ADR for this decision: {{decision}}\n{{#context}}\nContext and constraints:\n{{context}}\n{{/context}}\n{{#options}}\nOptions considered:\n{{options}}\n{{/options}}\n\n1. If you can read the repository, look for existing ADRs (for example `docs/adr/`, `doc/adr/`, `docs/decisions/`, `adr/`). If you find any, copy their layout, numbering and tone, and use the next free number. Otherwise use the {{template}} layout in the output format below.\n2. Extract the decision drivers: the requirements, constraints and quality attributes that actually push the choice (for example latency, cost, team skills, deadline, compliance, existing systems). Use only drivers present in the input or the code.\n3. List the options. Include \"keep the current approach\" when it is a real option. For each option, give pros and cons measured against the drivers, not generic ones.\n4. State the decision in one active sentence (\"We will …\") and say why it wins on the drivers.\n5. Write the consequences: what becomes easier, what becomes harder, new risks, follow-up work, and the signal that should make the team revisit this decision.\n6. Record the status as {{status}}. If the input does not support a decision yet, record it as proposed and list what is missing under Open questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One decision per ADR. If the input bundles several, write the main one and list the others under Open questions as candidates for their own ADRs.\n- Never invent facts: no made-up benchmarks, prices, dates, names, quotes or product limits. Where a number would matter and none was given, write `TODO: measure …` with what to measure.\n- Every option, including the chosen one, gets at least one real downside.\n- Keep it readable in five minutes: about 300 to 800 words.\n- Plain language. Define any acronym a new team member might not know.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nFirst line: the suggested file name, `NNNN-short-kebab-title.md`, using the next number when you know it and `NNNN` when you do not.\nThen the ADR in Markdown.\n\nmadr layout:\n# [Short title of the decision]\n- Status: [status] · Date: [today if known, else TODO] · Deciders: [names given, else TODO]\n## Context and problem statement\n## Decision drivers\n## Considered options\n## Decision outcome\nThe chosen option and why, then a \"Consequences\" list of good, bad and neutral bullets.\n## Pros and cons of the options\nOne subsection per option.\n## Open questions\nOmit when there are none.\n\nnygard layout:\n# [N]. [Title]\nDate line, then `## Status`, `## Context`, `## Decision`, `## Consequences`, and `## Open questions` only when needed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "software-architect"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "compare-design-options"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6f380225f1aa4347613c5cc8ca169cfe2e47b117f2edba47bd83cca4a0d08053"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-agent-handoff",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an agent handoff",
      "description": "Writes a self-contained handoff note so a fresh agent or a teammate can continue the current task without the conversation history. Use before ending a long session, switching tools or delegating.",
      "category": "meta",
      "tags": [
        "handoff",
        "context-window",
        "multi-agent"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "reader",
          "description": "Who picks up the work.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "a fresh agent with no memory of this session"
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Anything the handoff must stress, such as a deadline or a decision still pending.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe next reader has none of this session's context: not the conversation, not the files you read, not the dead ends you already ruled out. A handoff fails when it says \"as discussed\", when it reports work as done that was never verified, or when it leaves out the approaches that did not work, so the next agent repeats them. It should let {{reader}} start working within a minute.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a handoff for the task in this session.\n{{#focus}}\nStress this: {{focus}}\n{{/focus}}\n\n1. Check the actual state before writing: `git status`, the current branch, uncommitted changes, and the last commands and test results in this session. Do not rely on memory of what you intended to do.\n2. Separate what is done and verified (with the evidence), what is done but unverified, and what is in progress (the exact point where work stopped).\n3. Write the next steps as concrete, ordered actions with file paths and commands, so they can be executed without interpretation.\n4. Record decisions with their reasons, and the approaches that were tried and rejected, with why.\n5. Note gotchas: environment quirks, flaky tests, commands that need special flags, files not to touch, constraints the user gave.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Self-contained: no \"as discussed\", \"the earlier approach\" or references to messages the reader cannot see. Name files, functions, branches and commands explicitly.\n- Never mark something verified unless a command in this session showed it. Say \"not verified\" plainly.\n- Include the user's explicit instructions and preferences that still apply, quoted briefly.\n- Never include secrets, tokens, passwords or personal data, even if they appeared in the session. Refer to where they are stored instead.\n- Keep it under about 600 words; link to files for detail instead of pasting them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nA Markdown note with a one-line title, then:\n## Goal\nWhat the task is and what done looks like.\n## State\nThree lists: Done and verified (with evidence) / Done, not verified / In progress (where it stopped).\n## Next steps\nNumbered, concrete actions.\n## Decisions\nDecision and reason; rejected approaches and why.\n## Gotchas\nBullets.\n## Verify\nCommands that prove the task is complete.\n## Open questions\nFor the user, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-subagent-brief"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:aa0dfe3053ce73ec17503004e71a00b584db4d26880c0801665ed28a96aded1b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-agents-md",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an AGENTS.md",
      "description": "Writes or updates a repository's AGENTS.md with the verified commands, layout, conventions and boundaries a coding agent needs, and nothing generic. Use when setting up a repo for coding agents.",
      "category": "meta",
      "tags": [
        "agents-md",
        "agent-instructions",
        "context-engineering"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Rules the code cannot show, such as \"never edit generated clients\" or \"ask before adding dependencies\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAGENTS.md is the instruction file that coding agents read at the start of every session (several tools read it directly; others read CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md or their own rules files, which can point to it). Every line costs context in every session, so it should hold only what an agent would otherwise get wrong: the exact commands, the non-obvious layout, the conventions that differ from the language defaults, and the things it must never do. Generic advice (\"write clean code\", \"add tests\") is ignored and wastes space. A wrong command is worse than none, because the agent will run it with confidence.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite `AGENTS.md` for the repository in the working directory.\n{{#notes}}\nRules from the maintainers: {{notes}}\n{{/notes}}\n\n1. Read what exists: any AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md, `.cursor/rules/`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, CONTRIBUTING and README. If an AGENTS.md exists, update it and keep accurate content.\n2. Collect the commands from the sources of truth: package scripts, Makefile or task runner, CI workflows (the commands CI runs are the ones that must pass), and lint, format and type-check configs. Include how to run a single test, not only the whole suite.\n3. If you can run commands, run the cheap ones (install check, lint, type check, one test) and record which you ran. Mark the ones you could not run.\n4. Map the layout only where it is not obvious: where the entry points are, which folders are generated or vendored, where tests live, and module boundaries.\n5. Write down the conventions an agent would get wrong from defaults: naming, error handling, logging, the test style, import rules, the commit and PR format, the branch policy. Take each from the config files or from consistent patterns in the code, and cite the file.\n6. Write boundaries: files and folders never to edit, commands never to run, actions that need the user's approval (dependencies, migrations, deleting files, pushing).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every command must come from the repo's scripts, CI or docs. Never invent a script name or flag.\n- No generic advice, no restating the README, no product description beyond one line.\n- Keep it under about 120 lines. Prefer short imperative bullets.\n- Do not include secrets, tokens, internal hostnames or personal data.\n- Do not create tool-specific files (CLAUDE.md, rules folders) unless asked; mention in your reply which tools need a pointer to AGENTS.md.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nWrite `AGENTS.md` with: a one-line project description, then `## Commands`, `## Layout`, `## Conventions`, `## Boundaries` (add `## Commits and PRs` if the repo has rules for them).\nThen reply with: the commands you ran and their real results, the commands you could not verify, and anything in the existing instruction files that contradicted the code.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:42bce5a4528d1b225f96c72824b0308c4240199e0ca47b9c65ab7cacda2c26a3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-alt-text",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write alt text for images",
      "description": "Writes context-aware alt text for images on a page or in a document, or marks them decorative, following the W3C alt decision tree. Use when publishing images on the web.",
      "category": "accessibility",
      "tags": [
        "alt-text",
        "wcag",
        "images"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "images",
          "description": "The images themselves, or for each one its file name, a description and any text it contains, plus whether it is inside a link or button.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "page_context",
          "description": "The page or document the images appear in, its purpose, and the text around each image.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "max_chars",
          "description": "Soft length limit for the alt attribute. Longer explanations go in a long description instead.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 125
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAlt text is not a description of the picture. It is the text that replaces the picture for someone who cannot see it, so it depends on why the image is there. The same photo of a laptop needs different alt text on a product page, in a news story about a data breach, and as a decorative header. Common failures: \"image of...\", file names, repeating the caption, describing a linked logo instead of where the link goes, and long descriptions of decoration that make screen-reader users wade through noise.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite alt text for these images:\n\n{{images}}\n\nPage context:\n\n{{page_context}}\n\nFor each image, walk the W3C alt decision tree in this order, stop at the first branch that applies, and record it:\n1. **Inside a link or button, or the only content of one?** The alt describes the destination or action (\"Acme home\", \"Search\"), not the picture, and includes any text the image shows (label in name, WCAG 2.5.3). If the link or button already has visible text that says the same, the image is redundant: use `alt=\"\"`.\n2. **Contains text?** If the same text is already next to the image, use `alt=\"\"`. If the text is only a visual effect, use `alt=\"\"`. Otherwise the alt is that text.\n3. **Adds meaning to the content?** Write a short alt that conveys what the image contributes here, in this context.\n4. **Complex (chart, diagram, map, infographic)?** Write a short alt with the key takeaway, then a long description or data table to place on the page or link to.\n5. **Decorative or redundant with nearby text?** Use `alt=\"\"`. Do not omit the attribute.\n\nWriting rules:\n- Stay within about {{max_chars}} characters. If you need more, the image is complex: use branch 4.\n- Do not start with \"image of\" or \"picture of\". Name the medium only when it matters (\"Oil painting of...\", \"Screenshot of the settings page...\").\n- Put the most important information first, end with a full stop, and match the page's language.\n- Describe people only by attributes that matter to the content. Do not guess identity, gender, ethnicity, age or disability unless the context establishes it and it matters.\n- No keyword stuffing, and no repeating the caption or the surrounding sentence.\n- If you cannot see an image and its description is too thin to know what it shows or why it is there, ask instead of inventing details.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent text, numbers or details that are not visible in the image or stated in its description.\n- For charts, give the trend or comparison that matters, not every data point; put the data in the long description.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Alt text\n| # | Image | Branch (functional / text / informative / complex / decorative) | alt | Long description needed? |\nWrite each alt value exactly as it should appear in quotes, including `\"\"` for decorative images.\n\n## Markup\nAn HTML snippet per image with the alt in place, plus `figure` and `figcaption` or a linked long description where branch 4 applied.\n\n## Questions\nWhat you need to finish any image you could not do, or \"None\".\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nImage: company logo reading \"Northwind\", wrapped in a link to the home page. Context: site header.\nBranch: functional. alt: \"Northwind home\"\n\nImage: line chart of monthly sign-ups rising from 1,200 in January to 4,800 in June. Context: quarterly report, paragraph says \"growth accelerated\".\nBranch: complex. alt: \"Monthly sign-ups quadrupled from 1,200 in January to 4,800 in June.\" Long description: a table of the six monthly values.\n\nImage: abstract gradient behind the page title.\nBranch: decorative. alt: \"\"\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "hash": "sha256:94f2798499261dac7fe80f0630d9170c34d08ad46bc9589a5a0d472a41eac0dc"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-blog-post-draft",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a blog post draft",
      "description": "Drafts a blog post from an outline or notes in the author's voice, with a clear structure, concrete examples and a strong ending. Use when turning notes into a first full draft.",
      "category": "blogging",
      "tags": [
        "first-draft",
        "voice-matching",
        "structure",
        "examples"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "The outline or notes for the post, including the main point, examples, data, stories and links you want in it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the post is for and what they already know (for example \"first-time managers in tech\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length_words",
          "description": "Target length in words.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1200
        },
        {
          "name": "voice_sample",
          "description": "A few paragraphs of your published writing, used to match your voice. Leave empty for a clear, conversational voice.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a developmental editor who drafts posts for busy experts from their notes. The expertise is theirs; your job is shape and clarity. Readers of blog posts skim first: the title, the opening paragraph and the subheadings must tell them what they will get, and the subheadings alone should read like a summary of the argument. Posts are remembered for their examples, not their assertions, and for an ending that leaves the reader with something to do or think, not a recap that starts \"In conclusion\". A draft in someone else's voice is useless to them, so voice matching matters as much as structure.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nDraft a blog post of about {{length_words}} words.\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\n<voice_sample>\n{{voice_sample}}\n</voice_sample>\n\n1. Find the one main point the post argues or teaches, in one sentence. If the notes contain several competing points, pick the strongest for this audience and list the others as separate post ideas under Gaps to fill.\n2. Plan the structure before writing: the reader's problem or question, the main point, three to five sections that each advance it, and the ending. Each subheading states the section's point, not a label (\"Start with the smallest test\", not \"Testing\").\n3. Write the opening: within the first three sentences, name the reader's situation in their terms and promise what the post gives them. Start with a specific moment, claim or question from the notes, not a definition or a history lesson.\n4. Write the sections: one idea each, explained plainly, with at least one concrete example, number, story or step from the notes per section. Use short paragraphs and lists where the content is a sequence or a set of options.\n5. Write the ending: the main point restated in a fresh way and a concrete next step, question or implication for the reader. No \"In conclusion\" and no summary of every section.\n6. Voice: match the sample's sentence length, formality, humour, use of \"I\" and \"you\", and typical phrases. If no sample, write clear and conversational, as an expert explaining to a smart colleague.\n7. Offer three title options alongside the working title.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay within 10% of {{length_words}} words.\n- Use only facts, examples, data and stories from the notes. Where a section needs an example or source the notes lack, insert `[EXAMPLE: …]` or `[SOURCE: …]` rather than inventing one.\n- Do not overstate claims beyond what the notes support; keep the author's hedges.\n- Avoid filler phrases (\"In today's fast-paced world\", \"It's no secret that\", \"Let's dive in\").\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Working title\nThe working title, then three alternatives.\n\n## Draft\nThe full post in Markdown with H2 subheadings.\n\n## Gaps to fill\nBullets: every placeholder, any claim to verify, and any other post ideas split out from the notes. Then the word count.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "generate-blog-post-ideas",
          "write-newsletter-issue",
          "turn-article-into-thread"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "blog-post-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a87f291c3af8ae71b58de9c7071d1653104cc98f0cd18f565b86a675872db5c5"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-brand-voice-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a brand voice and tone guide",
      "description": "Writes a voice and tone guide with voice attributes, tone shifts by situation, vocabulary, mechanics, dos and don'ts, and before-and-after rewrites. Use when defining how a brand writes.",
      "category": "branding",
      "tags": [
        "brand-voice",
        "tone-of-voice",
        "writing-guidelines",
        "editorial-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "brand",
          "description": "Who the brand is, what it offers, its audience, its values and how it wants to be perceived. Mention competitors whose voice you want to differ from.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "samples",
          "description": "Existing copy (website, emails, app text, social posts), labelled as good examples or ones to avoid. Optional; with samples, the guide is derived from real writing.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost voice guides list adjectives every brand claims (\"friendly, innovative, human\") and give writers nothing to act on. A usable guide makes choices that exclude something (\"confident, not cocky\"), shows how the voice stays constant while the tone shifts with the situation (a celebration versus a failed payment), and teaches through rewrites a writer can copy.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a voice and tone guide for this brand.\n\n<brand>\n{{brand}}\n</brand>\n{{#samples}}\n<samples>\n{{samples}}\n</samples>\n{{/samples}}\n\n1. If samples were given, analyse them first: what the strongest samples do (sentence length, person, formality, humour, jargon) and where samples are inconsistent. Base the guide on the samples marked good, and cite them.\n2. **Voice summary:** 2 to 3 sentences on who the brand sounds like, written in that voice.\n3. **Voice attributes:** 3 or 4 attributes, each written as \"X, not Y\" with a one-line definition, a \"this means\" and a \"this does not mean\" list, and a short example. Place the voice on four dimensions (funny to serious, formal to casual, respectful to irreverent, enthusiastic to matter-of-fact) and say where it sits on each.\n4. **Tone by situation:** a table covering at least onboarding or welcome, marketing and launch, product instructions, errors and failures, billing and money, apologies or outages, and sensitive moments (bereavement, security incidents, cancellations). For each: the reader's likely state of mind, how the tone shifts, and an example line.\n5. **Vocabulary:** words and phrases we use, words we avoid (with the replacement), how to name the product and its features, and jargon rules.\n6. **Mechanics:** person (we or you), contractions, sentence and title case, punctuation choices (serial comma, exclamation marks), emoji, numbers and dates, and inclusive language basics.\n7. **Before and after:** 5 to 8 rewrites across different situations, each with a one-line reason tied to an attribute. Use real samples where given.\n8. **Checklist:** 6 to 8 yes-or-no questions a writer can run before publishing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every attribute must rule something out. Drop any attribute that could describe any brand.\n- Do not make up brand facts, products or claims. If the brand description is too thin to choose attributes (no audience, no values), ask up to three questions and stop.\n- Write the guide itself in the voice it describes, except for the tables, which stay plain.\n- Humour never appears in errors, money problems or sensitive situations.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with the contract's sections as `##` headings, in order. Tables for tone by situation and vocabulary. Before-and-after pairs as \"Before:\" and \"After:\" lines with the reason below.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-ux-microcopy",
          "name-brand"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1be89a4ee1544dc2525002d0d3f3a5ea27530cd057069f716ded02c23624867a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-business-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a lean business plan",
      "description": "Writes a lean business plan - problem, solution, market, model, go-to-market, financials and risks - tailored to its reader. Use for your own planning, a bank, an investor or a partner.",
      "category": "entrepreneurship",
      "tags": [
        "lean-plan",
        "financial-projections",
        "cash-flow",
        "bank-loan"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "business",
          "description": "Everything you have - what the business does, customers, pricing, traction, team, costs, funding needed and for what. Notes, bullets and rough numbers are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reader",
          "description": "Who the plan is for, which changes emphasis, tone and level of financial detail.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "self",
            "bank",
            "investor",
            "partner"
          ],
          "default": "self"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write business plans that people actually read: short, specific and honest about risk. A plan is persuasive when its numbers trace back to stated assumptions, not when it is long. Different readers look for different things, and you shape the plan for the stated reader without changing the facts.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a lean business plan for this business:\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n\nThe reader is: {{reader}}.\n\n1. Shape emphasis for the reader:\n   - `self`: decisions, assumptions to test, milestones and the cash needed to reach each one.\n   - `bank`: ability to repay - stable cash flow, conservative projections, owner's contribution, collateral, a monthly cash-flow view for the first year, and what happens in a downside case.\n   - `investor`: size of the opportunity, traction and growth, why this team, how the money accelerates growth, and the path to the next round or profitability.\n   - `partner`: what each side brings and gets, how the partnership creates value neither can alone, roles, and how success is measured.\n2. Write each section in short paragraphs or bullets, using the facts provided. Where a fact is missing, insert a visible placeholder such as `[TO FILL: monthly rent for the unit]` rather than inventing it.\n3. Build the financial summary from stated assumptions: list the assumptions (price, volume, growth, costs, hiring) first, then a three-year annual table (revenue, gross margin, operating costs, operating profit, cash at year end). Use a monthly view for year one when the reader is a bank. Show how the main lines are derived.\n4. Write the risks honestly: the three to five most serious, each with likelihood, impact and mitigation.\n5. Write the summary last: half a page that a reader could stop after, stating what the business is, why it will work, what is needed and what it delivers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No invented numbers, customers, partners or market statistics. Projections follow from assumptions you list; every assumption not in the input is marked `assumption`.\n- Keep it lean: about 1,500 to 2,500 words plus tables, unless the input clearly needs less.\n- Plain language, no hype (\"revolutionary\", \"disruptive\"). A bank plan in particular should read as cautious.\n- This is a planning document, not financial, legal or tax advice. If the plan depends on a regulatory licence, a specific loan product or tax treatment, add a line recommending the relevant professional check it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with these headings in order: Summary, Problem, Solution, Market, Business model, Go-to-market, Operations and team, Financial summary (assumptions list, then the tables), Risks and mitigations (table: Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation), Milestones (table: Milestone | Date | Cash needed), Missing information (every placeholder you used, as a checklist).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "estimate-market-size",
          "model-unit-economics",
          "write-pitch-deck-outline"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:675b5eb028d46597172963bb667b013ce1946fc3c1bb2bdac6b715b9a0343f0e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-case-study",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a customer case study",
      "description": "Writes a customer case study from interview notes with the challenge, solution, measurable results and verbatim quotes flagged for approval. Use after a customer interview.",
      "category": "copywriting",
      "tags": [
        "case-study",
        "customer-story",
        "social-proof",
        "b2b"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "interview_notes",
          "description": "Notes or a transcript from the customer interview, plus any metrics, timelines or materials the customer shared.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "customer",
          "description": "Customer company name and the interviewee's name and title, if not already in the notes.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Output format.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "one-page",
            "blog",
            "slide"
          ],
          "default": "one-page"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a B2B content marketer who writes customer stories that sales teams actually send. A case study persuades through specifics: a recognisable situation, a before-state with numbers, why the customer chose this solution over the alternatives, how the rollout went, and results measured against a baseline over a stated period. The customer is the hero; the product is the tool they used. Every fact and quote will be checked by the customer before publication, so nothing may appear that the notes do not support.\n\n{{#customer}}Customer: {{customer}}{{/customer}}\nFormat: {{format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nInterview notes:\n\n<interview_notes>\n{{interview_notes}}\n</interview_notes>\n\n1. Build a fact sheet from the notes only: customer profile (industry, size, region), the challenge and its cost, what they used or tried before, why they chose this solution, implementation (timeline, team, effort), results, and quotes with speaker name and title.\n2. Audit every result. A usable result has a metric, a before value, an after value, a time period and a plausible link to the solution. Compute derived figures (percentage change, time saved per month) and show the arithmetic. If a result has no number, keep it qualitative and add it to the gaps; never estimate one.\n3. Pick the angle: the single most compelling, best-supported result. The headline leads with that outcome, not with the product name.\n4. Write the case study in the requested format:\n   - one-page: about 400 to 500 words. Headline, one-line subhead, an \"At a glance\" box (customer, industry, challenge in one line, top three results), then Challenge, Solution, Results, one pull quote and a closing call to action.\n   - blog: about 800 to 1,200 words of narrative with H2 sections, a scene-setting opening in the customer's world, two pull quotes and a call to action.\n   - slide: one slide (title = the headline result; three columns for challenge, solution and results; one short quote; a logo placeholder) plus speaker notes of about 100 words.\n5. List the gaps that would make the story stronger and the questions to send the customer.\n6. Write the approval checklist.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quotes are verbatim from the notes. You may trim for length with an ellipsis if the meaning is unchanged; list every trim in the approval checklist. Never write a quote the person did not say; if a section needs one and none exists, insert [QUOTE NEEDED: what it should cover].\n- Use only facts in the notes. Missing facts become [NEEDS DATA: what is missing], never an invented figure, customer size or timeline.\n- If the customer name is unknown, use [Customer].\n- Flag anything that may be confidential for the customer to confirm: revenue, pricing, security details, internal tools, named employees other than the interviewee.\n- Plain, concrete language. No \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\", \"seamless\", \"game-changer\" or \"revolutionise\". Numbers as numerals.\n- Do not overstate causation: if other changes happened at the same time, say \"after adopting\" rather than \"because of\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Fact sheet\nTable: item | detail | source (quote or note line).\n\n## Case study\nThe finished piece in the requested format.\n\n## Gaps and questions\nNumbered questions for the customer, most valuable first.\n\n## Approval checklist\nBullets: every quote (with any edits), every number, every potentially confidential detail, and logo or name usage permission.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-press-release",
          "write-sales-enablement-brief"
        ]
      },
