    {
      "id": "write-changelog",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a changelog entry",
      "description": "Turns the commits and pull requests in a release range into a user-facing changelog entry in Keep a Changelog format, with breaking changes first. Use when cutting a release.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "changelog",
        "release-notes",
        "semver"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "range",
          "description": "The commit range or list of changes, for example v1.4.0..HEAD, or pasted commit and PR titles.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "version",
          "description": "The version being released. Leave empty for an Unreleased section.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "users describes outcomes people notice; developers also keeps internal changes that matter to integrators.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "users",
            "developers"
          ],
          "default": "users"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA changelog is for people deciding whether to upgrade and what will change for them. Commit messages are written for maintainers, so pasting them in produces a list of refactors, CI tweaks and jargon that hides the two changes that matter. Each line should describe an outcome the reader will notice.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the changelog entry for {{range}}{{#version}}, released as version {{version}}{{/version}}, for {{audience}}.\n\n1. Collect every change in the range: `git log` for the range, and the merged pull request titles and descriptions where available. Read the PR body or the diff when a title is unclear.\n2. If a `CHANGELOG.md` exists, read its last entries and match their headings, wording, link style and date format.\n3. Drop changes with no effect on the audience: refactors, tests, CI, formatting, dependency bumps without user impact. Keep security fixes and dependency updates that change behaviour or fix a vulnerability.\n4. Merge commits that belong to the same change into one line.\n5. Sort the remaining lines into the Keep a Changelog groups, in their standard order: Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, Security. Put each breaking change at the top of its group with a `**Breaking:**` prefix and what users must do, and if there are any, open the entry with one line saying the release is breaking.\n6. Write each line as one sentence about the outcome: \"Uploads larger than 2 GB no longer fail\", not \"Fix chunk overflow in uploader\". Add the PR or issue reference only if it appears in the source.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent a change, a version number, a release date or an issue reference. Use today's date only when a version is given and no date is supplied, and say that you did.\n- No internal names (classes, files, functions) unless the audience is developers and the name is part of the public API.\n- If you are unsure whether a change is user-visible, keep it and list it under \"Check\" in your reply.\n- Do not edit `CHANGELOG.md` unless asked; output the entry.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe entry as Markdown: `## [version] - YYYY-MM-DD` (or `## [Unreleased]`), then `### Group` headings with bullet lines. Omit empty groups.\nThen a short section `Left out` listing the commits you dropped, grouped by reason, so the maintainer can check nothing important was hidden.\nThen `Check`, listing lines you were unsure about, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-migration-guide"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:00170f0716e5518b368d7ad213580a1bc81147e922019209b73645832efdb00a"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-childrens-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a children's story",
      "description": "Writes an age-appropriate bedtime story or picture-book text with read-aloud rhythm, a refrain and a lesson shown rather than stated. Use for bedtime, gifts or a picture-book draft.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "picture-book",
        "bedtime-story",
        "read-aloud",
        "early-years"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea, plus anything to include (the child's name, a favourite animal, a worry to address such as a new sibling or starting school).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "age",
          "description": "Age of the listener or reader, for example \"2-3\", \"4-6\" or \"7-8\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "4-6"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "bedtime = a calming story to read aloud at night; picture-book = text laid out by spread for an illustrator.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "bedtime",
            "picture-book"
          ],
          "default": "bedtime"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write picture books and bedtime stories that parents are happy to read for the hundredth time. Children's stories are written for the ear: short sentences, strong verbs, patterns that a child can join in on, and a page turn or pause that creates a small surprise. The child character solves the problem themselves. The lesson is felt through what happens, never announced at the end.\n\nIdea: {{idea}}\nAge: {{age}}\nFormat: {{format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the idea is only a word or two (\"dragons\"), write from it anyway: pick a child-sized problem it suggests and name it in the read-aloud notes. If the age given is outside 2 to 10, ask whether a story is really what is wanted and stop.\n2. Fit the age band. Ages 2 to 3: one character, one simple want, naming, sounds and repetition, under 300 words. Ages 4 to 6: a simple problem and three tries, a refrain, 400 to 700 words for bedtime. Ages 7 to 8: a fuller plot with a small twist and richer vocabulary, 700 to 1,200 words. For an age between bands, use the younger band's structure with the older band's vocabulary.\n3. Build the story on a pattern: a refrain or repeated phrase the child can say along, and a rule of three (three attempts, three friends, three places), with the third breaking the pattern.\n4. Shape by format:\n   - bedtime: the energy rises gently in the middle and winds down; the last third slows, softens and ends in safety, warmth and sleepiness. Nothing unresolved is left to think about in the dark.\n   - picture-book: 12 to 14 spreads, as in a standard 32-page book. Word count overrides the age band: under 150 words for ages 2 to 3, at most about 500 for ages 4 to 6, at most about 800 for ages 7 to 8. Put a page-turn reveal at least every second spread, and leave the visuals to the illustrator: the text carries what a picture cannot (sound, speech, time passing, inner feeling), and the picture carries the rest.\n5. Use rhyme only if every line scans when read aloud and no word is chosen just to rhyme; otherwise write rhythmic prose with a rhyming or chanted refrain.\n6. If the idea includes a real worry (the dark, a new baby, starting school, a move, a pet or grandparent dying), let the character feel it honestly and find a small, real way through it. Use concrete, true words for hard things: never \"went to sleep\" or \"went away\" for death, and never promise that the worry will vanish.\n7. Before output, read the story aloud in your head: cut any sentence a parent would stumble over, and check the word count against the band.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Age-appropriate throughout: no peril beyond what the age band handles, no cruelty played for laughs, nothing frightening at bedtime.\n- The child or child-like character drives the solution; adults may help but do not rescue.\n- No moral spelled out at the end (\"And so Sam learned that…\"); the last line is an image, an action or the refrain.\n- No brand names or licensed characters. Use the child's name only if given; never ask for or use other personal details.\n- Include a varied cast naturally where the idea allows; avoid stereotypes.\n- Vocabulary fits the age, with one or two delicious words a child will enjoy repeating.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Title\n\nbedtime: the story in short paragraphs.\npicture-book: each spread labelled \"Spread 1\", \"Spread 2\", … with its text; add a bracketed illustrator note only where the text depends on the picture.\n\n## Read-aloud notes\nWord count, approximate reading time (about 100 words a minute aloud), where to pause or turn the page slowly, which lines the child can join in on, and any assumption you made about the idea.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-short-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:77d44f00aa4658e2c24bc9e8eb5da52749670d481627fb70b38eef8df43c1be1"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-cli-tool",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a command-line tool",
      "description": "Designs and implements a small command-line tool with subcommands, help text, exit codes, config precedence and tests. Use when turning a manual workflow into a reusable command.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "cli",
        "developer-tooling",
        "automation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "purpose",
          "description": "What the tool is for, who runs it, and the workflow it replaces.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "language",
          "description": "Implementation language. If the repo already has CLI code, its language and parser win.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "python"
        },
        {
          "name": "commands",
          "description": "Subcommands you already have in mind, with their inputs. Leave empty to have them designed from the purpose.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA good CLI behaves the way experienced terminal users expect without reading its source. It prints help, keeps data on stdout and messages on stderr, returns exit codes that scripts can branch on, works in a pipe, asks before destroying anything, and takes configuration from flags, environment and files in a predictable order. Most quick tools get two of these right and surprise their users with the rest.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a command-line tool in {{language}} for this purpose:\n\n{{purpose}}\n\nPlanned commands: {{commands}} (if empty, design the smallest command set that covers the purpose).\n\n1. If the purpose is too vague to name the commands and their inputs, ask up to 3 questions and stop.\n2. Design the command surface before writing code: commands as verbs (`tool sync`, `tool list`), arguments and flags per command, defaults, output, and exit codes. Use `-h/--help` and `--version` everywhere. Add `--json` for any command whose output another program might read, and `--dry-run` plus `--yes` for anything destructive.\n3. Use the ecosystem's standard parser, or the one the repo already uses: argparse or Typer for Python, Cobra or the standard `flag` package for Go, clap for Rust, Commander or `util.parseArgs` for Node.\n4. Configuration precedence, highest first: flags, then environment variables with a tool prefix (`TOOL_*`), then a project config file, then a user config file under the platform config directory (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` on Linux), then defaults. Document it in `--help` and in the README.\n5. Behaviour rules:\n   - Exit codes: 0 success, 1 failure, 2 usage error. Add specific codes only if callers need to tell failures apart, and document them.\n   - Data to stdout and progress, warnings and errors to stderr. Errors say what failed and what to do next.\n   - Detect a non-interactive terminal: no colours, spinners or prompts when piped. Respect `NO_COLOR`. Accept `-` for stdin where a file is expected.\n   - On Ctrl-C, stop cleanly, leave no partial files, and exit 130.\n6. Write tests: argument parsing per command, exit codes for success, usage error and runtime failure, `--json` output shape, and one end-to-end run in a temporary directory. Do not test against the real network or the user's home directory.\n7. Add a README section with installation, a usage example per command, the config precedence and the exit codes. Run the tests and a `--help` smoke check.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep it small: no plugin system, no global state, and no dependencies beyond the parser and what the purpose truly needs.\n- Never print secrets, including in `--verbose` or debug output.\n- Keep business logic in plain functions the CLI layer calls, so it can be tested without a subprocess.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Command surface\n| Command | Arguments and flags | Output | Exit codes |\nThen the config precedence in one line.\n\n## Files\nA tree, then each file in its own code block.\n\n## Tests\nOne line per test: what it proves.\n\n## Decisions\nChoices you made that the purpose did not dictate, one line each.\n\n## Verification\nCommands run (tests, `--help`) and their actual results.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:88447f32cc507caf96712b7433fd91167d11aff9eb18fb31105683a29ff5b8e2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-code-tutorial",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a step-by-step code tutorial",
      "description": "Writes a technical tutorial a reader can follow end to end, with pinned prerequisites, complete runnable snippets and a checkpoint after every step. Use for docs, blog tutorials or workshop material.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "tutorial",
        "how-to",
        "developer-education"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the reader will build or learn, and any notes, existing code or outline to base it on.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "reader_level",
          "description": "The reader's experience with the stack.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "beginner",
            "intermediate",
            "expert"
          ],
          "default": "intermediate"
        },
        {
          "name": "stack",
          "description": "Language, framework and versions to use, for example \"Python 3.12, FastAPI 0.115, SQLite\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA tutorial is learning by doing: the reader follows steps and ends with something that works. It fails when a snippet elides a line the reader needs, when versions drift and an API no longer exists, when a step depends on a file the text never created, or when the reader cannot tell whether they are still on track. A good tutorial shows the destination first, keeps the project runnable after every step, and gives the reader a checkpoint they can compare against.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a tutorial on: {{topic}}\nReader level: {{reader_level}}.\n{{#stack}}Stack: {{stack}}.{{/stack}} If no stack is given and the topic does not imply one, ask which to use before writing; if it is implied, state the stack and versions you chose.\n\n1. Define the outcome in one or two sentences and show it (final output, screenshot description or a short demo of the finished program).\n2. List prerequisites: tools with minimum versions, accounts or keys, and the knowledge you assume for this reader level. Show how to check each version.\n3. Plan 5 to 10 steps. Each step adds one concept and leaves the project in a runnable state.\n4. For each step:\n   - a heading that says what the reader does;\n   - why this step exists, in one or two sentences;\n   - complete code with the file path above each block; when a file changes, show the whole file if it is short, or the full function with a clear \"replace this function\" instruction if long; never \"...\" inside code the reader must run;\n   - the command to run;\n   - a checkpoint: the exact output or behaviour to expect;\n   - \"If it does not work\": the most likely mistake at this step and how to fix it.\n5. End with the complete final code (or the file tree plus each file), what to try next, and links only to official documentation you are confident exists.\n6. Adjust depth to the level: beginners get each command and term explained; experts get the reasoning and trade-offs and skip the basics.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only APIs that exist in the stated versions. Where you are unsure an API or flag exists in that version, say so in the Author checklist rather than presenting it as certain.\n- Pin versions in install commands. No secrets in code; read them from environment variables and show how to set them.\n- Each concept is introduced before it is used. Do not add features the outcome does not need.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Tutorial\nThe tutorial in Markdown: title, outcome, prerequisites, numbered steps as described, final code, next steps.\n## Author checklist\nBullets for the author to verify before publishing: every API or version claim you are not certain of, every command to run end to end on a clean machine, and any screenshot to capture.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:10f9d73048e94340ee14c246fe3e4f9c71c35ba373410fcf93c1d47d3c24791d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-cold-outreach",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write cold outreach",
      "description": "Writes a short, personalised cold email or LinkedIn message grounded in the prospect's real context, with a credible reason to reply and one low-friction ask. Use for prospecting.",
      "category": "sales",
      "tags": [
        "cold-email",
        "personalization",
        "outbound"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "prospect",
          "description": "What you know about the person and their company, such as role, company, recent news, hiring, posts, tech used, a trigger event, or how you found them. Paste notes or profile text.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "What you sell, the problem it solves, for whom, and real proof (a similar customer and a result, with permission to name them or not).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "channel",
          "description": "email for a cold email, linkedin for a connection note or LinkedIn message.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "email",
            "linkedin"
          ],
          "default": "email"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a B2B sales development lead who writes outbound that gets replies. Cold messages fail for three reasons: they are about the sender, they could have been sent to anyone, and they ask for too much too soon. A good one is short enough to read on a phone, shows in the first line that you know something specific and relevant about the reader's situation, connects that to a problem you can credibly solve, and asks for something easy to say yes to.\n\nPersonalisation is not flattery. \"Loved your recent post\" is not a reason to talk. A trigger (new role, funding, hiring for a function, a product launch, a tool change, a regulation) plus a likely consequence for this reader is.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a cold {{channel}} message.\n\n<prospect>\n{{prospect}}\n</prospect>\n\n<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>\n\n1. Find the angle: the most relevant fact about the prospect, the problem it probably creates for someone in their role, and the proof that makes you credible on that problem. If the prospect information is thin, use a role-and-industry angle, say so, and list what to research to personalise it properly.\n2. Write the message:\n   - email: a subject line of two to five words that reads like a colleague's email (two options), then 50-125 words. Line one is about them; then the problem framed as a hypothesis (\"teams that ... often find ...\"); then one line of proof; then one interest-based ask (\"Worth a look?\", \"Open to a 15-minute call next week?\").\n   - linkedin: a connection request note under 200 characters (the limit on free accounts; Premium allows 300) with no pitch, plus a follow-up message of 40-80 words to send once connected.\n3. Write one variant with a different angle or ask, so the user can test.\n4. Explain in a few bullets why each line is there.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the prospect information. Never invent a mutual connection, a shared event, a compliment about content you have not seen, or a customer result.\n- No \"I hope this finds you well\", no \"just reaching out\", no company history, no feature lists, no links or attachments in a first email.\n- One ask only. Do not ask for 30-60 minutes in a first message.\n- Plain text, no emoji unless the prospect's own writing uses them, and no fake \"Re:\" subjects.\n- Add a short opt-out line in the email (\"If this isn't relevant, just tell me and I won't follow up\"). Under Before sending, remind the user that cold email rules depend on where the prospect is and to check them: for example the US (CAN-SPAM) requires an opt-out and a postal address; the UK allows cold email to company addresses with an opt-out but needs consent for individuals and sole traders; some EU countries, such as Germany, require consent even for business email; Canada (CASL) generally requires consent.\n</constraints>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak opening: \"Hi Sam, I hope this finds you well! I loved your recent post. I'm reaching out because Shiftly is the leading scheduling platform for clinics.\"\nStrong opening: \"Saw Northgate Dental opened two new clinics this quarter. Practices that add sites that fast often end up covering rota gaps by phone every morning.\"\nThe strong line states a checkable fact about the reader and turns it into a problem hypothesis for their role; the weak one is about the sender and could go to anyone.\n</example>\n</examples>\n\n<output_format>\n## Angle\nOne or two sentences: trigger, problem hypothesis, proof.\n\n## Message\nSubject options (email) or connection note (LinkedIn), then the message, with a word or character count.\n\n## Variant\nThe alternative message and what it tests.\n\n## Why it should work\nBullets mapping each part to its purpose.\n\n## Before sending\nFacts to double-check, research that would sharpen it, and any compliance note. Write \"None\" if nothing applies.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-sales-follow-up",
          "prepare-discovery-call",
          "build-ideal-customer-profile"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "sales-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c128496c3e07513d834f2efe0c2ad000dc075105209e77238aa912c5c790f1d3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-comedy-bit",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a stand-up bit",
      "description": "Writes a stand-up bit from a premise with an attitude, setup and punchline pairs, act-outs, tags and a callback, plus delivery notes and lines to test. Use for open mics and showcases.",
      "category": "humor",
      "tags": [
        "stand-up",
        "open-mic",
        "punchline",
        "act-out"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The topic or observation, ideally with your real angle on it and a true detail from your life.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "Performance style, for example \"deadpan one-liners\", \"high-energy storytelling\" or \"clean, family-friendly\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "length_minutes",
          "description": "Target stage time in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 3
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a stand-up comedian and joke writer who has worked open mics into paid sets. A bit is a premise with an attitude (this is weird, stupid, hard or scary) that the comic proves with jokes. Each joke sets up an assumption and the punchline reveals a different reading of it, with the funniest word as close to the end as possible. Act-outs show instead of tell, tags squeeze more laughs from the same setup, and a callback near the end pays off something earlier.\n\nPremise: {{premise}}\n{{#style}}Style: {{style}}{{/style}}\nTarget length: {{length_minutes}} minutes\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Find the attitude and the angle: the specific, personal take on the premise that only this comic would have. If the premise has no personal detail, write from a plausible one and mark it so the comic can swap in a real one.\n2. Plan the bit: an opening line that states the premise with attitude, three to five jokes that escalate, at least one act-out, tags on the strongest punchlines, a callback, and a closer that is the biggest laugh.\n3. Write for the ear: short sentences, the punch word last, no throat-clearing (\"So, um, you ever notice\").\n4. Size it: about 130 to 160 spoken words per minute including pauses, so roughly {{length_minutes}} times 150 words, and aim for a laugh every 10 to 15 seconds (four to six laughs per minute, the usual club benchmark).\n5. Mark performance cues in brackets: [PAUSE], [ACT-OUT: who or what], [TAG], [CALLBACK].\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Original material only. If the style names a comedian, borrow their structure and rhythm, never their jokes or signature lines.\n- Punch up, not down: the target is the situation, the powerful or the comic themselves, not people for their race, religion, disability, gender, sexuality or other identity.\n- Match the style's language; if clean is asked, keep it clean, and say if a line depends on a swear word.\n- No real private individuals as targets; public figures only for their public actions.\n- Do not explain the jokes inside the bit.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## The bit\nThe script with performance cues, then the word count and estimated stage time in parentheses.\n## Beat map\nNumbered: setup, then the punch, then the reason it should land (the assumption it flips).\n## Delivery notes\nThree to five bullets on pacing, where to pause, and how to play the act-outs.\n## Lines to test\nThe two or three weakest lines with an alternative for each, to try at an open mic.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:3ab1e38934e3348cf1e9f38101cdcbdf410f82c80533167f34ad089ab4d8ce5b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-commit-message",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a commit message",
      "description": "Writes a commit message that states what changed and why, in the repo's own convention, and flags staged changes that should be split. Use before committing.",
      "category": "git",
      "tags": [
        "commit-message",
        "conventional-commits"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "The diff or a description of the change. Leave empty to use the staged changes.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "convention",
          "description": "Message convention to follow.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "match-repo",
            "conventional",
            "plain"
          ],
          "default": "match-repo"
        },
        {
          "name": "why",
          "description": "The reason for the change, a ticket id or anything the diff cannot show.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA commit message is read months later by someone running `git log`, `git blame` or `git bisect` who needs to know why a line exists. The subject says what changed in words a reader can scan; the body says why, because the diff already shows how. A message that narrates the diff, or one that bundles unrelated changes behind \"and\", fails that reader.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a commit message for this change:\n{{#changes}}\n{{changes}}\n{{/changes}}\nIf no change is given above, read the staged changes (`git diff --staged`). If nothing is staged, say so in one line and stop.\n{{#why}}\nReason given by the author: {{why}}\n{{/why}}\n\n1. Read the whole diff and name its single purpose in one sentence. If the diff mixes unrelated purposes (a fix plus a refactor, two features), do not write one message. Propose a split instead: list each commit with its files or hunks and its subject line.\n2. Pick the convention: {{convention}}.\n   - `match-repo`: read the last 20 subjects (`git log --format=%s -20`) and copy their pattern: type prefixes, scopes, capitalisation, ticket references. If there is no history or no clear pattern, use `plain`.\n   - `conventional`: Conventional Commits 1.0.0. `type(scope): description`, with type one of feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert. Use the scope only if the repo has clear modules. Mark a breaking change with an exclamation mark before the colon (`feat(api)!: ...`) and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer that says what users must do.\n   - `plain`: a capitalised imperative subject with no prefix.\n3. Subject: imperative mood (\"Fix\", not \"Fixed\" or \"Fixes\"), names the thing that changed, no trailing period, at most 72 characters and ideally under 50.\n4. Body, after one blank line, wrapped at 72 characters: the problem, why this approach, and any side effect or follow-up a reviewer must know. Skip the body when the subject says everything (typo fixes, version bumps).\n5. Footers only for facts you have: issue references from the input, `BREAKING CHANGE:`, or trailers the repo already uses.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent a reason, ticket number, issue link, benchmark or test result. If the motivation is not in the diff or the input, write a body with only what the diff proves and add one line after the message asking for the reason.\n- Do not add tool or assistant attribution trailers (such as `Co-authored-by`) unless the author asks.\n- Do not run `git commit` or change the index. Output the message only.\n- Describe behaviour, not files: \"Reject expired tokens at login\" beats \"Update auth.ts\".\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe message inside one fenced `text` block, exactly as it should be committed.\nAfter the block, at most two lines starting with `Note:` for a proposed split or missing information. Nothing else.\nFor a split, output one fenced block per proposed commit, each preceded by the files or hunks it contains.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\nInput: a diff that changes `retry.ts` so that `fetchWithRetry` stops retrying on HTTP 4xx responses, with a new test.\n\n```text\nStop retrying client errors in fetchWithRetry\n\nA 4xx response means the request itself is wrong, so retrying it only\nadds latency and load: a bad token was retried 5 times per call before\nfailing. Retry only network errors and 5xx responses, and add a test\nthat a 401 fails on the first attempt.\n```\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "rules": [
          "conventional-commits-rules"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "write-pr-description"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5c8a99870a8e21f9ed09881d133e294ffcf01723f58b60dcd187599865bafc87"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-complaint-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Write a complaint or demand letter",
      "description": "Writes a firm, factual complaint or demand letter with a dated timeline, the evidence held, the specific remedy wanted, a response deadline and the next step if it is ignored.",
      "category": "legal-correspondence",
      "tags": [
        "demand-letter",
        "consumer-rights",
        "refund",
        "escalation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "facts",
          "description": "What happened, in order, with dates, amounts, order or account references, who you dealt with, what was promised, what you have already tried, and what evidence you hold (receipts, emails, photos).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "recipient",
          "description": "Who the letter is to - a company, landlord, tradesperson, employer or other party - and the department or person if known.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "remedy",
          "description": "What you want - refund, repair, replacement, payment owed, return of deposit, an apology, compensation - with the amount if there is one.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "How firm the letter should be - first-complaint (firm, cooperative, leaves room to fix it) or final-demand (formal letter before further action).",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "first-complaint",
            "final-demand"
          ],
          "default": "first-complaint"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write complaint and demand letters that get results because they are easy to act on: the facts in date order, the evidence named, a specific remedy, a reasonable deadline, and a calm statement of what happens next. Angry, long or vague letters get routed to a queue; precise ones get a decision. A letter like this can also become evidence later (in a regulator complaint, an ombudsman case or small claims), so it must be accurate, unexaggerated and free of threats the writer cannot or should not carry out.\n\nRecipient: {{recipient}}\nTone: {{tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFacts:\n\n<facts>\n{{facts}}\n</facts>\n\nRemedy wanted:\n\n<remedy>\n{{remedy}}\n</remedy>\n\n1. Build a dated timeline from the facts. If dates or amounts are missing or inconsistent, use [BRACKETS] and list them under \"Before you send\".\n2. Write the letter:\n   - Sender and recipient address blocks and the letter date, as [BRACKETS] where not given.\n   - Subject line with the reference number and a short description (\"Complaint: order [123], faulty washing machine, request for refund\").\n   - Opening: who you are in relation to the recipient and what the letter is about, in two sentences.\n   - Facts: short numbered paragraphs in date order, factual and specific.\n   - Evidence: the documents you hold, listed and referred to as enclosed.\n   - Basis: why the remedy is due, by reference to what was promised, the contract or terms, or the fact that the item or service was not as agreed. Refer to legal rights only in general terms (\"my rights as a consumer\") unless the person cites a specific law.\n   - Remedy: exactly what you want and by when, with amount and how to pay or perform it.\n   - Deadline: 14 days for a first complaint, 7 to 14 days for a final demand, unless the facts suggest otherwise, as a calendar date where possible.\n   - Next step: for a first complaint, escalation in general terms (a formal complaint process, the relevant ombudsman or regulator); for a final demand, that the sender may start a claim without further notice.\n3. Write a short pre-send checklist and an escalation plan if there is no satisfactory reply. If the person paid by card, direct debit or a payment service, include asking their card issuer, bank or the payment service about a chargeback or payment dispute (and, for ongoing charges after cancellation, stopping the payment), noting that these routes have their own time limits to check.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Use only the facts given. Do not invent dates, conversations, laws, statute names, regulator names or amounts; use [BRACKETS] where something is missing.\n- No insults, sarcasm, threats of public shaming, threats of criminal reports to extract payment, or claims for amounts not supported by the facts. These can weaken the person's position or create legal risk for them.\n- Keep the letter to one page where possible.\n- Do not predict the outcome of a claim. If the amount is large, the matter involves employment, housing, personal injury or discrimination, or a limitation deadline may be close, recommend getting legal advice (a lawyer, legal aid, or a consumer or tenant advice service) before sending a final demand.\n- Advise sending by a method that proves delivery and keeping a copy.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Letter\nThe complete letter, ready to adapt, with [BRACKETS] for anything missing.\n\n## Before you send\nChecklist: missing details, enclosures, delivery method, copy kept, deadline date on the calendar.\n\n## If they do not respond\nThree to five bullets: escalation steps in general terms and what to check locally.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-small-claims-case",
          "explain-legal-letter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:3cd89bbd6543eb1001c5b09d78fa75740add3ffa5c25ed67ec337c94736dfba7"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-component-spec",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a design-system component spec",
      "description": "Writes a design-system component spec covering anatomy, variants, states, behaviour, tokens, content rules, dos and don'ts, and accessibility. Use when adding or documenting a component.",
      "category": "design-systems",
      "tags": [
        "component-spec",
        "design-documentation",
        "usage-guidelines",
        "aria-patterns"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "component",
          "description": "The component to specify, e.g. \"toast\", \"date picker\", \"segmented control\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "The design system and product it belongs to, existing token names, related components, platforms, and any existing implementation or screenshots. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nComponent docs often show the happy-path picture and a props table, and leave out what matters for consistent use: when not to use it, what every state looks like, how it behaves with a keyboard and a screen reader, which tokens it reads, and what to do with long or translated text. Teams then rebuild the same component three ways. A good spec is the single source both designers and engineers build from.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the spec for the **{{component}}** component.\n{{#context}}\n\n<context_from_user>\n{{context}}\n</context_from_user>\n{{/context}}\n\n1. **Overview:** what it is for in one sentence, when to use it, and when not to (name the component to use instead).\n2. **Anatomy:** numbered parts (container, label, icon, and so on), each marked required or optional.\n3. **Variants and sizes:** each variant with the job it does. Keep the set minimal; if two variants differ only cosmetically, propose merging them. Give sizes with the minimum target size each must keep.\n4. **States:** default, hover, focus-visible, active or pressed, disabled, and those that apply (selected, loading, error, read-only, expanded). Say what changes visually and what the disabled state communicates, and whether a disabled control should instead stay enabled and explain why it cannot act.\n5. **Behaviour:** interactions with mouse, touch and keyboard, timing (for example auto-dismiss and how pausing works), overflow and truncation, responsive behaviour, and motion with a reduced-motion alternative.\n6. **Content:** label rules, length limits, casing, icon use, and how it handles long, empty and translated text (allow about 30 to 40 percent expansion).\n7. **Tokens:** a table of the semantic tokens each part uses. Use the system's token names if given; otherwise propose names following `{category}.{property}.{variant}.{state}` and mark them \"proposed\". Never hard-code raw values.\n8. **Accessibility:** the native element or WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices pattern it should follow, role and accessible name, keyboard map, focus management, announcements for dynamic changes, contrast and target-size requirements, and what must not rely on colour alone.\n9. **Dos and don'ts:** 4 to 8 pairs, each a concrete situation, not a general principle.\n10. **Related components** and how to choose between them.\n11. If the component name is ambiguous (a \"card\" can be a container or an interactive tile), state the interpretation you chose, or ask if the difference would change most of the spec.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Prefer native platform elements over custom ARIA. If ARIA is needed, specify it completely.\n- Do not invent the system's existing tokens, components or values. Mark anything you propose as \"proposed\".\n- Keep it implementation-neutral: describe behaviour and properties, not one framework's code.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with the sections from the contract, in order. Use tables for anatomy, variants, states, tokens and the keyboard map. End with Open questions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "define-design-tokens",
          "audit-design-consistency",
          "create-wireframe-spec"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "product-designer",
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a01f16389e1db829ff3f541edc3d1b2c135e3482c0ff1652ea5fb588b9461a39"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-contract-tests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write consumer-driven contract tests",
      "description": "Writes consumer-driven contract tests between two services and the CI gate that runs them, so a breaking API change fails before deploy. Use when services that call each other ship independently.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "contract-testing",
        "pact",
        "microservices",
        "api-compatibility"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "consumer",
          "description": "The consumer's client code that calls the provider, or a description of the calls it makes and the fields it reads.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "provider_api",
          "description": "The provider's API description (OpenAPI or other schema, handler code, or example requests and responses).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Contract approach. pact = Pact consumer tests plus provider verification; openapi-schema = validate both sides against the provider's OpenAPI document; custom = shared contract fixtures checked by both test suites.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pact",
            "openapi-schema",
            "custom"
          ],
          "default": "pact"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nContract tests catch the integration bug where each service passes its own tests but the provider renames a field, tightens validation or changes a status code that a consumer depends on. In consumer-driven contracts the consumer records only what it actually sends and reads, so the provider is free to change everything else. Contracts that copy whole responses with exact values are brittle and block harmless changes; contracts that are never verified against the real provider, or never gate a deploy, catch nothing.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite contract tests using the {{tool}} approach.\n\nConsumer:\n{{consumer}}\n\nProvider API:\n{{provider_api}}\n\n1. List each interaction the consumer really uses: method, path, query, headers that matter, request body fields, the response status and only the response fields the consumer reads. Trace field reads in the consumer code; do not include fields it ignores. Include the error responses the consumer handles (404, 409, 422 and so on).\n2. For each interaction, name the provider state it needs (\"order 42 exists and is paid\").\n3. Consumer side: write tests that exercise the real client code against the contract mock and check the client's own parsing, not just the mock. Use type and format matchers (like-type, regex, each-like with a minimum) instead of literal values, except where the exact value is the contract (enums, status codes).\n4. Provider side: write the verification that replays the contract against the running provider, with a state handler per provider state that sets up data through the provider's own code or test fixtures.\n5. CI: show how the contract is published (with consumer version and branch), how the provider verifies on every build, and the pre-deploy check that blocks a deploy when the deployed counterpart's contract is not verified (for Pact, a broker or PactFlow with `can-i-deploy --to-environment`, `record-deployment` after each deploy so the broker knows what runs where, and a contract-changed webhook that triggers provider verification). For openapi-schema, validate consumer mocks against the spec and provider responses against the spec, and fail on spec drift. For custom, store fixtures in one place both builds read, and version them.\n6. Show one concrete breaking change (a renamed field, say) and which check fails.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent endpoints, fields or status codes that are not in the inputs. If the provider API and the consumer disagree, report the mismatch as a finding instead of choosing one.\n- Contracts are not functional tests: do not assert business rules of the provider beyond the shape and semantics the consumer relies on.\n- Use the client library and test runner the consumer already uses. Name any package to install with its ecosystem.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Interactions\nTable: Interaction | Request | Response fields used | Provider state.\n## Consumer tests\nComplete test file(s) in code blocks.\n## Provider verification\nComplete verification test with state handlers.\n## CI gate\nThe pipeline steps (as config or a numbered list) for publish, verify and the pre-deploy check, and the breaking-change example.\n## What this does not catch\nBullets: behaviour contract tests miss here (performance, auth flows, data semantics) and what covers it instead. Mismatches found between consumer and provider go first, if any.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "backend-engineer",
          "test-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:8ff31a124dc7521fe6c7e0c409077f85ab348934711c49a28865535d18fdf473"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-cover-letter",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a cover letter",
      "description": "Writes a tailored cover letter under 350 words that connects two or three specific achievements to the role's most important needs. Use for any job application that asks for one.",
      "category": "job-search",
      "tags": [
        "job-application",
        "tailoring",
        "achievements"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "job_posting",
          "description": "The full job posting text, plus anything you know about the team or company that the letter could reference.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "resume",
          "description": "Your resume, or notes on the achievements you want to draw from.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Register of the letter. formal suits law, finance and public sector; warm suits mission-driven and small teams; direct suits most tech and business roles.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "formal",
            "warm",
            "direct"
          ],
          "default": "direct"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a hiring manager who has read thousands of cover letters and remembers almost none of them. The forgettable ones restate the resume, open with \"I am writing to apply for\", and claim traits (\"passionate\", \"detail-oriented\") without proof. The ones that get a candidate an interview answer one question fast: \"Can this person solve the problem we are hiring for?\" They do it with two or three specific achievements chosen for this role, in the candidate's own voice.\n\n<job_posting>\n{{job_posting}}\n</job_posting>\n\n<resume>\n{{resume}}\n</resume>\n\nTone: {{tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Identify the two or three needs that matter most to this hiring manager: the problems in the responsibilities, not the generic requirements.\n2. For each need, pick the single strongest piece of evidence from the resume: an achievement with action, scope and result. Prefer evidence that is close to the role's context (same kind of user, scale, industry or problem).\n3. Write the letter:\n   - Opening (2-3 sentences): name the role, then lead with the strongest match or a specific, true reason for wanting this role at this company. No \"I am writing to apply\".\n   - Body (one short paragraph per need, or a tight paragraph plus 2-3 bullets): need, evidence, result, and what it means for them.\n   - If there is an obvious question (career change, gap, relocation, overqualification), answer it in one confident sentence. Do not apologise.\n   - Close (1-2 sentences): what you would bring in the first months and a plain call to talk.\n4. Match the tone: formal (complete sentences, no contractions, restrained), warm (personal motivation, connection to the mission), direct (short sentences, lead with results).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Under 350 words for the letter body. Shorter is better if it is complete.\n- Use only facts in the resume. Never invent metrics, employers, tools or motivations. If a strong letter needs a fact you do not have (the hiring manager's name, why this company, a number), write a [placeholder] and list it under \"Check before sending\".\n- Do not repeat the resume line by line; select and connect.\n- Mirror two or three of the posting's key terms naturally, without keyword stuffing.\n- No clichés: \"passionate\", \"team player\", \"hit the ground running\", \"perfect fit\", \"I believe I would be a great asset\".\n- Address \"Dear Hiring Manager\" unless a name is given; when none is, add \"find the hiring manager's name\" to Check before sending rather than a name placeholder in the salutation. Sign off with [Your name].\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Letter\nThe full letter, ready to paste.\n## Why these choices\nThree bullets at most: which needs you targeted and which evidence you used for each.\n## Check before sending\nBullets: every [placeholder] to fill and any claim the candidate should verify.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "analyze-job-posting",
          "tailor-resume-to-job"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "job-application-track"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:152c32604893c2b2831e65a09127b1ca1bd1aa34e8e3c410d9f5b1e3c1fce962"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-creator-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a creator brief",
      "description": "Writes a brief for UGC creators or influencers covering deliverables, key messages, creative direction, dos and don'ts, ad disclosure and usage rights. Use before contacting creators.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "influencer-marketing",
        "ugc",
        "creator-brief",
        "ad-disclosure",
        "usage-rights"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "campaign",
          "description": "Campaign goal, audience, timing, budget or compensation model, number of creators, and any must-use offer, code or link.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product is, who it is for, the key benefits, and the claims you can substantiate.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platforms",
          "description": "Platforms and placements (for example \"TikTok and Instagram Reels, plus paid usage\"). Defaults to the platforms in the campaign description.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an influencer and UGC campaign manager. Good creator briefs are short enough to read on a phone, firm on the few things that must be true (deliverables, claims, disclosure, rights, dates) and loose on everything creative, because creators know their audience better than the brand does. Over-scripted briefs produce stiff videos that underperform; vague briefs produce content you cannot use. Most disputes come from usage rights, exclusivity and revision rounds that were never written down.\n\n{{#platforms}}Platforms: {{platforms}}{{/platforms}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nCampaign:\n\n<campaign>\n{{campaign}}\n</campaign>\n\nProduct:\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n1. Write a one-paragraph campaign overview: the goal, who the audience is, and what a creator's content should make a viewer think, feel or do.\n2. Summarise the product in plain words with the facts a creator can say on camera. Only include claims the product description supports.\n3. Give at most three key messages, written as ideas to express in the creator's own words, not lines to read.\n4. Specify deliverables in a table: platform, format, length, quantity, aspect ratio, captions or on-screen text, raw footage or not, draft due and go-live date. Use [TBD] for anything not given. Tell creators to check the current platform specs rather than stating limits that change.\n5. Give creative direction: three to five hook ideas for the first seconds, how to show the product in real use, and two example angles. No full scripts.\n6. List dos and don'ts, including no claims beyond the approved list, no disparaging competitors, no medical, financial or results claims unless substantiated, and brand-safety limits.\n7. Write the disclosure requirements: use the platform's paid-partnership label, and a clear \"ad\" or \"sponsored\" disclosure at the start of the caption and, for video, said or shown in the video itself; hashtags buried in a block do not count. Note that rules vary by market (for example FTC guidance in the US, ASA and CAP Code in the UK) and the brand should confirm the requirements for each market.\n8. Set out usage rights and terms as fields to confirm: organic reposting by the brand, paid usage (whitelisting or partnership ads), duration, territories, channels, exclusivity window and category, raw footage ownership, number of revision rounds, payment amount and schedule, and what happens if a post is removed early.\n9. Give the timeline and approval process, then list open questions for the brand.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The brief fits on about two phone screens per section; use bullets and tables.\n- Never invent compensation, dates, discount codes, links or claims; use [TBD] or [CONFIRM].\n- Rights and terms are a checklist for the brand and creator to agree in a contract, not legal advice; say so in one line.\n- Speak to creators as professionals: direct, warm, no corporate jargon.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown with these H2 sections in order: Campaign overview, The product, Key messages, Deliverables, Creative direction, Dos and don'ts, Disclosure, Usage rights and terms, Timeline and approvals, Open questions.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "growth-marketer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:20bb7fce56c6b208f83ff88e378e9ed84ca4790e42804a248d788f372554a9cc"
