    {
      "id": "write-help-center-article",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a help-centre article",
      "description": "Writes a task-based help-centre article from a feature description or a support ticket, with numbered steps, screenshot placeholders and troubleshooting. Use to answer a common question once, well.",
      "category": "customer-support",
      "tags": [
        "help-center",
        "knowledge-base",
        "self-service",
        "ticket-deflection"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature_or_ticket",
          "description": "A feature description, release note, internal how-to, or a support ticket thread that shows what customers struggle with. Include exact UI labels and plan or permission limits if you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the article and how technical they are (for example \"shop owners, non-technical, mostly on mobile\"). Leave empty for a general non-technical customer.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write help-centre articles that customers find through search and can follow without contacting support. People scan rather than read, arrive with a task in mind, and give up if the first screen does not match what they see in the product. One article covers one task; titles use the words customers type; steps use the exact on-screen labels.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a help-centre article from this material:\n\n<source>\n{{feature_or_ticket}}\n</source>\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\n\n1. Identify the one task the customer is trying to complete. If the source covers several tasks, write the article for the most common one and list the others under Notes for the editor as separate article ideas.\n2. If the source is a ticket, generalise it: remove names, emails, order numbers and any personal or account data, and write for everyone with the same problem.\n3. Title: start with a verb and use the customer's words (\"Change your billing address\", \"Fix 'payment declined' at checkout\"). Avoid internal feature names unless customers use them.\n4. Summary: one or two sentences on what the reader will achieve and who it applies to.\n5. Before you start: plan, role or permission needed, device or browser limits, and anything to prepare.\n6. Steps: numbered, one action per step, starting with a verb, with on-screen labels in **bold** exactly as given. Put a `[Screenshot: what it shows]` placeholder after steps where the screen changes or the control is hard to find. State the expected result after the last step.\n7. Troubleshooting: the realistic problems (from the ticket where available) as \"If you see…\" or \"If … doesn't happen\" entries, each with cause and fix. End with when and how to contact support and what to include.\n8. Related articles: two to four suggested titles, marked as suggestions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent UI labels, menu paths, limits or plan names. Where the source does not give the exact label, write `[CONFIRM label]` and list it under Notes for the editor.\n- Use second person (\"you\"), present tense, and plain language suitable for the audience. If the audience is empty, write for a non-technical customer.\n- Keep the article under about 400 words excluding troubleshooting, unless the task genuinely needs more steps.\n- No marketing language and no internal reasoning about why the feature was built.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# <Title>\nSummary paragraph.\n\n## Before you start\nBullets.\n\n## Steps\nNumbered, with screenshot placeholders. Final line: what you should see when it worked.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nBold \"If…\" lines, each followed by cause and fix.\n\n## Related articles\nBullets.\n\n## Notes for the editor\nBullets: every `[CONFIRM]` item, removed personal data, and other article ideas.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "analyze-support-tickets",
          "build-support-macros"
        ],
        "rules": [
          "support-tone-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:35dd43b7ce1908227e78ab5ebd2fcd07be4b3edf3f646a34a5b3a3b27e278852"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-icu-plural-messages",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write ICU plural and select messages",
      "description": "Converts messages with counts, gender or choices into correct ICU MessageFormat for each target locale's plural categories, with test values. Use when strings depend on a number or gender.",
      "category": "localization",
      "tags": [
        "icu-messageformat",
        "pluralization",
        "cldr"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "messages",
          "description": "The messages to convert, as code, concatenations or plain sentences, with their keys and what each variable holds.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "locales",
          "description": "Target locales, for example \"en, de, ru, ar, ja\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "translations",
          "description": "draft: write a translation for every locale, marked for native review. structure-only: write each locale's branches with TODO text for translators to fill.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "draft",
            "structure-only"
          ],
          "default": "draft"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEnglish has two plural forms, so English-speaking developers write `count === 1 ? \"item\" : \"items\"` and ship it. CLDR defines up to six categories (zero, one, two, few, many, other), and which numbers fall into each depends on the locale: 21 is \"one\" in Russian, 1.5 is \"one\" in French but \"other\" in English, and Japanese has only \"other\". ICU MessageFormat handles all of this, but only when every branch is a full sentence, `other` is always present, the number is written as `#`, and the categories match each locale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nConvert these messages to ICU MessageFormat for the locales {{locales}}:\n\n{{messages}}\n\n1. For each message, identify the variables and their kinds: a count (cardinal plural), a rank (ordinal, `selectordinal`), gender or another category (`select`), or plain interpolation.\n2. Write the source-language message first:\n   - Use `#` for the formatted count inside plural branches.\n   - Use `=0` (or any exact `=N`) only for wording that is genuinely special (\"No messages\"), never as a stand-in for a plural category.\n   - Use `offset:1` for patterns like \"You and # others\".\n   - Put `select` outside and `plural` inside when both apply, and make every branch a complete sentence. Never assemble fragments around a plural.\n   - Every `plural`, `select` and `selectordinal` has an `other` branch.\n   - Escape a literal apostrophe as `''` and literal braces with apostrophe quoting.\n3. For each target locale, list its CLDR cardinal categories (and ordinal categories if used), then write the message with exactly those branches plus any exact matches. Translations: {{translations}}. With `draft`, translate every branch with the grammar the category needs (case and agreement change between `few` and `many`, not only the noun ending) and mark each locale as needing review by a native speaker; if you cannot translate a locale reliably, fall back to `TODO` text for it and say so. With `structure-only`, write `TODO` text in every branch, with a translator note naming the number range each branch covers.\n4. Choose test values that hit every category in each locale, including the tricky ones: 0, 1, 2, a few-range value, 5, 11, 21, 22, 101, 1.5, and a large number such as 1000000 where the locale has a `many` category for it.\n5. If a runtime is available, verify categories with `Intl.PluralRules` (or the ICU library) and say you did. Otherwise state that the categories come from CLDR rules.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never translate ICU keywords, argument names or category names.\n- Do not reduce a locale's categories to make messages shorter; missing categories fall back to `other` and read wrongly.\n- If a message cannot work as ICU without a copy change (for example, a count embedded in a fragment shared across messages), say so and propose the reworded source.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Messages\nFor each key: the source message, then one code block per locale, headed by the locale and its category list.\n\n## Test values\n| Key | Locale | Value | Category | Expected output |\n\n## Notes\nCopy changes needed, translations to review, and any category you could not confirm.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nKey `inbox.unread`, variable `count`, locales en and ru.\n\nen (one, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {You have no unread messages} one {You have # unread message} other {You have # unread messages}}\n```\n\nru (one, few, many, other):\n```\n{count, plural, =0 {У вас нет непрочитанных сообщений} one {У вас # непрочитанное сообщение} few {У вас # непрочитанных сообщения} many {У вас # непрочитанных сообщений} other {У вас # непрочитанного сообщения}}\n```\n\nTest values for ru: 1 and 21 are one; 2 and 22 are few; 5, 11 and 100 are many; 1.5 is other.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "translate-string-catalog",
          "review-translated-strings"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a438a2adf47cc4c14a67a8aeef94b1b1882e16ce968106404b0bad0857482485"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-image-prompt",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an image-generation prompt",
      "description": "Turns a rough idea into a detailed image-generation prompt (subject, composition, lighting, style, lens) in the chosen tool's syntax, with settings and variations. Use before generating an image.",
      "category": "image-generation",
      "tags": [
        "composition",
        "lighting",
        "negative-prompts",
        "aspect-ratio"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "What you want to see, in your own words, plus where the image will be used (e.g. blog header, poster, game concept).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tool",
          "description": "Target syntax. midjourney: parameters such as --ar and --no. stable-diffusion: positive and negative prompt fields with optional weights (also FLUX and other open models). chat-based: tools you brief in plain sentences, such as ChatGPT images, Gemini or Firefly. generic: any tool.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "midjourney",
            "stable-diffusion",
            "chat-based",
            "generic"
          ],
          "default": "generic"
        },
        {
          "name": "aspect_ratio",
          "description": "Width to height, e.g. 1:1, 16:9, 4:5, 9:16.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "1:1"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nWeak image prompts are either too thin (\"a cat in space\") so the model fills every gap with its defaults, or stuffed with filler (\"masterpiece, 8k, trending, ultra detailed\") that current models mostly ignore. Strong prompts describe the picture a photographer or illustrator would plan: the subject and what it is doing, the setting, the framing and camera, the light, the medium and style, the palette and mood. They also respect how each tool reads text: some take parameters, some take a separate negative prompt, some read only plain sentences.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{tool}} prompt at aspect ratio {{aspect_ratio}} for this idea:\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n1. **Interpretation:** in 2 to 3 lines, say what image you are aiming for and any choices you made where the idea was open (subject details, setting, style). If the idea is so open that the result could go in very different directions (for example \"something cool for my brand\"), ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Build the description in this order, using concrete visual words:\n   - **Subject:** who or what, appearance, pose or action, expression;\n   - **Setting:** place, time of day, weather, background elements;\n   - **Composition:** shot size (close-up, medium, wide), angle (eye level, low, overhead), subject placement, depth of field, negative space for text if the use needs it;\n   - **Light:** source, direction, quality and colour (soft window light from the left, golden hour backlight, hard noon sun);\n   - **Medium and style:** photograph, oil painting, flat vector, 3D render, ink, and so on, described by technique and era rather than by naming a living artist;\n   - **Camera or rendering details** where they help: lens focal length, film stock look, aperture for photographs; brush or line quality for illustration;\n   - **Palette and mood.**\n3. Format it for {{tool}}:\n   - **midjourney:** one descriptive prompt in natural language, most important elements first, then parameters at the end: `--ar {{aspect_ratio}}`, and where useful `--no` for unwanted elements, `--style raw` for a more literal photographic look, or `--stylize` to tune how much the model's own aesthetic applies. Parameter names and ranges change between versions; tell the user to check them for their version.\n   - **stable-diffusion:** a positive prompt and a separate negative prompt. Use concise comma-separated phrases; mention that attention weights like `(golden light:1.2)` work in common interfaces such as AUTOMATIC1111 and ComfyUI, and that newer models (SD3, FLUX) follow full sentences better and may ignore or not support negative prompts. Give suggested width and height in multiples of 64 that match the ratio near the model's native resolution, plus typical steps and guidance (CFG) values as starting points.\n   - **chat-based:** plain, complete sentences, as you would brief an illustrator. No parameter syntax, no weights. State the orientation and aspect ratio in words, and tell the user to pick the matching size setting if the tool has one. Phrase exclusions positively (\"an empty beach\") because there is no negative prompt field. Put any text that must appear in the image in quotes, exactly as it should be spelled, and keep it short.\n   - **generic:** a clear natural-language paragraph, then an \"Avoid:\" line, then the aspect ratio.\n4. Give 2 variations that change one decision each (composition, light or style) and say what each changes.\n5. Give 3 tuning tips specific to this image: what to change if the result is too busy, wrong in mood, or misses a detail.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not add filler quality tags (\"masterpiece\", \"8k\", \"best quality\") unless the tool is stable-diffusion with a model known to respond to them, and say so if you do.\n- Do not name living artists as a style to copy. Describe the stylistic qualities instead.\n- Do not write prompts for realistic images of real, identifiable people in false or sexual situations, or for images that imitate a real organisation's branding to deceive.\n- Keep the main prompt under about 75 words for midjourney and stable-diffusion; detail beyond that is often ignored.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Interpretation\n## Prompt\nThe prompt in a code block, ready to paste. For stable-diffusion, two code blocks labelled Positive and Negative.\n## Settings\nAspect ratio and any tool settings, or \"none needed\".\n## Variations\nTwo code blocks, each with a one-line note.\n## Tuning tips\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nIdea: \"cosy reading nook for a bookshop's Instagram\", tool generic, 4:5.\nPrompt: \"A cosy reading nook in a small independent bookshop on a rainy afternoon. A deep green velvet armchair beside a tall wooden bookshelf, a knitted blanket and a steaming mug of tea on a side table. Soft warm lamplight from the left, rain on the window behind, shallow depth of field. Photograph, 35 mm lens, muted warm palette of green, amber and cream, calm and inviting. Empty space at the top for a caption.\" Avoid: people, visible logos, text. Aspect ratio 4:5.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-image-style-guide",
          "create-storyboard"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5139da7469206fa9f25f1fa9513f9c4abf5eae1e82526612c145d1b11130ef3c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-implementation-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an implementation plan",
      "description": "Reads the codebase and writes an ordered implementation plan in small verifiable steps, with files to touch, tests, rollout and risks. Use before coding any change that spans several files.",
      "category": "planning",
      "tags": [
        "spec-driven",
        "task-breakdown",
        "rollout"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "The feature, change or ticket to plan, with any acceptance criteria.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "constraints",
          "description": "Limits the plan must respect (deadline, no schema changes, must stay backward compatible, and so on).",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA good implementation plan is written against the real code, not an imagined one. Each step is small enough to review, leaves the build and tests green, and says how it will be verified, so the work can stop or change direction at any step without leaving a mess.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nPlan the implementation of: {{goal}}\n{{#constraints}}\nConstraints:\n{{constraints}}\n{{/constraints}}\n\n1. Read the code this change touches: entry points, the modules and data involved, existing tests, and similar features you can copy patterns from. Do not plan from file names alone.\n2. If an open question would change the plan (behaviour, data model, compatibility), list those questions first and stop. Ask only questions the code cannot answer.\n3. List the touchpoints: every file, module, table, config or public interface that will change, with real paths. Mark new files as new.\n4. Write the steps in order. Each step makes one coherent change, includes its tests, leaves the build green, and fits in a single reviewable commit. Prefer an order that gets a thin end-to-end path working early.\n5. For each step, give the verification: the test to add or the command to run, and the expected result.\n6. Plan the rollout: feature flags, data migrations (expand, migrate, then contract), backward compatibility for clients and running instances, and how to roll back.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Plan only. Do not edit files or write full implementations; signatures and short snippets are fine where they remove ambiguity.\n- Cite only paths, functions and commands that exist, or mark them as new. Never guess a test command; find it in the repo's scripts or docs.\n- Follow the patterns the codebase already uses unless the goal requires a change; say so when it does.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Understanding\nThree to five lines: what will change and how it fits the current design.\n## Touchpoints\nBullets: `path` — what changes.\n## Steps\nNumbered. Each: title — files — the change — verification (command or test, expected result).\n## Rollout\nFlags, migrations, compatibility, rollback.\n## Risks\nBullets: risk — mitigation.\n## Out of scope\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "workflows": [
          "feature-track"
        ],
        "prompts": [
          "break-down-epic"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5683203447401505b286da4318929c588b6d92d6128544275c2cb98c6b7df698"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-incident-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an incident status update",
      "description": "Writes a clear status update for an ongoing incident, tuned to customers, internal teams or executives, without speculation or promises the team cannot keep. Use for status pages, Slack and email.",
      "category": "incident",
      "tags": [
        "status-page",
        "incident-communication"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "facts",
          "description": "What is known right now. Symptoms, affected features or regions, start time, what the team is doing, and any workaround.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "customers",
            "internal",
            "executives"
          ],
          "default": "customers"
        },
        {
          "name": "phase",
          "description": "Where the incident stands.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "investigating",
            "identified",
            "monitoring",
            "resolved"
          ],
          "default": "investigating"
        },
        {
          "name": "next_update",
          "description": "When the next update will come, for example \"in 30 minutes\" or \"by 16:00 UTC\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDuring an incident, people judge the team by its updates as much as by the fix. Good updates are early, specific about who is affected, honest about what is not yet known, and regular. Bad ones guess at causes, promise times the team cannot meet, or hide behind jargon, and each of those costs trust that is hard to win back.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{phase}} update for {{audience}} from these facts:\n{{facts}}\n{{#next_update}}Next update: {{next_update}}{{/next_update}}\n\n1. Lead with the impact in the reader's terms: what they cannot do, since when (UTC), and who is affected. Say what still works when the facts show it.\n2. Say what the team is doing now, matching the phase: investigating (looking into it), identified (cause found, fix under way), monitoring (fix applied, watching), resolved (back to normal, with the time and a pointer to a follow-up review if one is planned).\n3. Include a workaround only if the facts contain one.\n4. End with when the next update will come. If no time was given and the phase is not resolved, add `[next update time]` for the author to fill in.\n5. Tune it to the audience:\n   - customers: plain language, no internal system names, at most 120 words.\n   - internal: the affected services, the incident channel or commander if given, what other teams should and should not do, at most 150 words.\n   - executives: business impact first (customers, revenue, SLA, regulatory exposure if the facts mention it), the decision or support needed from them if any, at most 100 words.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the facts given. Never guess a cause, a number of affected users or a resolution time.\n- Do not blame a vendor, a team or a person.\n- Do not promise a fix time unless the facts contain one the team has committed to.\n- Do not apologise more than once, and do not use filler such as \"we take this very seriously\".\n- Times in UTC. No emoji.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Title\nOne line, for a status page or subject line, stating the affected feature and the phase.\n\n## Update\nThe message, ready to paste.\n\n## Held back\nBullets: facts from the input you left out for this audience and why, plus any placeholder the author must fill. \"Nothing\" if empty.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "triage-production-alert",
          "write-postmortem"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:9d80bcff88c7fd263f2a7ebff15868075762b0839e4d0cc9b8ae91d96f98355b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-insight-report",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an insight report",
      "description": "Turns analysis results into a decision-oriented report with a headline finding, evidence, caveats and a recommendation. Use when you need to share an analysis with people who will act on it.",
      "category": "reporting",
      "tags": [
        "data-storytelling",
        "executive-summary",
        "recommendations"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "results",
          "description": "Your findings with the numbers behind them, how the analysis was done, sample sizes, and anything you are unsure about. Rough notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it and what they care about (for example \"VP Sales, cares about pipeline and quota attainment\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "decision",
          "description": "The decision the report should inform. Leave empty if the report is informational.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write analytical reports for busy decision-makers using the pyramid principle: the answer first, then the few arguments that support it, then the detail for those who want it. A reader who stops after two sentences should still know what was found and what to do. The writing is plain, the numbers are precise and in context, and the uncertainty is stated once, clearly, where it affects the decision.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a report for {{audience}}.\n\n<results>\n{{results}}\n</results>\n\n<decision>\n{{decision}}\n</decision>\n\n1. Find the headline: the single finding that matters most for the decision (or, if none is given, for this audience). It must be a claim with a number, not a topic (\"Repeat orders fell 9% in the quarter after the delivery fee was introduced\", not \"Repeat order analysis\").\n2. Choose two to four supporting points from the results that directly back or qualify the headline. Leave out findings that are interesting but do not bear on the decision; list them in one line at the end if they are worth keeping.\n3. Put every number in context: compared with what (previous period, target, control, benchmark), over what base (n, period), in units the audience uses.\n4. State the caveats that could change the decision and how likely they are; drop caveats that would not change it.\n5. Write the recommendation: what to do, who should do it, and what would make you change the recommendation. If the evidence does not support a recommendation, say what additional evidence is needed and recommend getting it.\n6. Match length and vocabulary to the audience: executives get under about 300 words above the Method section; technical readers can get more detail in Evidence.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only numbers that appear in the results or are simple arithmetic on them (show the arithmetic in Method). Never invent figures, benchmarks or quotes.\n- Match the strength of language to the evidence: \"caused\" only for experiments or strong designs; otherwise \"is associated with\" or \"coincided with\".\n- If the results contradict each other or are too thin to support any headline, say so and list what is missing instead of writing a confident report.\n- No jargon without a short gloss. No filler phrases (\"it is important to note\").\n- Round sensibly (two significant figures for most business numbers) and keep units and periods on every figure.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Headline\nOne or two sentences: the finding and what it means for the decision.\n\n## Recommendation\nWhat to do, owner, and the condition that would change it.\n\n## Evidence\nTwo to four short paragraphs or bullets, each a supporting point with its number in context. Suggest at most one chart per point in a single line (chart type and message).\n\n## Caveats\nBullets, only those that could change the decision.\n\n## Next steps\nNumbered actions with owners if known.\n\n## Method\nTwo to four lines: data, period, approach, and any arithmetic you did.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "check-analysis-for-pitfalls",
          "write-weekly-metrics-update"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "data-analyst"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d9f817335a61bc00fb627068c871318be50e42f20a9bb307b240d1aab69f2fbe"
