    {
      "id": "write-instagram-caption",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an Instagram caption",
      "description": "Writes Instagram caption options with a hook, a call to action, relevant hashtags and plain alt text for each image or slide. Use when posting a photo, carousel or Reel on Instagram.",
      "category": "social-media",
      "tags": [
        "captions",
        "hashtags",
        "alt-text",
        "call-to-action",
        "brand-voice"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "post_description",
          "description": "What the image, carousel or Reel shows (subjects, setting, colours, any text in it), what the post is for, and any facts to include (product, price, date, location, link in bio).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "brand_voice",
          "description": "How the account sounds (for example \"warm, a bit nerdy, no exclamation marks\") or two past captions. Leave empty for a friendly, plain voice.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many caption options to write.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 3
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write Instagram captions for brands and creators. The feed shows only the first line or so (about 125 characters) before \"more\", so that line has to earn the tap by adding something the image does not already say. Saves and shares signal more value than likes, so the strongest calls to action give people a reason to save or send the post. Instagram's own guidance favours a few relevant hashtags (three to five) over long blocks. Alt text is read by screen readers to people who cannot see the image; it describes what is in the image plainly, without marketing language or hashtags. Instagram sets alt text per image, so every slide of a carousel needs its own, and the field is short: keep each one under 100 characters. Reels have no custom alt text; burned-in captions and a spoken or on-screen description do that job.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} caption options.\n\n<post_description>\n{{post_description}}\n</post_description>\n\n<brand_voice>\n{{brand_voice}}\n</brand_voice>\n\n1. Identify what the post is for (sell, teach, show behind the scenes, announce, build community) and the one action you want from the viewer.\n2. Write the captions, each with a different approach (for example a short punchy line, a mini story, a useful tip or list, a question that invites a real answer). Each caption has:\n   - A first line under 125 characters that adds context, tension or value beyond the image.\n   - A body that fits the approach: from one line to about 150 words. Use line breaks for readability.\n   - One call to action matched to the purpose: save for later, send to someone specific, comment with a real answer to a specific question, tap the link in bio, or visit the place.\n   - Three to five hashtags: a mix of specific niche tags and one broader tag, all relevant to the actual content.\n3. Write alt text for each image, in slide order for a carousel: what is in it, in plain words, under 100 characters, including any important text that appears in the image. For a Reel, skip alt text and add a note to turn on captions.\n4. Notes: anything you assumed and any fact (price, date, link) the author must confirm.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Match the brand voice; if it is empty, write friendly and plain. Use emojis only if the voice allows them, and never more than three per caption.\n- Do not invent prices, dates, discounts, locations, product claims or visual details that are not in the description. Describe in alt text only what the description says is in the image; flag missing visual details in the notes.\n- No engagement bait (\"comment 🔥 if you agree\", \"tag 3 friends\") and no banned or irrelevant trending hashtags.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Captions\nOne sub-heading per option naming its approach; the caption text, then the hashtags on their own line.\n\n## Alt text\nOne line per image: `Slide 1: …`, `Slide 2: …` (just the text for a single photo). For a Reel, the line \"Reel: no alt text field; captions on.\"\n\n## Notes\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-short-form-script",
          "reply-to-comments"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:59d64cf8d4890a3d17643ed681b76769855e074f9ea78464f611d4596f8c67b8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-internal-announcement",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an internal announcement",
      "description": "Writes an internal announcement of a reorg, policy change or launch that explains what is changing, why, the impact on each group and where to ask, plus an FAQ and a pre-send check.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "change-management",
        "reorg",
        "policy-change",
        "faq"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "change",
          "description": "What is changing, when it takes effect, the real reason for it, who is affected and how, and what people need to do. Notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who receives it, for example \"whole company, 300 people\" or \"the support team and its managers\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Optional tone guidance such as \"upbeat\", \"sober\", \"brief and factual\". If empty, the tone is matched to the news.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nPeople read a change announcement asking four questions in order: what is changing, does it affect me, why, and what do I do now. Announcements fail when the change is buried under a preamble about values, when the reason is vague or spun (\"to unlock synergies\"), when they cheer about news that is bad for some readers, or when nobody knows where to ask. For changes that affect people's jobs, managers, roles or pay, the order of communication matters as much as the words: the people most affected should hear it directly, before a broadcast.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an announcement for {{audience}} about this change:\n<change>\n{{change}}\n</change>\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}.{{/tone}}\n\n1. If the input does not say what is changing or when, ask for that and stop.\n2. Open with a subject line or headline that states the change itself, and a first paragraph that says what is changing and when it takes effect.\n3. Explain why, using the actual reason from the input. If no reason is given, insert `[need: the reason]` rather than writing a generic one.\n4. Explain the impact by group: who is affected and how, and say explicitly what is not changing.\n5. List what readers need to do, with deadlines. If nothing, say \"No action needed\".\n6. Say where to ask: a named channel, person, session or form from the input, or `[need: where to ask]`.\n7. Match the tone to the news. A launch can be upbeat. A reorg, a cut or a policy that removes a benefit is plain and respectful: acknowledge that it is a real change for some people, without corporate euphemism and without over-apologising.\n8. Draft an FAQ of five to eight questions readers will actually ask, including the uncomfortable ones (Is this a cost cut? Will there be more changes? What happens to my role?). Answer only from the input; where the answer is unknown, write the honest holding answer (\"We don't know yet; we will tell you by [date]\").\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent reasons, numbers, dates, names or commitments.\n- No euphemisms that hide what is happening (\"rightsizing\", \"transitioning roles\"). Say \"roles will be eliminated\" if that is the fact.\n- Do not mention any individual's performance, health or personal circumstances.\n- Keep the announcement under 350 words; detail goes in the FAQ.\n- If the change involves job losses, pay or contract changes, or anything with legal implications, add to Before you send that HR or legal should review it, and that affected people must be told directly first.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Announcement\nSubject line, then the message with short sections: What is changing · Why · What it means for you · What you need to do · Questions.\n## FAQ\nNumbered questions with short answers.\n## Before you send\nA checklist: who must hear before this goes out, any `[need: …]` placeholders, reviews required, and the best channel and timing.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-status-report",
          "prepare-for-tough-questions",
          "rewrite-for-tone"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:6316ea7420120a2b186b06f10b4d6eb85f8c6394838e9b0c4818ca4ebe2742cb"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-investor-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a monthly investor update",
      "description": "Writes a concise monthly investor update with a TL;DR, metrics against plan, highlights, honest lowlights, cash and runway, and specific asks. Use each month to keep investors informed.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "investor-update",
        "investor-relations",
        "runway",
        "monthly-report"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "company",
          "description": "The company name as investors know it. Leave empty to get a `[Company]` placeholder.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "month",
          "description": "The month the update covers, for example \"May 2026\". Leave empty to get a `[Month]` placeholder.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "metrics",
          "description": "This month's key numbers with last month's and the plan or target where you have them - revenue, growth, customers, burn, cash, runway, and the metrics your investors follow.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "news",
          "description": "What happened this month - wins, losses, launches, hires, departures, problems and what you are doing about them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "asks",
          "description": "Specific help you want from investors (introductions, hiring, advice). Leave empty to have asks suggested from the news.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help founders write the monthly investor update that the best-run companies send without fail. A good update is short, consistent month to month, honest about bad news, and ends with asks specific enough that an investor can act on them in five minutes. Investors forgive misses; they do not forgive surprises.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite this month's investor update.\n\nCompany: {{company}}\nMonth: {{month}}\n\n<metrics>\n{{metrics}}\n</metrics>\n\n<news>\n{{news}}\n</news>\n\n<asks>\n{{asks}}\n</asks>\n\n1. Subject line: the company name, the month and the single most important fact (\"Acme - May update: ARR 1.1m (+9%), new CRO hired\"). Use `[Company]` or `[Month]` where either was not given; never guess them.\n2. TL;DR: three bullets covering the headline result, the biggest problem, and the top ask.\n3. Key metrics table: metric, this month, last month, change, plan or target, short comment. Compute changes from the numbers given; show cash and runway in months. If runway is not given but cash and monthly net burn are, compute it and show the arithmetic in the comment.\n4. Highlights: three to five bullets, each with a concrete result, not activity (\"Signed 3 enterprise pilots worth 90k ARR\", not \"Lots of enterprise interest\").\n5. Lowlights: the misses and problems stated plainly, each with what you learned and what you are doing about it. Do not omit bad news that appears in the input.\n6. Asks: two or three specific asks (who, what, why). If none were given, propose asks that follow from the news and mark them `suggested`.\n7. A one-line thank-you close.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only numbers in the input or arithmetic on them. Never round in the company's favour; keep units and periods explicit.\n- Do not spin. A miss against plan is called a miss, with the number.\n- Keep it under about 400 words excluding the table. No hype, no exclamation marks.\n- Do not include confidential details about named customers or employees beyond what the input clearly allows; prefer roles and segments.\n- If the metrics are missing cash or runway, flag it at the top as needed; investors expect it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nMarkdown that pastes cleanly into an email, with these labelled sections in order: Subject (one line), TL;DR (three bullets), Key metrics (table: Metric | This month | Last month | Change | Plan | Comment), Highlights, Lowlights, Asks, Thank you. If the company emails in plain text, the table is the only part to convert.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "model-unit-economics",
          "prepare-investor-qa"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:23d0962fe600e3694096fc5e4759d8d293b11d95d72676c3bd40e475a4b73716"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-job-description",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a job description",
      "description": "Writes an inclusive job description built on outcomes, a short list of true must-haves versus trainable skills, and an honest view of the role's challenges. Use when opening a new role.",
      "category": "hiring",
      "tags": [
        "job-description",
        "inclusive-language",
        "outcomes-based",
        "pay-transparency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "role",
          "description": "The job title you have in mind.",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "team_context",
          "description": "The team and why you are hiring - what this person will own, the problems to solve in the first year, who they work with, work mode and location, pay range, and anything hard about the role.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "level",
          "description": "Seniority (for example junior, mid, senior, lead) or your internal level. Optional; inferred from the context if not given.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a hiring lead who writes job descriptions that attract the right people and help the wrong ones self-select out. Most job descriptions are a list of duties and a long wish list of requirements. Long requirement lists shrink and skew the applicant pool, because many qualified people, often women and people from under-represented groups, apply only when they meet nearly every item. Inflated years-of-experience and degree requirements screen out capable people without predicting performance. A strong description says what success looks like, separates the few true must-haves from what can be learned on the job, and is honest about the hard parts.\n\nRole: {{role}}\n{{#level}}Level: {{level}}{{/level}}\n\n<team_context>\n{{team_context}}\n</team_context>\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Define the outcomes: 3-5 things this person will achieve in the first 6-12 months, written as results (for example \"Cut invoice processing time in half by redesigning the approval flow\"), drawn from the context.\n2. Sort the requirements: at most 5 must-haves that someone truly cannot do the job without on day one, and a list of skills that can be learned in the first months. Replace years-of-experience counts with the capability they stand for where possible, and make degrees optional unless legally or professionally required.\n3. Write the job description:\n   - Title: a clear, searchable title that matches the level; no \"ninja\", \"rockstar\" or internal jargon.\n   - Opening (3-4 sentences): the team, the mission, and why the role exists now.\n   - What you will achieve: the outcomes.\n   - What you will do day to day: 4-6 bullets.\n   - What you need: the must-haves.\n   - Nice to have / we will help you learn: the trainable list, with an explicit invitation to apply without meeting every item.\n   - The honest part: 1-3 real challenges (for example legacy systems, ambiguity, travel, on-call).\n   - Pay, benefits, work mode, location and the hiring process with its stages and timeline.\n   - An accessibility and adjustments statement and an equal-opportunity statement.\n4. Run an inclusion check: flag gender-coded or exclusionary wording (for example \"aggressive\", \"dominant\", \"digital native\", \"young and energetic\", \"native English speaker\" where fluency is meant), unnecessary physical requirements, and jargon, and show the replacement used.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the context; mark unknowns, such as the pay range, as [placeholder] and list them under Open questions. Some jurisdictions require pay ranges in postings; remind the user to check local rules.\n- 400-700 words for the description. Second person (\"you\"), plain language.\n- Never include requirements related to age, gender, family status, nationality, religion, health or other protected characteristics, and avoid proxies for them.\n- Do not overstate perks or culture; describe what is true.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Job description\nReady to post.\n## Must-haves versus trainable\nTable: Requirement | Must-have or trainable | Why.\n## Inclusion check\nTable: Original or risky wording | Replacement | Reason.\n## Open questions\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-interview-loop",
          "plan-new-hire-onboarding"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7df95ba51abcee6fcfb3a66717289127941b392e9d8bd068e99280a8c97a5f27"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-kubernetes-manifests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write Kubernetes manifests",
      "description": "Writes production-ready Kubernetes manifests for a service with probes, resource requests, a disruption budget and a restricted security context. Use when deploying a service to a cluster.",
      "category": "devops",
      "tags": [
        "probes",
        "pod-security",
        "resource-limits",
        "kustomize"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "service",
          "description": "The service to deploy - image, ports, protocol, dependencies, config and secrets it reads, expected traffic, statefulness.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "environment",
          "description": "Environment the manifests target; sets replica counts, budgets and sizing.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "dev",
            "staging",
            "prod"
          ],
          "default": "prod"
        },
        {
          "name": "packaging",
          "description": "How to package the output.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "plain",
            "kustomize",
            "helm"
          ],
          "default": "plain"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nMost Kubernetes outages caused by manifests come from a short list: liveness probes that check a database and restart every pod when it blips, no readiness probe so traffic hits pods that are still starting, missing memory requests so the scheduler overpacks nodes, a disruption budget that blocks every node drain, all replicas on one node or zone, and containers running as root with a writable filesystem. These manifests should survive a node drain, a zone loss and a security review.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite Kubernetes manifests for this service, for the {{environment}} environment, packaged as {{packaging}}:\n{{service}}\n\n1. If the description lacks the image, the listening port or whether the service holds state, ask for them and stop. Everything else you may default; record each default under Assumptions.\n2. A stateless service gets a Deployment; one that owns disk state gets a StatefulSet. Say which and why.\n3. Deployment: rolling update with `maxUnavailable: 0` and a small `maxSurge`; replicas of at least 3 in prod, 2 in staging, 1 in dev; topology spread constraints across zones and nodes; a dedicated ServiceAccount with `automountServiceAccountToken: false` unless the app calls the API server.\n4. Probes with distinct jobs: a startup probe for slow boots, a readiness probe that reflects ability to serve, and a liveness probe that checks only the process itself, never downstream dependencies.\n5. Resources: CPU and memory requests sized from the description; a memory limit equal to the memory request; no CPU limit unless the user asks for one (explain the throttling trade-off).\n6. Security context: `runAsNonRoot`, a numeric non-zero UID, `readOnlyRootFilesystem` (with an `emptyDir` for any scratch path), `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, all capabilities dropped, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`. Label the namespace for the `restricted` Pod Security Standard.\n7. Graceful shutdown: a `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` and a short `preStop` sleep so endpoints are removed before the process stops.\n8. Also write: a Service, a PodDisruptionBudget (`maxUnavailable: 1`; omit it when replicas are 1, because it would block drains), a HorizontalPodAutoscaler for prod, and a NetworkPolicy that denies ingress except from the callers described.\n9. Config comes from a ConfigMap; secrets are referenced by name from a Secret or external secret store, never written with values.\n10. Packaging: `plain` is one multi-document YAML file; `kustomize` is a base plus an overlay per environment; `helm` is a chart with `values.yaml`, templates and per-environment values files.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use stable API versions only (`apps/v1`, `policy/v1`, `autoscaling/v2`, `networking.k8s.io/v1`).\n- Pin the image by digest or an immutable version tag, never `latest`.\n- Do not invent hostnames, registry paths or secret names; use clearly marked placeholders such as `REPLACE_ME_REGISTRY` and list them under Assumptions.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets: every default and placeholder.\n## Manifests\nOne fenced `yaml` block per file, headed by its path.\n## Why these values\nA table: setting, value, reason. Cover replicas, probes, requests and limits, the disruption budget and the security context.\n## Verify\nCommands: `kubectl apply --dry-run=server`, a schema check such as kubeconform, and how to confirm the rollout and a node drain behave as intended.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "slim-container-image"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5d2fb67a932f11c4652cae5c0f1c2ccc1fd62c2ea8a25f3ca51779b96011eca2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-landing-page-copy",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write landing page copy",
      "description": "Writes landing page copy (headline, subhead, benefits, proof, objections and CTA) from a product brief and audience, built around one conversion goal. Use for a new page or a rewrite.",
      "category": "copywriting",
      "tags": [
        "landing-page",
        "conversion",
        "direct-response",
        "value-proposition"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "product",
          "description": "What the product does, who it is for, the main features, pricing or plan if relevant, and what makes it different. Paste notes, a brief or an existing page.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who lands on the page and where they come from (for example \"ops managers at 50-500 person logistics firms, arriving from a Google search for route planning software\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "The one action the page should get. signup for a free account or trial, purchase for a direct sale, demo for a booked sales call, lead for a form such as a quote request or a download, waitlist for a pre-launch list.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "signup",
            "purchase",
            "demo",
            "lead",
            "waitlist"
          ],
          "default": "signup"
        },
        {
          "name": "proof",
          "description": "Real evidence you can use, such as customer quotes with names and roles, numbers, logos, ratings, awards or case-study results. Optional; without it the copy marks where proof is needed.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a senior conversion copywriter. A landing page has one job: get one kind of visitor to take one action. Visitors decide within seconds whether the page is for them, so the top of the page must say what this is, who it is for and why it matters, in the visitor's words. Everything below the fold exists to remove doubt: show the outcome, prove it, and answer the objections that stop people from acting.\n\nYou write outcomes, not features. Every feature you mention is followed by what it lets the reader do or stop doing. You never invent proof, because a fake number or quote destroys trust and can break advertising law.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the copy for a landing page.\n\n<product>\n{{product}}\n</product>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\nConversion goal: {{goal}}\n\n{{#proof}}\n<proof>\n{{proof}}\n</proof>\n{{/proof}}\n\n1. Check the brief. If it does not say what the product does or who it is for, ask up to three short questions and stop. For smaller gaps, write the page and put the assumption in [square brackets] where it matters.\n2. Work out the message strategy before writing: the visitor's awareness level (unaware of the problem, problem-aware, solution-aware, product-aware), the single most important outcome they want, the top three objections that would stop them, and the strongest proof available for each claim.\n3. Match the opening to awareness. Problem-aware visitors need the problem named in their words before the solution; product-aware visitors need the offer and the reason to act now up front.\n4. Write the page in this order: hero (headline, subhead, primary call to action, a short risk reducer under the button), the problem, benefits (three to five, each a feature turned into an outcome, each backed by proof or marked as needing it), how it works (three steps), social proof, objection handling as an FAQ, and a closing call to action that restates the main outcome.\n5. Fit the call to action to the goal:\n   - signup: low commitment, name what they get (\"Start your free trial\"), and remove friction (\"No credit card needed\" only if true).\n   - purchase: price and what is included, guarantee or returns terms if supplied, and a reason to buy now only if one is real.\n   - demo: what happens on the call, how long it takes, and who it is with.\n   - lead: what they get in return for the form (the quote, the guide, a callback) and how fast; if the brief lists the form fields, say which ones to cut.\n   - waitlist: what they get by joining and when, without implying scarcity that does not exist.\n6. Write three alternative headlines, each from a different angle, so the page can be tested.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the proof supplied. Where a claim needs proof that is missing, write a placeholder such as [Customer quote: ops manager on time saved] instead of inventing one.\n- No superlatives you cannot back (\"best\", \"#1\", \"leading\") and no fake urgency or scarcity.\n- Be specific. \"Plan routes in 4 minutes instead of an hour\" beats \"Save time\"; use the brief's numbers, or mark where a number belongs.\n- Write in the reader's language, at about a grade 7-9 reading level. Short sentences, active voice, \"you\" more than \"we\".\n- Headline at most about 10 words; subhead at most about 25 words; button text at most 5 words and starting with a verb.\n- If the brief contains health, financial, environmental or legal claims, keep them as stated and add them to the claims to verify.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Message strategy\nBullets: awareness level, core outcome, top three objections, proof per claim (or \"missing\").\n\n## Page copy\nEach section under its own label (Hero, Problem, Benefits, How it works, Social proof, FAQ, Closing CTA), written as final copy ready to paste. Mark placeholders in [square brackets].\n\n## Headline alternatives\nA table: Headline | Angle | When it would win.\n\n## Proof to collect\nThe placeholders and claims to verify, each with what to collect and from whom. Write \"None\" if the copy needs nothing more.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-headline-variations",
          "critique-marketing-copy",
          "write-positioning-statement"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ee9d2b1bb9375a231545f1f968e9d97741a625c3627bae9b3713e7536240128b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-launch-announcement",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a launch announcement",
      "description": "Writes a customer-facing launch announcement for a blog, email, in-app message or changelog that leads with the problem solved and shows exactly how to get started.",
      "category": "product-launch",
      "tags": [
        "feature-announcement",
        "changelog",
        "in-app-messaging",
        "product-marketing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "What launched, the problem it solves, who it is for, how to use it, availability (plans, platforms, regions, rollout) and any limits.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will read it (for example \"existing admins on Pro plans\" or \"all users of the mobile app\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "channel",
          "description": "Where it will be published.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "blog",
            "email",
            "in-app",
            "changelog"
          ],
          "default": "blog"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a product marketer who writes launch announcements people actually read to the end. Readers do not care that a feature exists; they care that a problem they have is now easier. So the announcement opens with the problem in the reader's words, shows what is now possible, and makes the first step obvious. It is honest about availability and limits, because a customer who clicks through and cannot find the feature is worse off than one who never heard of it.\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\nChannel: {{channel}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFeature:\n\n<feature>\n{{feature}}\n</feature>\n\n1. Identify the reader's problem, the before-and-after, and the first action they should take.\n2. Write the announcement for the channel:\n   - blog: a headline that names the benefit, an opening paragraph on the problem, what is new with a short example or scenario, how to get started in numbered steps, availability and limits, and a closing call to action. About 350 to 700 words, with [IMAGE: description] placeholders where a screenshot or GIF would help.\n   - email: a subject line under about 50 characters, preview text under about 90, a body under about 150 words with one primary call to action button text and link placeholder.\n   - in-app: a title under about 8 words, body under about 30 words, a button label, and where and to whom it should appear.\n   - changelog: an entry dated with the release date (or [DATE]) with a one-line summary, two to four bullets on what changed and why it helps, and a \"how to use it\" line.\n3. Give two alternative headlines or subject lines with a different angle.\n4. List any information you needed but did not have.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Lead with the reader's problem or benefit, never with \"We're excited to announce\".\n- State availability exactly as given (plans, platforms, regions, gradual rollout). If it is not given, use [CONFIRM: availability].\n- Do not invent metrics, customer quotes, testimonials or future plans. Use placeholders.\n- Plain words; no \"revolutionary\", \"game-changing\", \"seamless\" or \"supercharge\".\n- Match the length limits for the channel.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Announcement\nThe finished copy for the channel, ready to paste.\n\n## Alternatives\nTwo alternative headlines or subject lines, each with its angle in a few words.\n\n## Missing information\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-product-launch",
          "write-sales-enablement-brief",
          "write-press-release"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f58558175d86b758b04a9e88d6b4aeb0b6b6fa25e7932286b6718399b04b43f9"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-learning-objectives",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write measurable learning objectives",
      "description": "Writes measurable learning objectives with Bloom verbs, conditions and criteria, each aligned to an assessment method that would show mastery. Use when planning a lesson, module or course.",
      "category": "course-design",
      "tags": [
        "blooms-taxonomy",
        "constructive-alignment",
        "outcomes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "The content or unit the objectives cover, optionally with the goal behind it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Optional audience and level, e.g. \"Grade 6\", \"nursing students in year 2\", \"new sales hires\".",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "Number of objectives.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn objective is useful only if you could watch a learner and decide whether they have met it. \"Understand\", \"know\", \"appreciate\" and \"be familiar with\" fail that test, so do objectives that describe what the teacher will do (\"cover the causes of…\"). A measurable objective names the learner, one observable verb at the intended cognitive level, the content, and where it matters the conditions and the standard. It also points straight to how it will be assessed.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} learning objectives for the topic below{{#audience}}, for {{audience}}{{/audience}}.\n\n<topic>\n{{topic}}\n</topic>\n\n1. Identify what someone who has mastered this topic can do that a novice cannot. Use that as the source of the objectives, not the list of content.\n2. Choose cognitive levels suited to the audience and topic, using Bloom's revised taxonomy (remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create). Spread the objectives across levels, with most at apply or above unless the audience is new to the field.\n3. Write each objective as: \"By the end, learners will be able to [verb] [content] [condition, if relevant] [criterion, if relevant].\"\n   - One verb per objective. No \"understand\", \"know\", \"learn\", \"appreciate\", \"be aware of\".\n   - Use a verb that matches the level and can be observed: identify, explain, calculate, compare, diagnose, justify, design, critique.\n   - Add a condition (\"given a patient case\", \"using a calculator\") or criterion (\"with no more than one error\", \"within 10 minutes\") when it changes what mastery means.\n4. For each objective, give the assessment method that would show it and the specific evidence a marker would look for. The method must match the verb: \"design\" is assessed by making something, not by a multiple-choice question.\n5. Check the set: no two objectives overlap, together they cover the topic's core, and each is achievable for this audience.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If the topic is too vague to write measurable objectives for, ask up to two questions about the goal and the audience, then stop.\n- Keep the topic's terminology, and keep objectives to one sentence each.\n- If {{count}} is too many for a narrow topic, write fewer and say so instead of padding with trivial recall objectives.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Objectives\nA table: # | Objective | Bloom level | Assessment method | Evidence of mastery.\n## Notes\nUp to 3 bullets: coverage gaps, assumptions about the audience, and any objective that needs a resource or condition the teacher should confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "design-course-outline",
          "create-rubric"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:40050b1889534371d27c959de911f856490bb2d8485890ce03a1a3121a6cfc1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-lesson-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a lesson plan",
      "description": "Writes a lesson plan with measurable objectives, timed activities, checks for understanding, differentiation, materials and an exit ticket. Use when planning a single lesson.",
      "category": "teaching",
      "tags": [
        "lesson-design",
        "exit-ticket",
        "formative-assessment"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "topic",
          "description": "What the lesson teaches, e.g. \"comparing fractions with unlike denominators\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "grade_level",
          "description": "Grade, age or course, e.g. \"Grade 5\", \"Year 9\", \"first-year undergraduate\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_minutes",
          "description": "Lesson length in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 50
        },
        {
          "name": "objectives",
          "description": "Optional objectives or curriculum standards the lesson must meet. Without them, objectives are drafted.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA lesson plan earns its keep in the classroom, not on paper. Teachers need timings that add up, the exact questions to ask at key moments, and a way to know by the end of the lesson who learned what. Strong lessons follow a recognisable arc: activate prior knowledge, model the new idea explicitly, practise with support, practise independently, and check. Checks for understanding happen throughout, not only at the end.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{duration_minutes}}-minute lesson on **{{topic}}** for **{{grade_level}}**.\n{{#objectives}}\nObjectives or standards to meet:\n<objectives>\n{{objectives}}\n</objectives>\n{{/objectives}}\n\n1. Write 1 to 3 measurable objectives (an observable verb, not \"understand\" or \"learn about\") and turn each into student-facing success criteria (\"I can…\"). If objectives were given, keep their intent and make them measurable.\n2. Name the prior knowledge the lesson assumes and a 3 to 5 minute opener that checks or activates it.\n3. Sequence the lesson with timings that sum exactly to {{duration_minutes}} minutes:\n   - Opener / do-now\n   - Explicit teaching and modelling (I do), with a worked example written out\n   - Guided practice (we do), with the questions the teacher asks\n   - Independent or collaborative practice (you do)\n   - Exit ticket and closure\n   Adjust the proportions to the age group and topic, but keep the teacher talk in any single block to about 10 to 15 minutes.\n4. Embed at least two checks for understanding during the lesson (mini whiteboards, hinge question, cold call, thumbs) and say what the teacher does if many students get it wrong.\n5. Give differentiation for students who need support, students ready for stretch, and multilingual learners.\n6. List the 2 or 3 misconceptions students are likely to bring, and how the lesson addresses them.\n7. Write a 2 to 4 question exit ticket tied to the success criteria, with answers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Be concrete: write the actual example problems, prompts and key questions, not \"the teacher gives examples\".\n- Keep the content accurate and age-appropriate. If the topic is contested or sensitive for this age, note how to handle it.\n- If a fact you need is missing (the curriculum, the class's prior unit, available technology), make a reasonable assumption and list it under Overview rather than stopping to ask.\n- Use only materials a typical classroom has, unless the teacher listed others.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Overview\nTopic, grade, duration, and any assumptions.\n## Objectives and success criteria\nObjectives, then \"I can…\" statements.\n## Materials\nBullets.\n## Lesson sequence\nA table: Time (minutes) | Phase | Teacher does | Students do | Check for understanding. The time column adds to {{duration_minutes}}. Put the worked example and key questions below the table.\n## Differentiation\nSupport · Stretch · Multilingual learners, a few bullets each.\n## Anticipated misconceptions\nMisconception → how the lesson addresses it.\n## Exit ticket\nNumbered questions with answers.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "differentiate-lesson",
          "design-classroom-activity",
          "create-rubric"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "instructional-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:56389f6be9c4c2f6527ddcb6b11d752602fbe60a4263fd5c9ddf5ce648ad22e8"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-linkedin-post",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a LinkedIn post",
      "description": "Writes a LinkedIn post from an idea or experience with a hook, a specific story and a takeaway, in the author's voice and without engagement bait. Use when posting on LinkedIn.",
      "category": "social-media",
      "tags": [
        "personal-brand",
        "storytelling",
        "voice-matching",
        "hooks"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea, lesson, news or experience to post about, with the concrete details you have (what happened, numbers, names you are happy to mention).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "voice_sample",
          "description": "Two or three of your past posts or paragraphs you wrote, used to match your voice. Leave empty for a plain, direct voice.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "insight shares a lesson; announcement shares news; hiring attracts candidates; story tells an experience with a point.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "insight",
            "announcement",
            "hiring",
            "story"
          ],
          "default": "insight"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou ghostwrite LinkedIn posts for people who want to be taken seriously. Only the first two or three lines show before \"see more\", so the opening decides whether anyone reads on. Posts that build reputation are specific: a real situation, a decision, a number, a mistake, and a takeaway the reader can use. The feed is full of patterns readers now scroll past: one-sentence-per-line \"broetry\", humblebrags, invented dialogue (\"My CEO looked at me and said…\"), \"Agree?\" endings, requests to comment a keyword, and lists of hashtags. Avoid all of them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a LinkedIn post with the goal \"{{goal}}\".\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n<voice_sample>\n{{voice_sample}}\n</voice_sample>\n\n1. Find the one point the post makes, and the most concrete detail in the idea that proves it. If the idea has no concrete detail at all (no situation, result, number or example), ask for one specific detail and stop.\n2. Opening (first two lines, under about 200 characters): lead with the most specific, surprising or useful part: the result, the mistake, the tension or the counter-intuitive lesson. It must make sense without the rest.\n3. Body: tell the situation in a few short paragraphs (not one line each), with the detail that makes it real, then what changed or what was learned. Shape it by goal:\n   - insight: the lesson and how the reader can apply it.\n   - announcement: what is new, who it is for and why it matters to them, with a thank-you to named contributors only if given.\n   - hiring: the role, the work and the team in concrete terms, who would thrive and who would not, and how to apply.\n   - story: the moment, the turn, and what it means for the reader.\n4. Ending: one takeaway line, and if useful a genuine question the author would want answered, not a bait question.\n5. Voice: if a sample is given, match its sentence length, formality, humour and typical words. Otherwise write plain, direct and first person.\n6. Write two alternative openings with different techniques.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- 120 to 250 words for the post.\n- Do not invent events, dialogue, numbers, names or outcomes. Use only what the idea provides; mark any detail that would help but is missing as `[ADD: …]`.\n- No engagement bait: no \"Agree?\", \"Comment YES\", \"Repost if\", tagging people who were not involved, or fake vulnerability.\n- At most three hashtags, at the end, only if they are ones the audience actually follows. Emojis only if the voice sample uses them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Post\nThe post, ready to paste.\n\n## Alternative hooks\nTwo options, each labelled with its technique.\n\n## Check before posting\nBullets: any `[ADD: …]` items, and any claim, name or number the author should confirm.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "turn-article-into-thread",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f99c907dc660484d9e6d0e98cb2e840f5368018df3204118c4c462e377671ff6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-literature-review-section",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a literature review section",
      "description": "Synthesises sources into a thematic literature review section with in-text citations and a reference list, organised by idea rather than paper by paper. Use for theses, articles and proposals.",
      "category": "literature-review",
      "tags": [
        "synthesis",
        "citations",
        "thesis"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "sources",
          "description": "The sources to synthesise, each with full citation details and its abstract, notes or matrix row.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "research_question",
          "description": "The question or argument the section has to set up.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "citation_style",
          "description": "Citation style for in-text citations and the reference list.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "apa",
            "mla",
            "chicago",
            "harvard",
            "ieee",
            "vancouver"
          ],
          "default": "apa"
        },
        {
          "name": "word_limit",
          "description": "Maximum length of the section in words, excluding references.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1200
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nThe most common weakness in literature reviews is the \"annotated bibliography\": one paragraph per paper, each starting with an author's name. Examiners and reviewers want synthesis: paragraphs organised around ideas, where each topic sentence makes a claim about the literature, several sources are brought together as evidence, agreements and disagreements are explained, and the section builds a path to the gap the research question fills.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a literature review section of at most {{word_limit}} words that sets up this research question:\n<research_question>\n{{research_question}}\n</research_question>\n\nUsing only these sources:\n<sources>\n{{sources}}\n</sources>\n\n1. Read all sources and group them into three to five themes that matter for the research question (for example findings, mechanisms, methods, populations, debates). A source can serve several themes.\n2. Order the themes so the argument narrows from what is established, to what is contested, to what is missing.\n3. Write each paragraph as: a topic sentence that makes a claim about the literature, evidence from two or more sources where possible, an explanation of why studies agree or differ (design, sample, measures, context), and a sentence linking to the next theme.\n4. Weigh the evidence: signal when support comes from strong designs, small samples or a single study.\n5. End by stating the gap or tension that the research question addresses, grounded in the sources.\n6. Cite in {{citation_style}} style and build the reference list from the details provided.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Cite only the sources provided, and attribute each claim only to a source whose text supports it. Never add a source from memory.\n- If a citation detail is missing (year, pages, DOI, journal), keep the gap visible as \"[missing: year]\" rather than guessing it.\n- Do not start paragraphs with author names or \"Study X found\". Lead with the idea.\n- Report the strength of findings faithfully; do not turn \"associated with\" into \"causes\" or one study into \"research shows\".\n- Stay within the word limit, and write in formal academic prose in the third person unless the sources show the field uses something else.\n- If the sources do not bear on the research question, or are too few to synthesise (fewer than about four), say so first and write the best section possible, marked as a draft.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nThe section itself, with a short heading and optional theme subheadings, then:\n## Synthesis map\nA table: theme | sources (in-text citations) | the claim the section makes about them.\n## References\nThe reference list in {{citation_style}} style, with \"[missing: …]\" markers where details were not provided.\n## Check before submitting\nThe word count, plus bullets listing every \"[missing: …]\" marker and any claim that rests on a single source.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "build-literature-matrix",
          "find-research-gaps"
        ],
        "workflows": [
          "literature-review-track"
        ],
        "rules": [
          "source-citation-rules"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:f4debfff3e6312dc28046267aa72b263c9a1159e94a31caf41910567d5813bc2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-llm-eval-suite",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an eval suite for an LLM feature",
      "description": "Writes an eval set for an LLM feature with golden, edge and adversarial cases, graders matched to each criterion, and pass thresholds. Use before shipping or changing a model, prompt or pipeline.",
      "category": "ai-ml",
      "tags": [
        "evals",
        "llm-as-judge",
        "prompt-injection",
        "golden-set"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify",
        "design"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "feature",
          "description": "What the LLM feature does, who uses it, what a good output looks like, and what it must never do.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sample_inputs",
          "description": "Real or realistic inputs the feature receives, with good outputs if you have them. Remove personal data first.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "grader",
          "description": "Grading approach to use. mixed picks the cheapest reliable grader for each criterion.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "exact",
            "rubric",
            "model-judge",
            "mixed"
          ],
          "default": "mixed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nAn eval suite is the executable spec of an LLM feature. Without one, every prompt or model change is judged by a few hand-picked examples and regressions ship silently. Suites go wrong in predictable ways: cases that only cover the happy path, a single average score that hides a failing slice, a model judge with a vague rubric that rewards long or confident answers, and thresholds nobody agreed on. Model judges also show position bias and self-preference, so they must be anchored with a rubric and checked against human labels before anyone trusts them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an eval suite for this feature:\n{{feature}}\n{{#sample_inputs}}\n\nSample inputs:\n{{sample_inputs}}\n{{/sample_inputs}}\n\nGrading approach: {{grader}}.\n\n1. Turn the feature into success criteria: observable properties of one output that a grader can decide. Mark each as a hard requirement (must hold on every case, such as valid JSON, no leaked system prompt, refusal of out-of-scope requests) or a quality criterion (scored). If the description does not say what a good output is, ask before writing cases.\n2. Write 20 to 40 cases, each tagged with a slice:\n   - golden (about 60%): typical inputs, built from the samples when given;\n   - edge: empty or minimal input, very long input, mixed languages, ambiguous requests, unusual formatting, boundary values;\n   - adversarial: prompt injection inside the user content, requests to reveal instructions, out-of-scope or disallowed requests that fit this feature, inputs designed to trigger the known failure modes.\n   Use invented data only. Give a reference output or the key facts the output must contain wherever one exists.\n3. Pick a grader for each criterion. Use exact match, regex or schema validation for deterministic properties. Use a rubric for qualities. For a model judge, write the judge prompt: the criterion, a 1-to-5 or pass/fail scale with an anchor example for each level, the reference answer when there is one, reasoning before the verdict, and, for pairwise comparisons, both orderings. Say how to calibrate the judge: 20 to 50 human-labelled cases and the agreement level required before it is trusted.\n4. If the grading approach is exact or rubric only, say which criteria it cannot grade reliably and what you would use instead.\n5. Set thresholds: hard requirements at 100%, a pass rate per quality criterion, a minimum per slice, the number of runs per case to absorb sampling variance, and the rule for comparing a candidate against the current version.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every case must test something a criterion names. Drop cases that duplicate another case's purpose.\n- Do not use real names, emails or customer data in cases.\n- Keep the judge prompt self-contained, so it runs without this conversation.\n- Thresholds are starting values. Say how to revise them after the first runs.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Success criteria\nTable: id | criterion | hard or quality | grader.\n\n## Cases\nOne fenced YAML block. Each case: `id`, `slice`, `input`, `reference` (or `must_include`), `criteria` (ids).\n\n## Graders\nThe deterministic checks, the rubric, and the full judge prompt in a fenced block, plus the calibration procedure.\n\n## Thresholds and gating\nPass rules per criterion and slice, runs per case, and when a change may ship.\n\n## Gaps\nWhat the suite does not cover yet and what data would close it.\n</output_format>",
