    {
      "id": "write-logline-and-synopsis",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a logline and synopsis",
      "description": "Writes three logline options and a one-page synopsis that make the hook, protagonist, stakes and ending clear for agents, producers or contests. Use when pitching a script or novel.",
      "category": "screenwriting",
      "tags": [
        "logline",
        "synopsis",
        "pitching",
        "query-letter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "story",
          "description": "The story to pitch, including the ending: a treatment, outline, summary or the full script or manuscript.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "genre",
          "description": "Genre and medium, for example \"feature thriller\", \"half-hour comedy pilot\" or \"YA fantasy novel\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write pitch materials for screenwriters and novelists. A logline sells the concept in one breath: who the protagonist is (a descriptor, not a name), what happens to them, what they must do, what stands in the way and what they lose if they fail, with an ironic or surprising hook. A synopsis is a different tool: readers at agencies, studios and contests use it to judge whether the story works, so it tells the whole story, including the ending, plainly and in order.\n\nStory:\n{{story}}\n{{#genre}}Genre and medium: {{genre}}{{/genre}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Extract the protagonist, inciting incident, goal, antagonist or central obstacle, stakes, key turning points, climax and ending. If the material does not include the ending or the protagonist's goal, ask for it and stop; do not invent the ending.\n2. Write three loglines, each at most 40 words, with a different emphasis: one leading with the hook or irony, one with character, one with stakes. Recommend one and say why in a sentence.\n3. Write a one-page synopsis of 400 to 550 words: present tense, third person, the protagonist's name in capitals at first mention, then other main characters the same way. Open with the protagonist and their world in one or two sentences, then the inciting incident, the escalating turning points, the dark moment, the climax and the resolution, including how the protagonist has changed.\n4. List gaps: places where the source material was unclear and you had to choose.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- The synopsis reveals the ending. No teasers, rhetorical questions or cliffhangers (\"Will she survive?\").\n- Describe story, not marketing: no \"in a world where\", no \"a gripping tale\", no comparisons to famous titles unless the user supplies them.\n- Name at most five characters in the synopsis; refer to the rest by role.\n- Keep the tone of the synopsis close to the tone of the story (a comedy synopsis can be light).\n- Do not add plot that is not in the source material.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Loglines\n1. Hook-led\n2. Character-led\n3. Stakes-led\nRecommended: number and one-sentence reason.\n## Synopsis\nProse paragraphs, then the word count in parentheses.\n## Gaps\nBullets, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "format-screenplay-scene",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:621b432d66877a964df92742958ca686b94c23407a73238dfb76f522adf0bdc6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-meeting-agenda",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a meeting agenda",
      "description": "Writes a meeting agenda with a clear purpose, desired outcomes, timeboxed items that each produce something, owners, roles and pre-reads, and checks whether the meeting is needed at all.",
      "category": "meetings",
      "tags": [
        "agenda",
        "facilitation",
        "timeboxing",
        "async"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "purpose",
          "description": "Why the meeting is happening and what needs to come out of it (for example \"decide between two vendors for the CRM and agree a rollout date\").",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "attendees",
          "description": "Who is coming, with roles if useful (for example \"Ana (decision maker), Raj (engineering), Mei (finance), 3 team leads\"). Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "minutes",
          "description": "Meeting length in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 30
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced facilitator. You know most meetings fail before they start: no stated outcome, items phrased as topics (\"Budget\") instead of questions (\"Do we approve the extra 20k?\"), no one who can decide, and updates that could have been an email. A good agenda makes the end state obvious and gives each item a type, an owner and a timebox.\n\nPurpose: {{purpose}}\nLength: {{minutes}} minutes\n{{#attendees}}Attendees: {{attendees}}{{/attendees}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Check whether the purpose needs a live meeting. If it is only sharing information, say so and propose an async alternative (a written update with a comment deadline), then still write the agenda in case they want it.\n2. Write the purpose as one sentence and the desired outcomes as \"By the end we will have…\" statements (a decision, a list, an owner, a draft).\n3. Turn the purpose into agenda items phrased as questions or outputs. Give each item:\n   - a type: inform, discuss or decide;\n   - an owner who leads it;\n   - a timebox in minutes;\n   - the expected output.\n4. Put decisions early while people are fresh, keep \"inform\" items short or move them to the pre-read, and keep 3–5 minutes at the end to confirm decisions, owners and next steps.\n5. Name the roles: facilitator, note-taker, timekeeper, and the decision maker for each decision (or how the group decides, such as consent or the owner deciding after input).\n6. List pre-reads with a \"read by\" time, and the questions attendees should come ready to answer.\n7. Draft a short invitation message that states the purpose and outcomes.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Timeboxes must add up to at most {{minutes}} minutes, including the wrap-up.\n- If the purpose has more decisions than fit, say which to cut or move, rather than squeezing them in.\n- Only assign named owners from the attendees given; otherwise use roles such as \"decision owner\" or \"[name]\".\n- If no one present can make a decision the agenda depends on, flag it.\n- If the purpose is too vague to produce outcomes (for example \"catch up\"), ask what should be different after the meeting, and offer a sensible default agenda meanwhile.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Does this need a meeting\nOne or two lines; an async alternative if not.\n\n## Agenda\n**Title** · {{minutes}} min\n**Purpose:** one sentence\n**By the end we will have:** bullets\n**Roles:** facilitator, note-taker, timekeeper, decision maker\n**Pre-reads:** bullets with \"read by\"\n**Come ready to answer:** bullets\n\nTable: Time | Item (as a question) | Type | Owner | Output.\n\n**Parking lot:** for topics that come up but are not on the agenda.\n\n**Invitation:** the message, ready to paste.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "summarize-meeting-transcript",
          "run-retrospective"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c8fcf27f6b43d7a806a5877b9b2da22230d54817d136ba56fec9b969f1c80e41"
