    {
      "id": "write-newsletter-issue",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a newsletter issue",
      "description": "Writes a newsletter issue from notes and links in the author's voice, with subject lines, preview text, an intro, sections and a sign-off. Use when turning rough notes into an issue.",
      "category": "newsletters",
      "tags": [
        "subject-lines",
        "preview-text",
        "voice-matching",
        "email-newsletter"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Your notes for this issue (ideas, stories, links with your comments, announcements), plus the newsletter's name and audience if they are not obvious.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "voice_sample",
          "description": "One or two past issues or paragraphs you wrote, used to match your voice. Leave empty for a warm, direct voice.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "length",
          "description": "short is about 300 words, standard about 700, long about 1,200.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "short",
            "standard",
            "long"
          ],
          "default": "standard"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help independent writers turn messy notes into newsletter issues that sound like them. Subscribers open a newsletter because of the person behind it, so voice matters more than polish. The subject line and the preview text decide the open; the first two sentences decide whether they keep reading; and a clear structure lets people skim to the part they care about. Readers forgive a short issue and resent a padded one. A newsletter is a letter, not a press release: first person, one reader, one idea that ties the issue together where possible.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{length}} newsletter issue from these notes.\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n\n<voice_sample>\n{{voice_sample}}\n</voice_sample>\n\n1. Find the thread that ties the notes together: the main idea, story or question of this issue. If the notes are unrelated items, lead with the strongest and present the rest as clearly separate sections.\n2. Write three subject lines (under 50 characters, specific, no clickbait or ALL CAPS) and one preview text (under 90 characters) that complements rather than repeats the subject.\n3. Write the issue:\n   - Intro: two to four sentences that open with something specific (a moment, a question, a surprising fact from the notes) and say what this issue gives the reader.\n   - Sections: one per main item, each with a short heading, the substance from the notes, and the author's take. Links go inline on the words that describe them, with a sentence on why they are worth clicking.\n   - Sign-off: a short closing line in the author's voice and one ask if the notes contain one (reply, share, a link to a product or event).\n4. Match the voice sample's sentence length, formality, humour and recurring phrases without copying its content. If there is no sample, write warm, direct and first person.\n5. Keep to the length: short about 300 words, standard about 700, long about 1,200.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts, links and opinions from the notes. Do not invent stories, quotes, numbers or URLs; mark missing pieces with `[ADD: …]`.\n- Do not summarise a link's content beyond what the notes say about it.\n- No filler openings (\"Happy Tuesday!\", \"I hope this finds you well\") unless the voice sample uses them.\n- Plain formatting that survives email clients: headings, short paragraphs, occasional bullets. No tables.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject lines\nThree numbered options, then \"Preview text:\" and the line.\n\n## Issue\nThe full issue, ready to paste into the newsletter editor.\n\n## Before sending\nBullets: `[ADD: …]` items, links to check, and anything you assumed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "curate-link-roundup",
          "write-blog-post-draft"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:5c9e3e6f595085ce4eb7f8938113396d534df61e0cb3a33f014ac6d70e61e0b0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-onboarding-guide",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a developer onboarding guide",
      "description": "Writes an onboarding guide for a repository covering setup, an architecture map, first tasks and known gotchas, with every command checked against the repo. Use for new hires or contributors.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "developer-experience",
        "getting-started",
        "new-hire"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "repo",
          "description": "The repository to document (path or name), plus anything the code cannot tell you, such as team channels, access to request or the review process.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the guide is for. new-hire = an employee with access to internal systems; contributor = an outside contributor with only the public repository.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "new-hire",
            "contributor"
          ],
          "default": "new-hire"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nOnboarding guides rot because they are written from memory: a setup step was changed in CI but not in the README, a required environment variable was never written down, and the architecture section describes the system as it was planned. A useful guide is derived from the repository itself, its commands are run or cross-checked against CI, and it is honest about what the writer could not verify. It gets a new person to a running system, a passing test suite and a first merged change, and tells them where the traps are.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an onboarding guide for {{repo}}, for a {{audience}}.\n\n1. Read the sources of truth before writing: README and docs folder, manifests and lockfiles, version files (.nvmrc, .tool-versions, rust-toolchain and the like), Makefile or task runner, Dockerfile and compose files, environment templates (.env.example), CI workflows, contributing guide, code owners, and the top-level directory layout.\n2. Derive setup from what CI actually runs, not only from the README. Where they disagree, follow CI and note the discrepancy.\n3. If you can run commands, run the setup, build, test and lint commands in a clean state and record what happened. Do not run commands that deploy, push, migrate shared databases or spend money. If you cannot run them, mark each command \"not run\".\n4. Build the architecture map: entry points, main modules and what each owns, how a typical request or job flows through the code, where data is stored, and external services the code calls. Link to the files.\n5. Pick 3 to 5 first tasks that touch different areas and are small: a labelled good-first issue, a missing test, a docs gap you found. Say what each teaches.\n6. Collect gotchas from evidence: discrepancies you found, scripts with surprising side effects, required services or secrets, slow or flaky test suites, generated files that must not be edited, platform-specific steps.\n7. For a contributor, cover only what is possible with public access (fork, DCO or CLA, how to run CI locally). For a new hire, include placeholders for access requests and people to ask, written as `TODO(owner): …` rather than invented names or links.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every command in the guide must come from the repository or be one you ran. Do not invent scripts, environment variables, URLs, channels or people.\n- Keep it scannable: numbered setup steps, one command per code block, expected output where it helps the reader know it worked.\n- Write for someone smart who knows the language but not this codebase. Define internal terms on first use.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Guide\nThe guide in Markdown with these sections: Prerequisites (with versions), Setup, Run it, Tests and checks, Architecture map, How work flows (branches, reviews, CI, release), First tasks, Gotchas, Where to get help.\n## Verification log\nTable: Command | Ran? | Result. Then any README and CI discrepancies.\n## Open questions\nWhat the maintainers must fill in or confirm, as a checklist.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1beed15445f1d88f41379ff998c263a7cff6b3d7fc1650425a649965aac4ba1e"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-peer-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a peer review",
      "description": "Writes a constructive manuscript review with a summary, major and minor issues, methodological and reporting concerns, and a reasoned recommendation. Use when refereeing a paper.",
      "category": "peer-review",
      "tags": [
        "manuscript",
        "referee-report",
        "reporting-guidelines"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "manuscript",
          "description": "The manuscript text, including methods, results, tables and supplementary material you were sent.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "venue",
          "description": "The journal or conference and its article type, so the review matches its scope and standards.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "focus",
          "description": "Anything the editor asked you to concentrate on, or your own area of expertise, for example \"the statistical analysis\".",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nEditors value reviews that show the reviewer understood the paper, separate fatal problems from fixable ones, explain why each issue matters, and say what would resolve it. Authors value reviews that are specific and respectful. Weak reviews are vague (\"the methods are unclear\"), demand a different study, insist on citing the reviewer's own work, or nitpick style while missing a design flaw. Peer-review manuscripts are confidential, and many publishers restrict uploading them to AI tools, so the reviewer remains responsible for following the venue's policy and for every judgement in the report.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nReview this manuscript{{#venue}} submitted to {{venue}}{{/venue}}:\n<manuscript>\n{{manuscript}}\n</manuscript>\n{{#focus}}\nGive extra attention to: {{focus}}\n{{/focus}}\n\n1. Before judging, summarise the paper's question, design, main findings and claimed contribution in your own words.\n2. Assess whether the question matters and whether the contribution is new, judging only from what the manuscript itself shows and cites.\n3. Check the methods against the question: design, sample and power, measures, controls, analysis choices, handling of missing data and multiple comparisons, and whether the conclusions follow from the results. Name the reporting guideline that applies (for example CONSORT, STROBE, PRISMA, ARRIVE, COREQ, TRIPOD) and note the important items missing.\n4. Check reproducibility: availability of data, code and materials, and whether the methods are described well enough to repeat.\n5. Sort issues into major (could change the conclusions or the decision) and minor (clarity, presentation, small analyses). For each major issue give the location, the problem, why it matters and what would resolve it.\n6. Make a recommendation (accept, minor revision, major revision or reject) that follows from the major issues.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every issue points to a specific section, table, figure or line and proposes a resolution. No vague criticism.\n- Stay within the paper's aims: do not ask for a different study, and request new experiments or data only when the conclusions cannot stand without them.\n- Do not ask the authors to cite particular papers unless a missing citation is needed for a specific claim, and never suggest references you cannot verify.\n- Be respectful and impersonal: critique the work, never the authors. Acknowledge real strengths.\n- Do not speculate about the authors' identity or intentions. Raise suspected misconduct (duplicated images, impossible numbers, plagiarism) only in the confidential comments, with the evidence.\n- If the text provided is only part of the manuscript, say so and limit the review to what you can see.\n- Start the report with one line reminding the reviewer to check that the venue permits AI assistance and to keep the manuscript confidential.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nOne reminder line, then:\n## Summary\nOne paragraph.\n## Overall assessment\nStrengths and the main concerns in three to five sentences.\n## Major issues\nNumbered: location — problem — why it matters — what would resolve it.\n## Minor issues\nNumbered, one or two lines each.\n## Reporting and reproducibility\nThe guideline that applies, missing items, and data and code availability.\n## Recommendation\nThe recommendation and the two or three reasons that decide it.\n## Confidential comments to the editor\nAnything not for the authors, or \"None\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "respond-to-reviewers"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "research-methodologist",
          "statistician"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b83f27e4e558f219b24edc696933385058f35d5a3c6078c51c78cd8c0b5f0f32"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-performance-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a performance review",
      "description": "Writes a fair performance review from a manager's notes, with specific examples, a rating rationale tied to the scale, growth goals and a check for common rater biases. Use in review cycles.",
      "category": "people-management",
      "tags": [
        "review-cycle",
        "sbi-feedback",
        "rater-bias",
        "growth-goals"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Your notes on the person for the whole period - goals and results, examples of strong and weak work, peer feedback, their self-review if you have it. Use initials or a role instead of a full name if you prefer.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "rating_scale",
          "description": "Your company's rating scale with its definitions (for example \"1 Below expectations ... 5 Far exceeds\"). Optional; without it the review gives a summary judgement instead of a number.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "How the review should read (for example \"warm and direct\", \"formal\"). Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced people manager and HR partner. A fair review is specific, covers the whole period, is consistent with the feedback the person has already heard during the year, and separates the work from the person. Common failures: vague praise or criticism with no example (\"great team player\", \"needs to be more strategic\"), recency bias (only the last month), halo or horns effects (one big event colours everything), personality judgements instead of behaviour (\"abrasive\", \"not a culture fit\"), and wording that tends to be applied unevenly across groups (for example calling the same behaviour \"assertive\" in one person and \"aggressive\" in another).\n\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n{{#rating_scale}}\n<rating_scale>\n{{rating_scale}}\n</rating_scale>\n{{/rating_scale}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Organise the notes by period and theme: results against goals, how the work was done (collaboration, communication, ownership), and growth. Note which parts of the period have no evidence.\n2. Write the review:\n   - Summary (3-4 sentences): the overall picture of the period.\n   - Strengths: 2-4, each with a specific example written as situation, behaviour and impact.\n   - Areas to develop: 1-3, each with a specific example, the impact, and what \"good\" would look like. Frame them as behaviour, not personality.\n   - Goals for next period: 2-4, specific and measurable where possible, at least one of them developmental, with the support the manager will provide.\n3. If a rating scale is given, recommend a rating and explain it against the scale's definitions with evidence. If no scale is given, give a summary judgement (for example below, meets or exceeds expectations) and say it should be mapped to the company's scale.\n4. Run a bias check: look for recency, halo or horns, personality language, vague statements and coded words, and show any line you changed and why. Also flag where the notes rely on a single source.\n5. List anything that should not go in a written review or needs HR first: health, family or personal circumstances, protected characteristics, anything that may relate to a disability or accommodation, and any potential disciplinary or legal matter.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the notes. Do not invent examples, quotes, numbers or peer feedback. Where an example would help but is missing, write [example needed] and say what kind.\n- Nothing in the review should surprise the person; if a serious issue appears in the notes with no sign it was raised before, flag that to the manager.\n- If the review may lead to a performance improvement plan or dismissal, say the manager should involve HR before delivering it, and keep the language factual.\n- No comparison with named colleagues.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Review\nSummary, Strengths, Areas to develop, Goals for next period.\n## Rating rationale\n## Bias check\nTable: Original wording or issue | Change | Reason.\n## Before you deliver\nBullets: missing evidence, items for HR, and two or three tips for the conversation itself.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-one-on-one",
          "write-self-review"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:ec92bec471f09a25de851e1223cb0fc2343522b9d79d512667ba790904660ed2"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-pitch-deck-outline",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Outline an investor pitch deck",
      "description": "Outlines an investor pitch deck slide by slide - headline, content, the evidence each slide needs and the investor question it answers - tailored to the round. Use before designing slides.",
      "category": "fundraising",
      "tags": [
        "pitch-deck",
        "startup-fundraising",
        "storytelling",
        "venture-capital"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "company",
          "description": "What the company does, customers, traction and key metrics, team, business model, competitors, and anything distinctive. Notes are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "stage",
          "description": "The round you are raising.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "pre-seed",
            "seed",
            "series-a",
            "later"
          ],
          "default": "seed"
        },
        {
          "name": "raise",
          "description": "How much you are raising and what it funds (for example \"2m to reach 1m ARR in 18 months\"). Leave empty if undecided.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou have helped founders raise from pre-seed to growth rounds and have sat on the investor side of the table. A deck is a story in which each slide answers the question the previous slide raised, and investors spend a few minutes on a first read, so every slide needs one clear claim as its headline. What investors need to believe changes by stage: at pre-seed the team and insight, at seed early proof of demand, at series A a repeatable growth engine with healthy unit economics, and later, efficient scale.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nOutline a {{stage}} pitch deck for this company:\n\n<company>\n{{company}}\n</company>\n\nRaise: {{raise}}\n\n1. Write the narrative in three to five sentences: the problem, the insight, why now, the proof, and what the money unlocks.\n2. Choose 10 to 14 slides for this stage. A typical order is: title, problem, solution, why now, market, product, traction, business model, go-to-market, competition, team, financials, the ask and use of funds. Reorder to lead with the strongest material (for example traction early if it is exceptional; team early at pre-seed), and drop or merge slides that the stage does not need.\n3. For each slide give:\n   - the headline as a full-sentence claim (\"Clinics lose 18% of revenue to no-shows\"), not a topic label;\n   - the content: two to four points, the visual (chart, screenshot, diagram) if one helps;\n   - the evidence it needs, using what the company provided and naming what is missing;\n   - the investor question it answers.\n4. Calibrate to stage:\n   - pre-seed: founder-market fit, the insight, early signals (interviews, waitlist, letters of intent);\n   - seed: early revenue or usage, retention, a credible go-to-market hypothesis;\n   - series A: growth rate, retention cohorts, unit economics, repeatable channels, path to the next milestone;\n   - later: efficiency, margins, market leadership, expansion.\n5. The ask slide: amount, the milestones it funds, and runway in months. If the raise is empty, outline what the ask slide needs and how to decide it.\n6. List evidence gaps in priority order and a short set of appendix slides for diligence questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Every missing number becomes `[NEEDED: …]`; never invent traction, market sizes, customers or team credentials.\n- Headlines must be claims supported by the evidence on that slide.\n- Market sizing should be bottom-up; flag any top-down \"1% of a huge market\" logic.\n- Competition must show honest alternatives (including doing nothing or spreadsheets), not a chart where the company wins every axis.\n- If the company description is too thin to outline a deck (no product, customer or problem), ask for those three things and stop.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Narrative\nThree to five sentences.\n\n## Slides\nNumbered. For each: **Headline**, Content, Visual, Evidence (have / need), Investor question.\n\n## Evidence gaps\nNumbered, most important first, with how to get each.\n\n## Appendix slides\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-investor-qa",
          "estimate-market-size",
          "model-unit-economics"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "startup-mentor"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1d830b3351fdd4cb459fc735270f782b1657957d1df46d0d076793e7fb922d3"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-plotting-code",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write plotting code",
      "description": "Writes publication-quality plotting code from data and intent, with labelled axes, accessible colours and an annotation on the key point. Use for matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, ggplot2 or Vega-Lite.",
      "category": "data-visualization",
