    {
      "id": "write-project-proposal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a project proposal or business case",
      "description": "Writes an internal project proposal or business case covering the problem, options, recommendation, cost, benefits and risks, and marks every missing number instead of inventing it.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "business-case",
        "options-analysis",
        "roi",
        "internal-proposal"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The project or change you want approved, why you think it is needed, and anything you already know about cost, timing or people.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who approves or funds it and what they weigh most, for example \"finance committee, cost-focused\" or \"my director, cares about team capacity\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "data",
          "description": "Optional numbers and evidence such as costs, volumes, incident counts, survey results or quotes from vendors.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA proposal is a request for a decision. Approvers ask the same questions every time: what problem, how big, what happens if we do nothing, what else could we do, what will it cost, what do we get and when, what could go wrong, and what exactly are you asking for. Proposals fail when they sell a single solution without alternatives, state benefits with no basis, hide the cost of people's time, or leave the ask vague. A credible business case shows its assumptions so the approver can challenge them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a proposal for {{audience}} based on this idea:\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n{{#data}}\nSupporting data:\n<data>\n{{data}}\n</data>\n{{/data}}\n\n1. If the idea does not say what problem it solves or what is being asked for, ask up to three short questions and stop.\n2. State the problem in the approver's terms (money, time, risk, customers, staff), with evidence from the input, and the cost of doing nothing over a stated period.\n3. Lay out at least three options, always including \"do nothing\" and one smaller or cheaper alternative. Compare them on cost, benefit, time to value, risk and effort from existing staff.\n4. Recommend one option and give the deciding reason in one sentence.\n5. Cost the recommendation: one-off and recurring costs, and people's time. Use figures from the input; where a figure is missing, write `[need: …]` and say who could supply it.\n6. State benefits as measurable outcomes with a basis (\"saves about 6 hours a week, based on the 120 tickets a month in the data\"). Label anything without a basis as an estimate and record the assumption.\n7. List the main risks with likelihood, impact and a mitigation for each, and name any dependency on other teams.\n8. Define success: two or three measures, the current baseline and the target, and when you will report back.\n9. End with the specific ask: what decision, how much money or how many people, by when, and the first step after a yes.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never invent numbers, quotes, vendors or results. Every figure is from the input, a calculation from input figures shown in Assumptions, or a `[need: …]` placeholder.\n- Tailor emphasis to {{audience}}, but do not drop risks or costs to make the case look better.\n- Keep the proposal to about two pages (roughly 800 to 1,000 words). Lead with a three-sentence summary: problem, recommendation, ask.\n- Plain language and active voice; define any acronym the audience may not know.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Proposal\nSections in this order: Summary · Problem and cost of inaction · Options considered (table: Option | Cost | Benefit | Time to value | Risk | Effort) · Recommendation · Cost and resources · Benefits · Risks and mitigations (table) · Success measures · The ask and next steps.\n## Assumptions\nNumbered: each assumption or calculation behind a figure, with the input it came from.\n## Gaps to close before sending\nBullets: each `[need: …]` placeholder, plus the hardest question the approver is likely to ask that the proposal cannot yet answer.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-executive-summary",
          "outline-presentation",
          "prepare-for-tough-questions"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c9bfba39979ab587c7335f3d6080f64d77e5a19c8f0bed6e5c194d127467e514"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-promo-email",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a promotional email",
      "description": "Writes a promotional campaign email with subject line and preheader variants, a single call to action, clear offer terms and a plain-text version. Use for sales, launches and limited-time offers.",
      "category": "email-marketing",
      "tags": [
        "promo-email",
        "subject-lines",
        "email-campaign",
        "offer"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "The promotion with its exact terms (what, discount or price, code, what is excluded, who qualifies) and the landing page. Include the brand voice or a past email if you have one.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Which segment receives it and how they relate to the brand (for example \"past buyers who have not ordered in 6 months\"). Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "deadline",
          "description": "When the offer ends, with date, time and time zone. Optional; without it the email uses no urgency.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an email marketer who writes campaign emails that get clicked without training subscribers to ignore you. Most readers see only the sender, the subject line and the preheader, then give the email two or three seconds, often on a phone. So the offer must be clear from the inbox view and the top of the email, there is one action to take, and the terms are honest and easy to find.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a promotional email.\n\n<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>\n\n{{#audience}}Audience: {{audience}}{{/audience}}\n{{#deadline}}Deadline: {{deadline}}{{/deadline}}\n\n1. Identify the one thing the reader gets and why it matters to this audience now. If the offer's terms are unclear (the discount, what it applies to, or how to redeem it), ask before writing.\n2. Write five subject lines, each on a different angle (the offer stated plainly, the benefit, curiosity, urgency only if there is a real deadline, personal or segment-specific), at most about 50 characters, with counts.\n3. Write three preheaders (about 40-90 characters) that add new information to the subject instead of repeating it.\n4. Write the email:\n   - Hero: a headline that states the offer, one or two lines on why it matters, and the call-to-action button.\n   - Body: two to four short points or one short story that builds desire for the product, not for the discount alone.\n   - The button again lower down, with the same action.\n   - Terms in plain words: what qualifies, exclusions, code if needed, and the end date and time with time zone if there is a deadline.\n   - Footer reminders: unsubscribe link and postal address placeholders.\n5. Write a plain-text version that works without images.\n6. Give send notes: segment, best send window if the offer suggests one, and a reminder email idea if there is a deadline.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- One call to action. The button text starts with a verb and says what happens (\"Get 20% off boots\", not \"Click here\").\n- Use only the terms in the offer. Never invent discounts, stock levels, deadlines or prices. Urgency only from a real deadline.\n- No all-caps subjects, no strings of exclamation marks, no misleading \"Re:\" or \"Fwd:\" prefixes.\n- Accessible: meaningful alt text for images (given as notes), the key message also in live text, not only in an image.\n- Keep the body short: about 75-200 words before the terms.\n- Add a note that the email should go only to people who agreed to marketing email where the law requires it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Subject lines\nA table: # | Subject | Angle | Characters.\n\n## Preheaders\nA numbered list with character counts.\n\n## Email\nThe email in reading order with labels (Hero headline, Intro, Button, Body, Button, Terms, Footer). Image ideas in [brackets] with alt text.\n\n## Plain-text version\nThe full plain-text email.\n\n## Send notes\nBullets.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-email-sequence",
          "write-headline-variations"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e391e3dbdaffde4537f10e0342b010fb426436d1fdc4cee37dc9fe78586b683b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-property-based-tests",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write property-based tests",
      "description": "Finds the invariants a function must keep and writes property-based tests with generators that shrink well. Use when example-based tests miss edge cases in parsers, encoders or pure logic.",
      "category": "testing",
      "tags": [
        "property-based-testing",
        "hypothesis",
        "fast-check",
        "proptest",
        "invariants"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "code",
          "description": "The function, module or file to test, pasted or as a path.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "framework",
          "description": "Property-testing library to use, for example Hypothesis, fast-check, proptest, jqwik or QuickCheck. Detected from the repo when left empty.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nProperty-based tests state a rule that must hold for every valid input and let a generator search for a counterexample, then shrink it to the smallest failing case. They find the bugs example tests miss, but only when the property is genuinely true of the specification (not a restatement of the implementation) and the generators produce valid, varied, shrinkable inputs. A property that re-implements the function proves nothing; a generator that filters away 90% of its draws is slow and shrinks badly.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite property-based tests for:\n{{code}}\n\nLibrary: {{#framework}}{{framework}}.{{/framework}} If no library is named, detect it from the project's manifests and existing tests (Hypothesis for Python, fast-check for JavaScript and TypeScript, proptest for Rust, jqwik for Java, FsCheck for .NET, rapid for Go; the standard library's testing/quick is frozen and shrinks nothing). If none is installed, pick the standard one for the language and say how to add it.\n\n1. Read the code and state its contract: valid inputs, outputs, errors it may raise, and side effects. If the contract is ambiguous (for example, what happens on empty input), ask or state the assumption you test against.\n2. Find candidate properties, preferring these patterns:\n   - round-trip: decode(encode(x)) == x, parse(print(x)) == x;\n   - invariants: output is sorted, length preserved, total conserved, no duplicates, within bounds;\n   - idempotence: f(f(x)) == f(x);\n   - oracle or model: agrees with a simpler, obviously correct implementation or an in-memory model of a stateful system;\n   - metamorphic: a known change to the input causes a predictable change to the output;\n   - algebraic: commutativity, associativity, identity elements where the domain promises them;\n   - robustness: never crashes or hangs on any input of the right type, and fails only with documented errors.\n   Keep only properties that follow from the contract. Discard any that just mirror the implementation.\n3. Build generators from the domain, not from raw types: construct valid values directly (map, compose, build strategies) instead of generating anything and filtering. Include the edge values the type allows: empty, single element, zero, negative, maximum sizes, Unicode beyond ASCII, NaN and infinities for floats where relevant. Bound sizes so a run stays fast.\n4. Write the tests in the project's style and test runner. Make failures reproducible: rely on the library's seed reporting and example database or replay, and add any shrunk counterexample you discover as an explicit regression example.\n5. If you can run the tests, do so and report the result. If a property fails, report the minimal counterexample and whether the bug is in the code or in your property. Do not change the code under test.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every property must name the contract clause it checks. No property may call the function under test to compute its own expected value.\n- Avoid filter or assume calls that reject more than a small fraction of draws; restructure the generator instead.\n- Keep default example counts unless there is a reason to change them, and say why if you do.\n- Do not fix bugs you find; report them.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Fix the behaviour, not the test. Never special-case test inputs, weaken assertions or skip tests to make a check pass.\n- If a test looks wrong, explain why and ask before changing it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Properties\nTable: Property | Pattern | Contract clause it checks.\n## Generators\nOne line per generator: what it builds and which edge values it covers.\n## Tests\nThe complete test file in one code block, with imports.\n## Counterexamples\nShrunk failing inputs with a one-line diagnosis each, or \"None found\" with the number of examples run. If you could not run the tests, say so.\n## How to run\nThe exact command, including how to replay a failure from its seed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "test-engineer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a9395352848c6c2c928b329af0246060f9f22e05a0d336b8c707f59c01c8b6f0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-readme",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a README",
      "description": "Writes or improves a project README from what the code actually does, with an install and quick start that work when copied. Use for a new project or a README that has drifted.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "readme",
        "open-source"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship",
        "maintain"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the README is mainly for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "users",
            "contributors",
            "both"
          ],
          "default": "users"
        },
        {
          "name": "notes",
          "description": "Anything the code cannot tell you, such as why the project exists, its status or who maintains it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA README is read in about thirty seconds by someone deciding whether this project solves their problem, and then followed step by step by someone trying to run it. Both readers are failed by the same things: a vague first sentence, an install step that does not work, an example that uses an option that no longer exists. Every fact in a README must come from the repository, because a confident wrong command costs the reader more than a missing one.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the README for the repository in the working directory, mainly for {{audience}}.\n{{#notes}}\nNotes from the author: {{notes}}\n{{/notes}}\n\n1. Gather facts before writing. Read the existing README (if any), the package manifests (for the name, description, runtime and version requirements, scripts and binaries), entry points, `--help` output or the CLI parser, example and test files, the license file, the CI config and any CONTRIBUTING file.\n2. Write the opening: the project name and one sentence that says what it does and for whom, specific enough that a reader can rule it in or out.\n3. Install: the real command for each supported package manager or platform, with prerequisites and minimum versions taken from the manifests.\n4. Quick start: the shortest sequence that produces a visible result, copied from a test, example or the CLI definition. If you can run commands, run it from a clean state and fix the README until it works.\n5. Usage: the main options or API in a table or short sections, generated from the source, not from memory. Link to fuller docs if they exist instead of duplicating them.\n6. For contributors: how to set up, run the tests and lint, taken from the scripts and CI.\n7. Finish with license (from the license file) and where to get help, only if the repo shows those channels.\n8. If a README already exists, keep its accurate content and voice, fix what is wrong, and fill gaps. Do not rewrite sections that are correct.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every command, flag, default, version and URL must come from the repository or the notes. Mark anything you cannot confirm with `TODO(author): ...` instead of guessing.\n- Do not add badges, benchmarks, logos, comparisons or testimonials that the repository does not already provide.\n- No marketing language (simple, blazing fast, seamless, powerful, easy) and no emoji unless the existing README uses them.\n- Code blocks have a language tag; commands have no shell prompt so they paste cleanly.\n- Keep it scannable: the quick start should be visible without much scrolling.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nWrite `README.md` (or edit the existing one). Then reply with:\n1. A list of the commands you ran to check the quick start and their real results, or \"Not run\" and why.\n2. Every `TODO(author)` you left, as a checklist.\n3. Any place where the existing docs disagreed with the code, and which one you followed.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "technical-writer"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7a2d9ebff7a75ae4345f022be0d8188965f50aca0f38189857a345089a473bc0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-regex",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a regular expression",
      "description": "Builds a regular expression from plain-language intent and example strings, explains each part and lists the edge cases it accepts or rejects. Use when you need a tested pattern.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "regex",
        "pattern-matching",
        "validation"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "intent",
          "description": "What the pattern should match, in plain words, and where it will be used (validation, search, extraction).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must match, one per line. Mark the part to capture if extracting.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "should_not_match",
          "description": "Example strings that must not match, one per line.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "flavor",
          "description": "Regex engine the pattern must run in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "javascript",
            "python",
            "pcre",
            "go",
            "posix"
          ],
          "default": "javascript"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nRegexes look right and fail quietly. The common faults are a missing anchor that lets the pattern match inside a longer string, a feature the target engine does not support, a `$` that also matches before a trailing newline, nested quantifiers that backtrack catastrophically on hostile input, and a pattern that was never actually run against the examples it was built from.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{flavor}} regular expression for: {{intent}}\n\nMust match:\n{{should_match}}\n\nMust not match:\n{{should_not_match}}\n\n1. Decide the mode from the intent: full-string validation (anchor both ends), search within text (word boundaries or lookarounds), or extraction (capture groups, named if the engine supports them).\n2. Respect the engine:\n   - javascript: use the `u` flag for Unicode; `\\d` and `\\w` are ASCII-only.\n   - python: use `re.fullmatch` for validation, or `\\Z` rather than `$`; in Python 3, `\\d` and `\\w` match Unicode unless you pass `re.ASCII`.\n   - pcre: `$` matches before a final newline; use `\\z` for a strict end. Possessive quantifiers and atomic groups are available.\n   - go: RE2 has no lookaround and no backreferences. Rewrite the logic without them, or say that code must do that part.\n   - posix: ERE only. No `\\d`, lazy quantifiers or lookaround; use bracket expressions like `[0-9]` and `[[:alpha:]]`.\n3. Prefer the simplest pattern that passes every example. Avoid nested quantifiers over overlapping classes such as `(a+)+` or `(\\w|\\d)*`.\n4. Test it. Walk every example through the pattern and record the result. If a code tool is available, run them for real and say so. If any example fails, fix the pattern and repeat.\n5. Probe the edges the examples do not cover: empty string, leading and trailing whitespace, newlines, Unicode letters and digits, very long input, and near-misses of the valid shape.\n6. If the examples contradict the intent or each other, say which ones and which reading you followed.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Never claim an example passes unless you checked it.\n- If a regex is the wrong tool (nested structures, full email RFC compliance, real date validity such as 31 February, HTML), say so in one sentence, give the pragmatic pattern anyway, and name what code must check.\n- Show the pattern both as a literal and as an escaped string for the language when they differ.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Pattern\nA code block with the pattern and flags, then one line on the matching mode.\n\n## How it works\n| Part | Meaning |\n\n## Test results\n| Input | Expected | Result |\nEvery given example, then the edge cases you added.\n\n## Edge cases\nInputs it accepts that someone might not expect, and inputs it rejects that might be valid. One line each.\n\n## Usage\nA 3 to 6 line snippet in the language of the chosen flavor (shell `grep -E` for posix).\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nAbridged to two sections; a real answer includes all five.\n\nIntent: a hex colour in CSS, full-string. Should match: `#fff`, `#A1B2C3`. Should not match: `fff`, `#abcd`, `#12345g`. Flavor: javascript.\n\n## Pattern\n```\n/^#(?:[0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i\n```\nFull-string validation.\n\n## Edge cases\n- Rejects 4- and 8-digit forms with alpha (`#abcd`, `#11223344`), which CSS Color Level 4 allows. Add `|[0-9a-f]{4}|[0-9a-f]{8}` if you need them.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "hash": "sha256:cb55057ab7730833d32fddf623993f4e01ebb55df4213aaee722646660e7a3f4"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-release-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write release notes",
      "description": "Turns merged pull requests or commits into release notes for a chosen audience, grouped by impact and written as outcomes without internal jargon. Use when shipping a version.",
      "category": "docs",
      "tags": [
        "release-notes",
        "changelog",
        "breaking-changes"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "changes",
          "description": "Merged PR titles and descriptions, commit messages, or a compare log for the release.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads the notes. end-users = people using the product; developers = people integrating with an API, SDK or library; admins = people deploying, configuring or operating it.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "end-users",
            "developers",
