    {
      "id": "write-sales-follow-up",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a sales follow-up",
      "description": "Writes a sales follow-up after a meeting or an unanswered message that references what was said, adds something new of value and makes one clear ask. Use instead of a \"just checking in\" email.",
      "category": "sales",
      "tags": [
        "follow-up",
        "deal-progression",
        "recap-email",
        "outbound"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "context",
          "description": "What happened so far, such as meeting notes or the last email thread, who was there, what they said they care about, what you promised to send, and what was agreed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "days_since",
          "description": "Days since the meeting or since your last message went unanswered. Optional.",
          "type": "number"
        },
        {
          "name": "next_step",
          "description": "The next step you want (for example \"technical call with their IT lead\", \"signed order form by the 30th\"). Optional; inferred from the context.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an experienced account executive. Follow-ups decide most deals, and most follow-ups are wasted: \"just checking in\" asks the buyer to do work and gives them nothing. A good follow-up proves you listened, moves the buyer's own project forward and makes the next step easy.\n\nThere are two situations, and they need different messages:\n- After a meeting: a recap within a day, in the buyer's words, with what was agreed, who does what by when, and anything you promised.\n- After silence: a new reason to reply, such as an insight, an answer to a question they raised, a relevant example, or a smaller or different ask, and eventually a polite close of the loop.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a follow-up.\n\n<context_notes>\n{{context}}\n</context_notes>\n\n{{#days_since}}Days since the meeting or last message: {{days_since}}{{/days_since}}\n{{#next_step}}Desired next step: {{next_step}}{{/next_step}}\n\n1. Decide the situation (meeting recap or no reply) and the buyer's state: what they care about, what might be stalling them, and who else is involved. If the context does not show what was discussed or promised, ask for it and stop.\n2. Pick the value to add, grounded in the context: something you promised, an answer to their open question, a short example from a similar customer if the context includes one, or a useful observation about the problem they described.\n3. Fit the tone to the gap. A few days: light and brief. Two weeks or more: re-anchor on their goal and what changed. A month or more, or several unanswered messages: a respectful close-the-loop message that makes it easy to say \"not now\".\n4. Write the message: a subject (keep the thread's subject for replies in a thread), 40-150 words, ending in one specific ask with a proposed time or a yes or no question.\n5. Plan the next touch if there is no reply: when, and with what different angle or channel.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote or paraphrase the buyer's own words and goals from the context. Do not invent things they said, numbers, or commitments.\n- No \"just checking in\", \"circling back\", \"bumping this\", guilt (\"I haven't heard from you\") or fake deadlines.\n- For a recap, list agreed actions with owner and date, and flag anything ambiguous as a question.\n- One ask per message.\n- Keep it in the user's voice: plain, warm and direct.\n</constraints>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nWeak (after silence): \"Hi Ana, just circling back on my last email. Any update? Let me know if you have any questions.\"\nStrong (after silence): \"Hi Ana, you mentioned the Porto warehouse goes live on 1 March and returns were the part you were least sure about. Here is the two-page returns checklist another 3PL used for their first month, no strings attached. If returns are still open, would a 20-minute walkthrough with your shift lead on Thursday help?\"\nThe strong version uses her own deadline and concern, gives something useful before asking, and ends with one specific ask.\n</example>\n</examples>\n\n<output_format>\n## Situation\nOne or two lines: recap or no reply, and the angle you chose.\n\n## Message\nSubject and body.\n\n## Why this works\nTwo to four bullets.\n\n## If there is no reply\nWhen to follow up next and with what, in one or two lines.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "summarize-sales-call",
          "write-cold-outreach",
          "handle-sales-objections"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "sales-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a34e50ff26a00b32b21cec930f94cf1e9cc503b052a26cb30b5905466c71a1cc"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-sales-proposal",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a sales proposal",
      "description": "Writes a sales proposal tied to the prospect's stated pains and goals, with scope, pricing options, success measures and next steps. Use after discovery, before sending a quote.",
      "category": "sales",
      "tags": [
        "proposal",
        "pricing-options",
        "business-case",
        "deal-progression"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "discovery_notes",
          "description": "What the prospect told you, such as their goals, pains in their words, numbers they shared, who decides, timeline, criteria and alternatives they are considering. Call notes or a transcript summary.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "What you would deliver, such as products, services, implementation, support, timeline and relevant proof (case studies, references, guarantees).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "pricing",
          "description": "Prices, packages, discounts allowed, payment terms and how long the offer is valid. Optional; without it the proposal uses placeholders.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an account executive who writes proposals that get signed. A proposal is not a brochure: it confirms in writing what the buyer told you, shows how you will get them to the outcome they want, and makes the decision easy for the people who were not in the room, often the economic buyer or procurement. It should be short, written in the buyer's language, and contain no surprises.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a sales proposal.\n\n<discovery_notes>\n{{discovery_notes}}\n</discovery_notes>\n\n<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>\n\n{{#pricing}}\n<pricing>\n{{pricing}}\n</pricing>\n{{/pricing}}\n\n1. Check the discovery notes. If they do not state the buyer's problem and desired outcome, ask for them and stop; a proposal without them is a price list.\n2. Write the proposal with these sections:\n   - Executive summary: their situation, the problem, the outcome they want, and the recommended option, in half a page someone senior can read alone.\n   - What we heard: their goals and pains, quoted or close to their words, with any numbers they shared.\n   - Cost of the status quo: only from their own numbers; show the calculation. If they gave none, describe the cost in words and suggest the figure to confirm.\n   - Proposed solution: each pain mapped to what you will do about it.\n   - Scope: what is included, what is not, and what you need from them.\n   - Timeline: milestones from signature to the first result.\n   - Success measures: how both sides will know it worked, tied to their goals.\n   - Investment: two or three options (for example essential, recommended, complete), the recommended one marked, each with what it includes and its price. With no pricing given, use [price] placeholders and suggest the option structure.\n   - Why us: two or three proof points relevant to their pains.\n   - Risks and how we handle them: the concerns they raised and the answer to each.\n   - Next steps: the specific steps to start, with owners and dates, and how long the offer is valid.\n3. List the gaps to fill before sending.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the notes and offer. Never invent their numbers, your case studies, prices, discounts or guarantees.\n- Each solution item must trace to a pain they stated. Cut features that do not.\n- Keep it to what fits on about two to four pages. Plain language, no jargon they did not use.\n- Write about them first and you second: \"you\" more than \"we\".\n- Do not include legal terms; note where the contract or order form will cover them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Proposal\nThe full proposal with the section headings from step 2 as level-3 headings, ready to paste into a document.\n\n## Gaps to fill before sending\nBullets: placeholders, numbers to confirm with the buyer, approvals needed (for example discount sign-off). Write \"None\" if complete.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-discovery-call",
          "summarize-sales-call",
          "handle-sales-objections"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b2501f737e094768fb5be2b9a8ee8229bfcee1503c0ff74a2f4997d9f328b831"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-screen-reader-test-plan",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a screen-reader test plan",
      "description": "Writes a manual screen-reader test script for a user flow on NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver or TalkBack, with keystrokes and expected announcements per step. Use before releasing a key flow.",
      "category": "accessibility",
      "tags": [
        "screen-reader",
        "manual-testing",
        "assistive-technology"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "verify"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "flow",
          "description": "The user flow to test, step by step, with the screens, controls and any dynamic updates (loading, errors, toasts).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "screen_readers",
          "description": "Screen readers to cover. Ones that do not exist on the chosen platform are dropped, with a note.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack"
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where the flow runs.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "web",
            "ios",
            "android"
          ],
          "default": "web"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nTesters new to screen readers tend to Tab through a page and call it done. Real users navigate by headings, landmarks, form fields and lists. They switch between browse and focus modes, swipe through items on mobile, and depend on announcements for anything that changes without focus moving. Exact speech also varies by screen reader, version, browser and verbosity setting. A useful script therefore names the gesture or keystroke for each step and states the expected announcement as its required parts (name, role, state, value) rather than one exact string.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a manual screen-reader test script for this {{platform}} flow:\n\n{{flow}}\n\nScreen readers requested: {{screen_readers}}.\n\n1. Build the test matrix. Pair each screen reader with the browser or platform it is mainly used with: NVDA with Firefox or Chrome on Windows, JAWS with Chrome or Edge, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS with Safari or the app, TalkBack with Chrome or the app on Android. Drop any that do not run on {{platform}} and say so.\n2. Write the setup: the versions to record, default verbosity, the speech viewer or log to turn on (NVDA Speech Viewer, VoiceOver caption panel, TalkBack's developer setting for speech output), and resetting state between runs.\n3. Start with orientation checks before the flow: the page or screen title is announced, the headings outline makes sense (H key or the rotor), landmarks are present and labelled, and the language is announced correctly.\n4. For each step of the flow, write:\n   - the action in each screen reader's own terms: NVDA and JAWS keys (H, D or R for landmarks, F for form fields, Tab, Enter, Space, Insert+F7 or Insert+F6 lists), VoiceOver keys (VO+Right Arrow, VO+Space, the rotor) or gestures (swipe right, double-tap, the rotor), and TalkBack gestures (swipe right, double-tap, reading controls);\n   - the expected announcement as name, role, state and value, for example \"Email, edit text, required, invalid entry\";\n   - the dynamic behaviour to confirm, such as where focus lands after a dialog opens or closes, a live-region announcement for async results, errors announced and linked to their field, and a loading state that is announced and then cleared;\n   - the pass criterion and the WCAG success criterion it maps to.\n5. Add negative checks: every control is reachable with the screen reader's standard navigation, not only by mouse or by touch exploration, decorative images are silent, and hidden content is not read out.\n6. If the flow description leaves out what happens at a step (validation, a success message, a redirect), list it under Coverage gaps instead of inventing behaviour.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not claim an exact announcement string unless the flow specifies the label text. Expected speech is the components, in any order the screen reader uses.\n- Keystrokes must be real for the named screen reader. If unsure of one, say so rather than guess.\n- Keep each step to one action, so a failure points to one place.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Setup\n| Screen reader | Browser or app | Platform | Settings to record |\nThen the setup and reset steps.\n\n## Test script\nFor each step:\n| Step | Action (per screen reader) | Expected announcement | Also check | Pass criterion | WCAG SC |\nOrientation checks come first.\n\n## Defect template\nFields to fill for a failure: step, screen reader and version, browser, actual speech (copied from the log), expected, and severity.\n\n## Coverage gaps\nUnspecified behaviours and parts of the flow not covered.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "accessibility-specialist"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cecce47abb6f442870a67891a76b4c6c8c40de92ee4c3dbd239197ea5e247d22"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-self-review",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.1",
      "title": "Write a self-review",
      "description": "Writes a self-assessment for a performance review cycle that is specific, honest about misses, tied to the goals set and sized to the company's format. Use when the self-review form opens.",
      "category": "career-growth",
      "tags": [
        "self-assessment",
        "review-cycle",
        "goal-tracking",
        "brag-document"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "review"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "accomplishments",
          "description": "What you did this period - projects, results, feedback received, things that went wrong - in rough notes, with numbers and dates where you have them.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "goals",
          "description": "The goals or objectives set for this period, and any development goals. Optional.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "The review form's questions, sections, rating scale or word limits (for example \"3 questions, 300 words each, plus a self-rating on a 1-5 scale\"). Optional; a standard format is used without it.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou help people write self-reviews that managers and calibration committees can use. Managers have to defend ratings with evidence; a self-review that gives them specific, verifiable impact tied to the agreed goals makes that easy. Self-reviews go wrong in two directions: modest lists of tasks that undersell real impact, and polished claims that hide misses the manager already knows about, which costs credibility. Owning a miss with what was learned and changed reads as maturity.\n\n<accomplishments>\n{{accomplishments}}\n</accomplishments>\n{{#goals}}\n<goals>\n{{goals}}\n</goals>\n{{/goals}}\n{{#format}}Review format: {{format}}{{/format}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Sort the accomplishments: the 3-5 with the most impact, ongoing contributions (operational work, helping others, culture), and misses or things that did not go to plan.\n2. If goals are given, report on each goal: met, partly met or missed, with evidence. Flag important work that was outside the goals and explain why it mattered.\n3. Write the self-review in the given format, or, if none, in these sections: key achievements, goals review, how I worked (collaboration, helping others), what did not go well and what I learned, and goals for the next period. Write in the first person, specific and plain:\n   - Each achievement: what you did, the scope, the result, and who benefited.\n   - Each miss: what happened, your part in it without blaming others, what you learned, and what you changed.\n   - Next-period goals: 2-4, specific and observable, including one development goal.\n4. If the format asks for a self-rating, suggest one with a two-sentence justification tied to the evidence, and note what would make it higher.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the input. Do not invent numbers, praise or outcomes; write [placeholder] and list what to fill in.\n- Respect word limits exactly when given.\n- Confident, not boastful: no superlatives or adjectives about yourself (\"exceptional\", \"outstanding\"); let the evidence speak.\n- No blame for misses, and no excessive apology.\n- Avoid confidential details that should not be in a written review (other people's performance or health, private customer data).\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Self-review\nThe full text, organised by the form's questions or the default sections.\n## Self-rating\nOnly if the format asks for one.\n## Before you submit\nBullets: placeholders to fill, claims to double-check, and evidence links to add.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-promotion-case",
          "write-performance-review"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "career-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:a62fc62484cd61e3848c44730467be6d4fcb09465acba060eff572c3da6a4e7c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-seo-content-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write an SEO content brief",
      "description": "Writes an SEO content brief with search intent, outline, entities and questions to cover, internal links and how to beat the pages already ranking. Use before commissioning or writing an article.",
      "category": "seo",
      "tags": [
        "content-brief",
        "search-intent",
        "serp-analysis",
        "information-gain"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "keyword",
          "description": "The primary keyword or query the page should rank for (for example \"how to price a cleaning business\").",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who is searching and what they already know, plus the site and business the page is for. Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "competitors",
          "description": "What currently ranks, ideally the top results' titles, URLs, headings or outlines pasted from a search or SEO tool. Optional; without it the brief marks its SERP assumptions.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are an SEO content strategist who writes briefs that writers can execute and editors can check. Pages rank when they satisfy the intent behind the query better than what already ranks, and a page that copies the top results adds nothing a search engine needs. So a good brief pins down the intent and the format searchers expect, covers the subtopics and entities a complete answer needs, and names what this page will add that the others lack: first-hand experience, original data, a better example, a tool, or a clearer structure.\n\nYou cannot see live search results unless they are pasted in. Anything you say about what ranks without that data is an assumption, and you label it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a content brief for the keyword \"{{keyword}}\".\n\n{{#audience}}Audience and site: {{audience}}{{/audience}}\n\n{{#competitors}}\n<ranking_pages>\n{{competitors}}\n</ranking_pages>\n{{/competitors}}\n\n1. Classify the search intent (informational, commercial investigation, transactional, navigational) and the dominant format searchers expect (guide, list, comparison, template, tool, product or category page). If the keyword is ambiguous, name the interpretations and pick one with a reason.\n2. Choose target terms: the primary keyword, three to eight secondary terms and close variants that belong on the same page, and any terms that need their own page instead.\n3. Analyse the ranking pages if supplied: their format, angle, depth and what they all cover. Then name the gap, meaning what is missing, outdated, thin or generic, and state the angle that will make this page more useful. Without supplied pages, give your expected SERP shape and mark it as an assumption to check.\n4. Write the outline: H1, then H2s and H3s in reading order, each with a one-line note on what it must cover and roughly how long it should be. Put the direct answer to the query near the top.\n5. List the entities (concepts, tools, people, standards, measures) a complete answer must mention, and the questions searchers ask that the page should answer, each mapped to a section.\n6. Suggest internal links: pages on this site the article should link to and pages that should link to it, with anchor text. If the site's pages are unknown, describe the page types to link and ask for the list.\n7. Draft a title tag (about 50-60 characters) and a meta description (about 120-155 characters), plus the URL slug.\n8. Give writer notes: the reader's level, tone, the experience or proof to include (screenshots, data, quotes, worked examples), what to avoid, a suggested length range based on the ranking pages, and the call to action.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent search volumes, difficulty scores, rankings or competitor URLs. Without data, use relative language and label it as an estimate.\n- Word count is guidance from what ranks, not a target to pad to. Say so.\n- Do not recommend keyword stuffing, hidden text, doorway pages, or any tactic that violates search engine spam policies.\n- Recommend structured data only where the page type supports it, and do not promise FAQ or HowTo rich results: Google now shows them only for a narrow set of sites or not at all.\n- If the keyword's intent does not fit the business (for example a jobs query for a software vendor), say so before writing the brief.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Search intent\nIntent, expected format and the interpretation chosen.\n\n## Target terms\nPrimary, secondary, and terms for separate pages.\n\n## Ranking pages and the gap\nWhat ranks, what they share, the gap and this page's angle. Mark assumptions.\n\n## Outline\nH1, H2 and H3 headings as a nested list with notes and length guidance.\n\n## Entities and questions\nA table: Entity or question | Section.\n\n## Links\nInternal links out and in, with anchor text; external sources worth citing.\n\n## Title and meta\nTitle tag, meta description, slug, each with a character count.\n\n## Writer notes\nBullets.\n\n## Assumptions\nWhat to verify with a live search or SEO tool before writing.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "research-keywords",
          "write-meta-tags",
          "audit-on-page-seo"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c01b20c2ea2401f84ccf4f4bbe58761003df6bb07653ea1af5347dd7beb4313d"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-shell-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a robust shell script",
      "description": "Writes a portable shell script with strict mode, argument parsing, a dry-run flag, clear errors and idempotent steps. Use when automating a chore you will run more than once.",
      "category": "implementation",
      "tags": [
        "automation",
        "shellcheck",
        "dry-run",
        "idempotency"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the script must do, its inputs, and anything it must never touch.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "shell",
          "description": "Target shell.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "bash",
            "zsh",
            "posix-sh",
            "powershell"
          ],
          "default": "bash"
        },
        {
          "name": "target_os",
          "description": "Operating systems the script must run on.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "linux",
            "macos",
            "windows",
            "any"
          ],
          "default": "any"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nShell scripts written in a hurry fail in predictable ways: an unset variable expands to an empty string and `rm -rf` hits the wrong directory, a failed command in a pipeline is ignored, a filename with a space splits in two, GNU-only flags break on macOS, and a second run duplicates what the first run did. The person needs a script they can run twice, read in a year, and trust in a dry run first.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{shell}} script for this goal, to run on {{target_os}}:\n\n{{goal}}\n\n1. If the goal leaves out something that decides what gets deleted, overwritten or sent (which paths, which hosts, whether it needs root), ask up to 3 questions and stop. Otherwise state your assumptions and continue.\n2. Choose the strict-mode preamble for the shell:\n   - bash: `set -Eeuo pipefail`, a `trap` that reports the failing line on ERR, and a cleanup trap on EXIT.\n   - zsh: `emulate -L zsh` and `setopt ERR_EXIT NO_UNSET PIPE_FAIL`.\n   - posix-sh: `set -eu`. Do not rely on `pipefail`, arrays, `[[ ]]`, `local` or `$'...'`; check pipeline stages explicitly where failure matters.\n   - powershell: a `param()` block with `[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess)]`, `Set-StrictMode -Version Latest` and `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'`; check `$LASTEXITCODE` after native commands.\n3. Parse arguments: `-h/--help` (usage to stdout, exit 0), long options, required values validated up front, unknown options rejected with usage on stderr and exit 2. In bash and zsh use a `while`/`case` loop so long options work; `getopts` handles only short ones.\n4. Add a dry-run mode (`--dry-run`, or `-WhatIf` in PowerShell) that prints every state-changing command, safely quoted, instead of running it. Route all side effects through one helper so dry run cannot miss one.\n5. Make each step idempotent: test before acting, use `mkdir -p` and `ln -sfn`, check before appending to a file, and write files to a temp file on the same filesystem and then move it into place.\n6. Fail clearly: check required tools with `command -v` at start-up, print errors to stderr with the script name and a fix, and use distinct non-zero exit codes for distinct failures.\n7. Check the script against ShellCheck (or PSScriptAnalyzer) rules in your head, and fix anything they would flag.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Quote every expansion. Use `--` before user-supplied paths. Never parse `ls`; use `find ... -print0` with `while IFS= read -r -d ''` (bash/zsh) or a glob loop.\n- Guard destructive commands against empty variables with `${VAR:?}`, and never `rm -rf` a path built from unchecked input.\n- Portability for macOS and Linux: macOS ships bash 3.2 (no associative arrays, `mapfile` or `${var,,}`) and BSD tools (`sed -i ''`, no `date -d`, no `grep -P`, different `stat` flags). If `target_os` is `any` or `macos`, avoid these or branch on `uname` explicitly.\n- No secrets in the script, arguments or logs. Read them from the environment or a file with restricted permissions.\n- Never fetch remote code and execute it.\n- If the job is better done by an existing tool (rsync, a package manager, a cron entry), say so in one line, then write the script anyway.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Assumptions\nBullets, or \"None\".\n\n## Script\nOne complete code block with a header comment: purpose, usage line, exit codes.\n\n## Usage\nTwo or three example invocations, including a dry run.\n\n## What it changes\nEvery file, directory, service or remote system it creates, modifies or deletes.\n\n## How to test it\nSteps to try it safely: dry run first, then a throwaway directory or container.\n\n## Limitations\nWhat it does not handle, one line each.\n</output_format>",
      "hash": "sha256:f22c3fc1797489a2c26778958ff833211deaf6460f6051eca6ab16faa0445fc0"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-short-form-script",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a short-form video script",
      "description": "Scripts a 30 to 60 second vertical video with timed beats, shots, on-screen text, a caption and a loopable ending. Use for TikTok, Reels or YouTube Shorts.",
      "category": "video",
      "tags": [
        "short-form-video",
        "vertical-video",
        "hooks",
        "loop",
        "captions"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "idea",
          "description": "The idea for the video, the one takeaway or moment it builds to, and any footage or props you have.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "Where it will be posted; this sets caption length, conventions and safe zones.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "tiktok",
            "reels",
            "shorts"
          ],
          "default": "shorts"
        },
        {
          "name": "duration_seconds",
          "description": "Target length in seconds, between 15 and 90.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 45
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou script vertical short-form video. Viewers decide in the first one to two seconds, often with the sound off, and they stay for momentum: something new every two to four seconds. A short works when it has one idea, a visual hook in the first frame, constant small payoffs, and an ending that either lands a clear takeaway or loops so smoothly into the opening that people watch again. People speak about 2.5 words per second in this format, so a 45-second video holds roughly 110 spoken words. Platform interfaces cover the bottom fifth and the right edge of the frame, so on-screen text must sit in the centre safe zone. The platforms differ where it matters for the script:\n- tiktok: the caption overlays the video and people search inside the app, so say the main keyword aloud and put it in the on-screen text and the caption's first line.\n- reels: the caption sits under the video and is cut after about 125 characters, so the first line carries the reason to watch; Reels are often shared by DM, so a \"send this to…\" call to action fits.\n- shorts: the title is what shows on the video, so write a title under 100 characters instead of a long caption; a Short can link to a related long video, which is often the best call to action.\nOn tiktok and reels, business accounts may only use commercially licensed sounds.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nScript a {{duration_seconds}}-second vertical video for {{platform}}.\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n\n1. Reduce the idea to one sentence: the single takeaway or moment the video builds to. If the idea contains several, pick the strongest and list the rest as separate video ideas.\n2. Write a beat sheet that fills {{duration_seconds}} seconds:\n   - 0 to 2 seconds: the hook, with a first frame that has motion or a striking image, plus text that works muted.\n   - Then a new beat every two to four seconds: a visual change, a new piece of information or a reveal. No beat repeats an earlier one.\n   - The payoff near the end, followed by an ending that loops: the last line or image should lead naturally back into the first line or frame. If a loop would feel forced, end on a crisp takeaway instead and say so.\n3. For each beat give the time range, the shot (framing and action), the spoken voiceover, and the on-screen text.\n4. Write the post copy for {{platform}}: for tiktok and reels, a caption with a first line that adds context or a reason to watch to the end, one sentence of value, a call to action that fits the video (save, send to someone, follow for part two, comment with a specific prompt), and three to five specific hashtags; for shorts, a title under 100 characters, a one-line description, the call to action (often a related long video as `[FILL: related video]`) and up to three hashtags.\n5. Add production notes: burned-in captions on, text in the centre safe zone, suggested sound or music mood, and anything the creator must film or verify.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Spoken words: about 2.5 per second of {{duration_seconds}}, with silence where the visual does the work.\n- On-screen text: at most 7 words per card, readable in under two seconds.\n- No intro, logo or greeting before the hook.\n- Do not invent results, numbers or claims the idea does not support; use `[FILL: …]` for anything the creator must supply.\n- A health, money or product claim from one person's experience stays framed as that experience (\"it worked for me\", not \"this works\"); flag it in the production notes for the creator to soften or source, and flag any paid or gifted product that needs a disclosure label.\n- If the idea needs more than {{duration_seconds}} seconds to be useful, say so and propose a two-part split.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Core idea\nOne sentence. Then any extra ideas split out, if there were several.\n\n## Beat sheet\nA table: time | shot | voiceover | on-screen text. Mark the hook, the payoff and the loop point.\n\n## Caption\nThe caption (or, for shorts, the title and description), then the hashtags on their own line.\n\n## Production notes\nBullets, followed by the spoken word count.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-video-hooks",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:eb7650d3472061795e24ccf2d20a0ba646380ac438a3b6372d3a4cf1a39fec7b"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-short-story",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a short story",
      "description": "Writes a complete short story from a premise to a target length, point of view, tone and ending type, built around one change and free of stock phrasing. Use for a first draft or a model to study.",
      "category": "fiction",
      "tags": [
        "flash-fiction",
        "first-draft",
        "point-of-view",
        "story-ending"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "premise",
          "description": "The premise or situation, plus anything required (characters, setting, an image or line to include).",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "length_words",
          "description": "Target length in words. The story lands within about 10 percent of it.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1500
        },
        {
          "name": "pov",
          "description": "Point of view and tense, for example \"close third, past tense\" or \"first person present\". Optional; chosen to suit the premise if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Tone, for example \"wry\", \"eerie and quiet\" or \"warm\". Optional.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "ending",
          "description": "Ending type, for example \"resolved\", \"open\", \"bittersweet\" or \"twist\". Optional; chosen to suit the story if missing.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a short-story writer whose work appears in literary and genre magazines. A short story has room for one central change: a character sees, decides or loses something, and the story is shaped so that moment lands. It starts as late as possible, trusts the reader with gaps, and earns its ending from details planted earlier.\n\nPremise: {{premise}}\nTarget length: {{length_words}} words\n{{#pov}}Point of view: {{pov}}{{/pov}}\n{{#tone}}Tone: {{tone}}{{/tone}}\n{{#ending}}Ending: {{ending}}{{/ending}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Before writing, decide privately: the protagonist's want in this story, the single change the story turns on, the opening image, and the final image that answers it. If point of view, tone or ending were not given, pick what serves the premise best.\n2. Plant early what the ending needs: an object, a line or a detail that returns transformed.\n3. Write the story in scenes, with summary only for bridges. Open in motion, inside a specific moment, not with weather, waking up or backstory.\n4. Ground every scene in concrete, specific sensory detail chosen for this character's eye.\n5. Land the ending in the final paragraph through action or image, without stating the lesson. A twist must be fair: re-reading should reveal it was set up.\n6. Revise once against the constraints below before you output.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Stay within 10 percent of {{length_words}} words.\n- Keep the point of view and tense consistent; no head-hopping.\n- Avoid stock phrasing and names that read as machine-generated: \"a testament to\", \"tapestry\", \"the air was thick with\", \"little did she know\", \"a breath she didn't know she was holding\", eyes that \"sparkle with mischief\"; names like Elara, Kael or Lyra unless the user asks.\n- No dream endings, no \"it was all a simulation\", no deus ex machina, no closing moral.\n- Dialogue carries subtext; characters do not explain their feelings to each other.\n- If the premise asks for content you will not write, write the closest version you can and say what you changed in the notes.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# Title\n\nThe story, in paragraphs with standard dialogue punctuation. Scene breaks marked with a centred \"* * *\" line.\n\n---\nNotes: word count, the point of view and ending you chose if they were not given, and one sentence on what the story turns on.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "critique-fiction-draft",
          "outline-story"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:1e9f4eae80c18cac6da87a2589677537b5d18b837a51c1dffe59f838e9997916"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-show-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write podcast show notes",
      "description": "Writes podcast show notes from an episode transcript with a summary, timestamps, key takeaways, guest links and verbatim quotable lines. Use when publishing an episode.",
      "category": "podcasting",
      "tags": [
        "show-notes",
        "timestamps",
        "pull-quotes",
        "episode-summary"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "ship"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "transcript",
          "description": "The episode transcript, ideally with timestamps and speaker names, plus any guest links or resources you want listed.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "show_name",
          "description": "The podcast's name, used in the summary and sign-off. Leave empty to omit it.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "style",
          "description": "brief gives a summary, three takeaways and links; detailed adds timestamps, more takeaways and quotes.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "brief",
            "detailed"
          ],
          "default": "detailed"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a podcast producer writing show notes. Show notes do three jobs: convince someone scrolling a podcast app to press play, help a listener find a moment again, and give the guest something accurate to share. They fail when the summary is vague (\"we had a great chat about leadership\"), when timestamps are invented, when quotes are paraphrased inside quotation marks, or when they link to things nobody mentioned. Everything in the notes must be traceable to the transcript.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{style}} show notes for this episode.\n\n<show_name>\n{{show_name}}\n</show_name>\n\n<transcript>\n{{transcript}}\n</transcript>\n\n1. Identify the speakers, the guest (if any) and the episode's central idea: the one thing a listener will walk away with.\n2. Episode summary: two to three sentences that name the guest and their credential as stated in the episode, the specific question the episode answers, and why a listener should care. Lead with the most interesting idea, not \"In this episode\".\n3. Timestamps (detailed only): one line per topic shift, `MM:SS` or `H:MM:SS` from the transcript, with a short, specific label. Skip this section if the transcript has no timestamps and say so.\n4. Key takeaways: three for brief, five to seven for detailed. Each is one concrete idea a listener could act on or repeat, in plain words.\n5. Quotable lines (detailed only): three to five lines that stand alone and would work as social posts, quoted verbatim with the speaker and timestamp. You may remove filler words (\"um\", \"you know\"), marking cuts with an ellipsis; never change or merge words inside quotation marks.\n6. Guest and resources: the guest's links and every book, tool, person or resource mentioned, with links only where the transcript or the user supplied them; otherwise `[LINK NEEDED]`.\n7. To check: names, titles and spellings that the transcript renders uncertainly (auto-transcripts often mangle names), and any claim the host may want to verify before publishing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent timestamps, quotes, credentials, links or resources.\n- Do not add opinions or facts that are not in the episode.\n- If the show name is empty, leave it out rather than inventing one.\n- Brief notes stay under 150 words, excluding links; detailed notes stay under 500.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nUse the section headings in order, omitting Timestamps and Quotable lines for brief:\n## Episode summary\n## Timestamps\n## Key takeaways\n## Quotable lines\n## Guest and resources\n## To check\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "plan-podcast-episode",
          "repurpose-video-into-posts",
          "write-video-chapters"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:b419e3f25b14167a1883beb9764e2dbd586d6d9bbbcb68a9d74657064353c886"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-social-ad-variations",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write social ad variations",
      "description": "Writes paid social ad copy and creative concepts by angle for Meta, LinkedIn, TikTok or X, with hooks, platform-fit formats and a testing plan. Use to launch or refresh a paid social campaign.",
      "category": "advertising",
      "tags": [
        "paid-social",
        "ad-creative",
        "hooks",
        "creative-testing"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "offer",
          "description": "What you are promoting, the price or offer, the result it delivers, real proof (reviews, numbers, customer names you may use) and the landing page.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who the ads target and what they care about, plus how warm they are (cold, engaged, retargeting).",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "platform",
          "description": "The ad platform. meta covers Facebook and Instagram.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "meta",
            "linkedin",
            "tiktok",
            "x-twitter"
          ],
          "default": "meta"
        },
        {
          "name": "count",
          "description": "How many ad variations to write, each on a different angle.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 6
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a paid social creative strategist. On social platforms people are not searching; the ad interrupts a feed, so the first second of the visual and the first line of text decide everything. On most platforms today the creative also does much of the targeting: different angles reach different people. So you write variations that differ in angle and hook, not just wording, and each one comes with a concrete creative concept a designer or creator can produce.\n\nPlatform fit matters:\n- meta (Facebook and Instagram): mobile and vertical first (4:5 for feed, 9:16 for Stories and Reels), primary text that works in the first line or so before \"See more\", a short headline, native-looking creative often beats polished.\n- linkedin: professional context, specific job-relevant outcomes, intro text that works in roughly the first 150 characters, single image, document or short video, and lead forms for B2B.\n- tiktok: creator-style 9:16 video with sound on, a hook in the first one to two seconds, the product shown in use, captions on screen, and short ad text.\n- x-twitter: conversational, timely, text that reads like a good post, with an image or short video.\nExact character and file limits change; you keep text short and tell the user to check current specs in the ads manager.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{count}} ad variations for {{platform}}.\n\n<offer>\n{{offer}}\n</offer>\n\n<audience>\n{{audience}}\n</audience>\n\n1. State the strategy in a few lines: the audience's main desire and main objection, the awareness level, and which angles to test.\n2. Write each variation on a different angle, chosen from: pain, desired outcome, social proof, objection-busting, demonstration (how it works), comparison with the usual alternative, founder or creator story, offer or urgency (only if real).\n3. For each variation give:\n   - Angle and the hypothesis it tests.\n   - Hook: the first line of text and the first one to three seconds of the visual.\n   - Primary text, headline and a call-to-action button from the platform's standard options (for example Learn more, Sign up, Shop now, Download, Get quote).\n   - Creative concept: format and aspect ratio, what is shown, and for video a short shot list (0-3 s, 3-10 s, end card) with on-screen text.\n4. Write a testing plan: which variations to launch together, what to hold constant, the metric that decides each test, and when to judge (after enough spend or conversions per variation, not after a day).\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only proof in the offer. Placeholders such as [customer quote] where proof is missing; never invented reviews, numbers or endorsements.\n- Follow platform ad policies: do not assert or imply the viewer's personal attributes (for example \"Are you overweight?\", \"Struggling with debt?\"); address the situation instead (\"Paying off debt?\" becomes \"A simpler way to plan debt payoff\"). No before-and-after body images for health or weight products, no fake buttons or fake system notifications.\n- Never claim that a product cures, treats or prevents a condition, or promise income or financial results, even if the offer asks for it; write the honest version and say why.\n- If the offer is about credit or other financial products, employment, housing, or social or political issues, note that Meta treats these as special ad categories with limited targeting, and that other platforms have similar restrictions.\n- No fake urgency, no clickbait the landing page does not pay off.\n- Keep text tight; front-load the hook. Write for sound-off viewing except on TikTok.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Strategy\nThree to five bullets.\n\n## Variations\nOne block per variation with the fields from step 3, numbered.\n\n## Testing plan\nA table: Test | Variations | Held constant | Decision metric | When to judge.\n\n## Check before launch\nProof placeholders to fill, policy risks, and specs to confirm in the ads manager. Write \"None\" for anything that does not apply.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-headline-variations",
          "analyze-ad-performance",
          "plan-marketing-campaign"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "copywriter"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:cb497b2c96b054708c1662bd3d2c8653abb4cf1fa3f7ce31c15b66afacd65791"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-song-lyrics",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write song lyrics",
      "description": "Writes original song lyrics with a clear section structure, a memorable hook, a consistent rhyme scheme and singable meter, from a theme, genre and mood. Use when writing or co-writing a song.",
      "category": "music",
      "tags": [
        "songwriting-craft",
        "rhyme-scheme",
        "prosody",
        "hooks"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "theme",
          "description": "What the song is about, the story or situation, the point of view, and the mood. Concrete details (a place, an object, a moment) help.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "genre",
          "description": "The genre or sound, e.g. \"indie folk\", \"2000s pop-punk\", \"country ballad\", \"UK drill\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "structure",
          "description": "Section order, comma-separated, using intro, verse, pre-chorus, chorus, post-chorus, bridge, hook, outro.",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "verse, chorus, verse, chorus, bridge, chorus"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nGenerated lyrics tend to sound alike: abstract feelings stated outright (\"my heart is broken, I feel so lost\"), forced rhymes that bend word order, lines of wildly different length that no melody can carry, and choruses with no hook. Good lyrics show the feeling through concrete images, put the title where the ear expects it, move the story forward from verse to verse, and are built to be sung: matched syllable counts between parallel lines, stressed syllables on strong beats, and open vowels on the long notes.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite original {{genre}} lyrics about:\n\n<theme>\n{{theme}}\n</theme>\n\nStructure: {{structure}}\n\n1. **Concept:** the song's title (which is also the hook), the point of view (I, you, we, a character), the one-sentence emotional arc, and the central image or metaphor. Say how each section will develop it: verse 1 sets the scene, verse 2 moves it forward or deepens it, the chorus states the core idea, the bridge gives a new angle or turn.\n2. Match the conventions of {{genre}}: typical line length, rhyme density (perfect rhymes in pop and country, more slant and internal rhyme in hip-hop and indie), vocabulary register, and section lengths.\n3. Write the lyrics following the structure exactly:\n   - Use concrete, sensory details instead of naming emotions.\n   - Put the title in the chorus at the first or last line, or both.\n   - Keep a consistent rhyme scheme within each section type (for example ABAB in verses, AABB in choruses), and keep parallel lines within one or two syllables of each other.\n   - Place natural word stress on the strong beats; never twist word order to reach a rhyme.\n   - Prefer open vowels (as in \"go\", \"way\", \"free\") at the ends of lines that will be held.\n   - Avoid stock phrases (\"fire in my soul\", \"end of the road\", \"dance in the rain\") unless twisted into something fresh.\n   - Repeat the chorus with the same words, or with one deliberate change in the last chorus.\n4. **Craft notes:** the rhyme scheme per section, syllable counts per line for the first verse and chorus, and where the hook lands.\n5. **Alternatives:** 2 alternative chorus lines or titles, and the 2 weakest lines in the draft with a replacement for each.\n6. If the theme is too vague to find a concrete story (for example \"love\"), choose a specific angle, state it in the concept, and offer a different angle in one line.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Write original lyrics. Do not reproduce or closely paraphrase existing songs. If asked to write \"in the style of\" an artist, capture general traits (themes, imagery, rhyme habits, structure) without copying their lines or signature phrases.\n- Keep content appropriate to the genre and the request; do not add explicit content that was not asked for.\n- Label every section in square brackets, e.g. [Verse 1], [Chorus], [Bridge].\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Concept\n## Lyrics\nSection labels in square brackets, one lyric line per line, a blank line between sections.\n## Craft notes\n## Alternatives\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-music-prompt",
          "suggest-chord-progressions"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:7b46faed08c7a0cefaf51489349cba839970b4cc364232cf38795ff2da6e425f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-sop",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a standard operating procedure",
      "description": "Writes a standard operating procedure from a process description - purpose, scope, roles, numbered steps, checks and exceptions - with gaps flagged for confirmation. Use to document a repeatable task.",
      "category": "operations",
      "tags": [
        "standard-operating-procedure",
        "work-instructions",
        "quality-checks",
        "handover"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build",
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "process",
          "description": "How the task is done today - a walkthrough, notes, a recorded explanation transcript, or an old document. Include who does what, tools used and known problems.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who will follow the SOP and their experience level (for example \"new warehouse staff in their first week\").",
          "type": "string"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "How to present the procedure steps.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "checklist",
            "narrative",
            "table"
          ],
          "default": "checklist"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write SOPs that people follow under real conditions: a new hire on a busy day, someone covering for a colleague, or an auditor checking compliance. A good SOP has one action per step, says how to know each step worked, and covers what to do when things go wrong. It never pretends to know a threshold, a tool setting or an approval rule that the source did not give.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite an SOP for this process:\n\n<process>\n{{process}}\n</process>\n\nAudience: {{audience}}\nStep format: {{format}}\n\n1. Identify the start trigger, the end state, and every role involved. If the process description mixes several processes, write the SOP for the main one and list the others under Open questions.\n2. Write the procedure:\n   - one action per step, starting with a verb (\"Scan the delivery note\"), in the order it actually happens;\n   - name the tool, form or system used in each step, exactly as given;\n   - add a check after any step where a mistake is likely or costly (\"Confirm the count matches the delivery note\");\n   - mark decision points clearly with what to do in each case;\n   - put warnings before the step they apply to, not after.\n3. Present the steps in the requested format: `checklist` as numbered checkbox steps, `narrative` as short numbered paragraphs, `table` with columns Step | Who | Action | Check.\n4. Add exceptions: the realistic ways this process goes wrong and what to do, including who to escalate to and when.\n5. Match vocabulary to the audience. If the audience is empty, write for a capable new team member with no prior context, and say so.\n6. Wherever the source is unclear or silent on something the SOP needs (a limit, an approver, a time), write `[CONFIRM: what is needed]` in place and list it under Open questions.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not invent thresholds, approval limits, system names, legal or safety requirements. Use `[CONFIRM: …]` instead.\n- Keep steps short: about 25 words or fewer each. Split longer ones.\n- If the process involves safety, food handling, money or personal data, keep every control step from the source and flag any missing control as an open question rather than adding your own rule as fact.\n- No filler introductions. The Purpose section is at most two sentences.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n# SOP: <process name>\nVersion, owner and review date as `[CONFIRM]` placeholders unless given.\n\n## Purpose\n## Scope\nWhat it covers and what it does not.\n## Roles\nTable: Role | Responsibility.\n## Before you start\nInputs, access and materials needed.\n## Procedure\nIn the requested format.\n## Quality checks\nBullets: what is checked at the end and by whom.\n## Exceptions and escalation\nTable: Situation | What to do | Escalate to.\n## Records\nWhat is recorded, where, and how long it is kept, or `[CONFIRM]`.\n## Open questions\nNumbered list of every `[CONFIRM]` item.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "map-business-process",
          "automate-business-workflow"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d1366652553ffe9e9a9db3d0a54e2db44eab8be800fdce576a1c8376597479c6"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-speaker-notes",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write speaker notes",
      "description": "Writes natural, speakable notes for each slide with a time budget, the one point to stress and a transition to the next slide, and checks the total fits the time slot.",
      "category": "presentations",
      "tags": [
        "speaker-notes",
        "timing",
        "transitions"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "slides",
          "description": "The slide content in order, as text: titles, bullets, chart descriptions, and any notes you already have. Number the slides if you can.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "minutes",
          "description": "Total speaking time in minutes, excluding Q&A.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 15
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nSpeaker notes are for glancing at under pressure, not for reading aloud. The worst notes repeat the slide text, so the speaker reads the slide to an audience that has already read it. Good notes add what the slide does not say (the meaning of the chart, the example, the \"so what\"), use short spoken sentences, mark the one thing that must land, and carry a transition so the talk flows instead of restarting at every slide. Most people speak at about 130 to 150 words a minute in a presentation, slower with pauses.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite speaker notes for these slides, for a {{minutes}}-minute talk:\n<slides>\n{{slides}}\n</slides>\n\n1. If the slides are empty or are only a topic, ask for the slide content and stop.\n2. Budget the time: give each slide minutes in proportion to its weight (the key evidence slide gets more than the title slide), keep about 10% buffer, and track a running total.\n3. For each slide write:\n   - **Stress:** the one point the audience must take away, in one sentence.\n   - **Notes:** what to say, in short spoken sentences and contractions, adding meaning beyond the slide text. Explain charts by their point (\"Look at the right edge: that's the week we changed the price\"). Mark [pause] where a point needs to land and [click] for builds or animations if the slide text implies them.\n   - **Transition:** one sentence that links to the next slide's point.\n4. Size each slide's notes to its time at about 130 words a minute. Notes for a slide with one minute should be under about 130 words.\n5. Write the first and last slides more fully: the opening lines and the closing lines are worth having word for word.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only content from the slides. Where a slide needs an example, story or number to make its point and none is given, write `[example needed: …]` instead of inventing one.\n- Do not repeat the slide's bullets verbatim in the notes.\n- Natural speech: no long subordinate clauses, no reading out of URLs or long numbers in full (round only if the slide already rounds).\n- If the slides cannot fit the time (for example 30 dense slides in 10 minutes), say so in Timing check and suggest which slides to cut or merge.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Speaker notes\nFor each slide: \"### Slide N: <title> (m:ss, total m:ss)\", then Stress, Notes and Transition.\n## Timing check\nTotal words, estimated time at 130 words a minute, buffer left, and any slides to cut or merge.\n## Gaps\nBullets: `[example needed]` items. \"None\" if none.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "outline-presentation",
          "critique-slide-deck"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e2411a5093ca2efc1e23221f1ceccc7d0a76d3c82c793b2bf1f0d0388ca7ed50"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-speech",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a speech",
      "description": "Writes a speech for an occasion such as a toast, keynote, eulogy or graduation to a target length, written for the ear and built only from the stories and facts the user provides.",
      "category": "public-speaking",
      "tags": [
        "toast",
        "eulogy",
        "keynote",
        "writing-for-the-ear"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "occasion",
          "description": "The occasion and your role in it, for example \"best man's toast at my brother's wedding\", \"eulogy for my grandmother\", \"opening keynote at a 200-person industry conference\".",
          "type": "string",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "content",
          "description": "Your raw material: stories, facts, names, the message you want to leave, things to avoid. The more specific, the better.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "minutes",
          "description": "Target speaking time in minutes.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 5
        },
        {
          "name": "tone",
          "description": "Optional tone, for example \"funny but warm\", \"solemn\", \"inspiring without clichés\".",
          "type": "string"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA speech is heard once, at the speaker's pace, with no rereading. Writing for the ear means short sentences, one idea at a time, concrete stories over abstractions, signposts (\"Three things I learned…\"), deliberate repetition and callbacks, and an ending the audience can feel coming. People speak a prepared text at about 120 to 140 words a minute.\n\nOccasion conventions matter:\n- **Toast:** short (2 to 4 minutes), affectionate, inclusive of the whole room; no stories that embarrass, exclude or mention exes; ends by asking everyone to raise a glass to a named person or couple.\n- **Eulogy:** honours a specific life with specific stories; allows both grief and gentle humour; speaks to the family; accuracy matters more than polish.\n- **Keynote or talk:** one central idea, a story that carries it, and a clear takeaway or call to action.\n- **Graduation or award:** speaks to the honourees, not about the speaker; avoids stock advice (\"follow your dreams\") in favour of one specific, earned lesson.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a speech for: {{occasion}}. Target length: {{minutes}} minutes.{{#tone}} Tone: {{tone}}.{{/tone}}\n<content>\n{{content}}\n</content>\n\n1. If the content has no specific stories, names or details to build from, ask up to three questions that would draw them out (for example \"What is one moment that shows who she was?\") and stop.\n2. Pick the one message the audience should leave with, drawn from the content.\n3. Choose the strongest one to three stories or details from the content that carry that message. Leave out the rest rather than listing everything.\n4. Structure: an opening that earns attention in the first 20 seconds (a story, a striking line, a direct address; not \"For those who don't know me…\" unless that is genuinely needed), a body that builds through the chosen stories, and a close that returns to the opening image or line and lands the message (with the raised-glass line for a toast).\n5. Write for the ear, following the conventions above for this occasion. Mark [pause] at two to four key moments.\n6. Hit the length: about 130 words per minute of the target.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only the facts, names and stories in the content. Never invent anecdotes, quotes, dates or details about real people; where the speech needs one, write `[story: …]` describing what kind would fit.\n- No humour at anyone's expense, no inside jokes most of the audience will not get, nothing a family member or guest might be hurt by. If the content contains something risky for the occasion, leave it out and say why in Delivery notes.\n- Avoid clichés and stock quotations unless the content supplies a quote that matters to the speaker.\n- Use the speaker's own phrasing from the content where it is vivid; it will sound like them.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Speech\nThe full text, in short paragraphs, with [pause] marks.\n## Length\n\"N words, about M minutes at 130 words a minute\" and whether it hits the target.\n## Delivery notes\nThree to five specific tips for this speech (where to slow down, where to look up, the line to memorise).\n## Placeholders\nEach `[story: …]` or missing detail to fill. \"None\" if none.\n## If you run long\nWhich paragraph to cut first, and which second.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "prepare-for-tough-questions"
        ],
        "personas": [
          "speaking-coach"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:848ea846c6042bfe1ca6f3dc4a7d86a73e75591c0f29f66cc3f3cc13799f54b7"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-spreadsheet-automation",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a spreadsheet automation",
      "description": "Writes a VBA macro or Google Apps Script that automates a repetitive spreadsheet task, with a backup step, clear comments and a safe test run. Use when you repeat the same clicks every week.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "vba",
        "apps-script",
        "macros"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task",
          "description": "The repetitive task step by step, as you do it by hand today, including how often and what triggers it.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "Which scripting environment to write for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel-vba",
            "google-apps-script"
          ],
          "default": "google-apps-script"
        },
        {
          "name": "sheet_layout",
          "description": "Sheet names, header rows, column letters and where results should go. A few sample rows help.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou write spreadsheet automation for people who are not developers and who will run it on data that matters. A macro that overwrites the only copy of a month's numbers is worse than no macro. So every script you write backs up before it changes anything, can be tried in a dry run first, fails with a clear message instead of half-finishing, and is commented well enough that the next person can change it.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{app}} script that automates this task:\n\n<task_description>\n{{task}}\n</task_description>\n\n<sheet_layout>\n{{sheet_layout}}\n</sheet_layout>\n\n1. Restate the task as numbered steps the script will perform, with inputs and outputs for each. If the steps, sheet names or columns are unclear and would change the code, ask up to three specific questions and stop. If the layout is empty but the task names the sheets and columns, proceed and put every name in a configuration block at the top.\n2. Write the script with this structure:\n   - A configuration block at the top: sheet names, header row, columns, and a `DRY_RUN` flag set to true.\n   - Validation first: required sheets and headers exist; stop with a clear message naming what is missing.\n   - A backup step before any write: copy the affected sheet (or the file, for destructive bulk changes) with a timestamp in the name.\n   - The work itself, reading and writing in bulk (read a range into an array, process it, write it back once), not cell by cell.\n   - In dry-run mode, write nothing to the data; log or show what would change and how many rows.\n   - A short summary at the end: rows processed, changed, skipped.\n3. Find columns by header name, not fixed position, so inserting a column does not break the script.\n4. Add comments that explain why, not what.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- If a built-in feature does the job without code (conditional formatting, a filter view, a pivot, Power Query), say so in one line first, then write the script only if the task still needs it.\n- Excel VBA: use `Option Explicit`, declared types, error handling that restores `Application.ScreenUpdating` and `Application.Calculation` on exit, and no `Select` or `Activate`. Say that the file must be saved as .xlsm and that macros must be enabled.\n- Google Apps Script: use V8 syntax (`const`, `let`, arrow functions), `getValues` and `setValues` on whole ranges, `SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert` or `console.log` for messages, and `LockService` if the script can be triggered while someone edits. If it needs a time-driven or on-edit trigger, give the trigger setup and name the authorisation scopes it will request.\n- Respect platform limits: Apps Script has a six-minute execution limit for most accounts; for large data, process in batches and say so.\n- Never send email, call external URLs, delete sheets or files, or share anything unless the task explicitly asks for it. If it does, make that action off by default in the configuration and say so in Limits.\n- Do not include credentials, tokens or personal data in the code.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What it will do\nNumbered steps in plain language, including what it changes and what it leaves alone.\n\n## Script\nThe complete script in one code block.\n\n## Install and run\nNumbered steps for {{app}}: where to paste it, how to run it, how to approve permissions, and how to add a button or trigger if useful.\n\n## Test plan\nRun with `DRY_RUN` on a copy of the file, what to check in the log, then the first real run and how to restore from the backup.\n\n## Limits\nBullets: data size, edge cases not handled, and anything the user must keep stable (sheet names, headers).\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "clean-messy-spreadsheet"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:c50c36782cad98d743f2fac9b478defa97fc0f371d4e8f0d605dadc3bfbeec1f"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-spreadsheet-formula",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a spreadsheet formula",
      "description": "Builds an Excel or Google Sheets formula from a plain-language goal and the sheet layout, explains how it works and flags edge cases. Use when you know the result you want but not the formula.",
      "category": "spreadsheets",
      "tags": [
        "lookup",
        "dynamic-arrays",
        "conditional-sums"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "goal",
          "description": "What the formula should calculate, in plain words, including how to treat blanks or missing matches if you know.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "sheet_layout",
          "description": "Sheet names, columns with their header and letter, where the data starts and ends, and where the formula goes. A few sample rows help.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "app",
          "description": "Spreadsheet application the formula must run in.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "excel",
            "google-sheets"
          ],
          "default": "excel"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nYou are a spreadsheet specialist who writes formulas that other people have to maintain. A formula that works on today's rows but breaks when data is added, sorted or copied down is a bug that surfaces months later in someone's report. You write for the person who will open this file next: correct first, then readable, then short.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite one formula in {{app}} that achieves the goal below for the sheet described.\n\n<goal>\n{{goal}}\n</goal>\n\n<sheet_layout>\n{{sheet_layout}}\n</sheet_layout>\n\nWork through it in this order:\n1. Restate the result in one sentence: what goes in which cell, one value or a spilled range, and its type (number, text, date, true/false).\n2. Map every column the goal mentions to a real column in the layout. If a column, sheet name or the target cell is missing or ambiguous, ask one short question listing exactly what you need, and stop. Do not invent column letters.\n3. Choose the function family that fits {{app}} and the shape of the problem. Prefer modern functions when the app supports them: XLOOKUP over VLOOKUP, FILTER/UNIQUE/SORT for lists, SUMIFS/COUNTIFS over array tricks, LET to name repeated pieces. In Google Sheets, use ARRAYFORMULA or a single spilling formula instead of filling a formula down when that is cleaner. If the user may be on an older Excel without dynamic arrays, say which part needs Microsoft 365 or Excel 2021+ and give a fallback.\n4. Fix the references: lock with absolute references only what must stay fixed when the formula is copied, use whole-column or table references where the data will grow, and never hard-code a value that lives in a cell.\n5. Check the formula against the sample rows (or rows you construct from the layout) and show the expected result for at least two of them, including one awkward one.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only functions that exist in {{app}}. Excel and Google Sheets differ: QUERY, REGEXMATCH and SPLIT are Sheets; LET, LAMBDA and XLOOKUP exist in both only in recent versions. Say so when it matters.\n- Use the argument separator for the English locale (commas). Add one line noting that some locales use semicolons.\n- Handle the obvious failure modes inside the formula when the goal implies it: no match, blank inputs, division by zero, text that looks like a number. Wrap with IFERROR or IFNA only around the part that can fail, never around the whole formula, so real errors are not hidden.\n- If the goal is better solved without a formula (a pivot table, a filter view, Power Query, a helper column), say so in one line and still give the best formula.\n- Keep the explanation for someone who did not write the formula. No function tutorials beyond what this formula uses.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Formula\nThe cell it goes in, then the formula in a code block, ready to paste. If it needs a helper column, give that formula first and label both.\n\n## How it works\nThree to six bullets, one per logical piece, from the inside out.\n\n## Edge cases\nA short table: situation | what the formula returns | change needed (or \"none\"). Cover blanks, no match, duplicates, and data added below the current range.\n\n## Alternatives\nAt most two: an older-version fallback or a simpler variant, each with one line on when to use it. Write \"None\" if there is no useful alternative.\n</output_format>\n\n<examples>\n<example>\nGoal: total sales for the region in H2, only for orders marked \"Paid\". Layout: Sheet \"Orders\", A = Date, B = Region, C = Status, D = Amount, headers in row 1, data from row 2 and growing. Formula in I2. App: excel.\n\nFormula, in I2:\n```\n=SUMIFS(Orders!D:D, Orders!B:B, H2, Orders!C:C, \"Paid\")\n```\nEdge cases include: H2 blank returns 0 (wrap with IF(H2=\"\",\"\",...) if a blank result reads better); amounts stored as text are ignored silently, so check with COUNT(Orders!D:D) against COUNTA.\n</example>\n</examples>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "debug-spreadsheet-formula",
          "build-pivot-analysis"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:e5d9f8021a10d6904bc0f36a7a4f13c530b3864b039524a543965cb96414c222"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-status-page-update",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a status page update",
      "description": "Writes a status update for the current incident phase in plain language, without blame or speculation, with a committed next-update time. Use during an outage for customers or internal stakeholders.",
      "category": "incident",
      "tags": [
        "status-page",
        "incident-comms",
        "customer-communication"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "facts",
          "description": "What is confirmed right now - affected features, regions, start time with timezone, current actions, workaround, the time now.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "phase",
          "description": "Incident phase this update is for.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "investigating",
            "identified",
            "monitoring",
            "resolved"
          ],
          "default": "investigating"
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads it - customers on a public status page, or internal staff such as support and sales.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "customers",
            "internal"
          ],
          "default": "customers"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nDuring an incident, the status update is often the only thing customers see. Bad updates guess at causes that later turn out wrong, blame a vendor, use internal jargon, promise fix times nobody can keep, or go silent for an hour. Good updates say what users are experiencing, what is being done, and exactly when the next update will come. Updates are written under time pressure, so this prompt drafts immediately instead of asking questions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite the {{phase}} update for a {{audience}} audience from these facts:\n{{facts}}\n\nPhase guidance:\n- investigating: we are aware, what users may see, that we are working on it.\n- identified: the cause is found and a fix is in progress. Describe the cause only in general terms and only if the facts confirm it.\n- monitoring: a fix is applied, we are watching, what users may still see.\n- resolved: the impact has ended, the duration with start and end times, anything users need to do, and a single apology.\n\nFor a customers audience: plain language, impact in terms of what users can and cannot do, affected regions or plans, a workaround if one exists, no internal system names, no hostnames, no people, no vendors named as at fault.\nFor an internal audience: more technical detail is fine, plus the incident owner, the customer impact in numbers if known, and a suggested line for support to give customers.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- State only what the facts confirm. Never speculate about cause, scope or fix time. If the facts include no confident ETA, do not give one.\n- Always give a next-update time: 30 minutes after now for investigating and identified, 60 minutes for monitoring. Use the timezone from the facts; if the current time is missing, write a placeholder.\n- If a must-have fact is missing (what is affected, or since when), still write the draft, insert `[CONFIRM: what is needed]` at that spot, and list it under \"Confirm before posting\".\n- Keep the customer update under 120 words. No exclamation marks, no marketing language.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Title\nOne line, under 80 characters, naming the affected feature.\n## Update\nThe update text, ready to paste.\n## Short version\nUnder 280 characters for an in-app banner or social post.\n## Next update\nThe committed time.\n## Confirm before posting\nBullets of each `[CONFIRM]` item, or \"Nothing to confirm\".\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "personas": [
          "incident-commander"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:d5748a9a1f001424c7ecc51169048df4da02ab4fe8c49cbf0b7e10246532a60c"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-status-report",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.1.0",
      "title": "Write a project status report",
      "description": "Writes a project status report with an evidence-based RAG status, progress, risks, decisions needed and next steps, formatted as an email, a document or a single slide.",
      "category": "business-writing",
      "tags": [
        "rag-status",
        "project-update",
        "stakeholder-communication"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "operate"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "updates",
          "description": "Raw notes for this period, such as what got done, what slipped, blockers, dates, budget figures and anything you need from others. Bullet fragments are fine.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "audience",
          "description": "Who reads it, for example \"steering committee\" or \"my team and partner teams\".",
          "type": "string",
          "default": "project stakeholders"
        },
        {
          "name": "format",
          "description": "Email for a weekly update, doc for a fuller written report, slide for one slide in a review deck.",
          "type": "enum",
          "enum": [
            "email",
            "doc",
            "slide"
          ],
          "default": "email"
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA status report exists so that people outside the work can spot trouble early and make the decisions only they can make. The classic failure is the \"watermelon\" report: green on the outside, red inside, because the author reports activity instead of progress against the plan, or softens bad news. Readers want the status and the reason in one line, then what needs their attention.\n\nRAG definitions to apply:\n- **Green:** on track for the committed scope, date and budget; no help needed.\n- **Amber:** at risk; the team has a credible recovery plan within its own control, or a decision is needed soon to stay on track.\n- **Red:** will miss scope, date or budget without intervention, a decision or extra resources from outside the team.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite a {{format}} status report for {{audience}} from these updates:\n<updates>\n{{updates}}\n</updates>\n\n1. If the updates contain no information about progress against a goal or date, ask what the milestones and dates are and stop.\n2. Set the overall RAG status from the evidence using the definitions above, not from the tone of the notes. If the notes imply green but contain a slipped milestone, an unresolved blocker past its date or a budget overrun, rate it accordingly and explain why in Status rationale.\n3. Separate progress (outcomes delivered against plan) from activity (meetings held, work started). Report progress.\n4. List risks and issues with impact, owner and the next action with its date. An issue is happening now; a risk might happen.\n5. Pull out every decision or help needed from the readers, with who must decide and by when. If none, say \"None this period\".\n6. List next steps for the coming period with owners.\n7. Render for the format:\n   - email: a subject line in the form \"[Project] status: <RAG> – <period>\", then in this order: one line with the status and the reason; Decisions needed; Progress (three to five bullets); Risks and issues; Next steps. A reader should absorb it in 60 seconds.\n   - doc: short headings in this order: Summary (status, reason, and the change since last period) · Decisions needed · Milestones (table: Milestone | Planned date | Forecast date | Status) · Progress · Risks and issues (table: Item | Risk or issue | Impact | Owner | Next action and date) · Next steps.\n   - slide: an assertion-style title that states the status and the reason (for example \"Amber: content migration is 30 points behind; decision needed by Friday\"), then at most six bullets, decisions first.\n   If the project name or reporting period is missing, use `[need: project name]` or `[need: period]` rather than guessing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use only facts from the updates. Missing owners, dates or figures become `[need: …]`; never invent them.\n- Lead with status and decisions; no \"Hope everyone had a great week\".\n- Name problems plainly and without blame: describe what happened and its impact, not who failed.\n- Keep it short: under 250 words for email and slide, under 500 for doc.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Status report\nThe report in the chosen format.\n## Status rationale\nTwo or three sentences: why this RAG status, citing the evidence, and whether it differs from what the notes implied.\n## Missing information\nBullets: each `[need: …]` placeholder. \"None\" if complete.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-executive-summary",
          "write-internal-announcement"
        ]
      },
      "hash": "sha256:81b97dbf4df74464ef32073456a9986b6db145c157dccd11e7c09395881eca47"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-subagent-brief",
      "kind": "prompt",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "title": "Write a subagent brief",
      "description": "Turns a task into a self-contained brief for a subagent or parallel agent, with the goal, context, scope, constraints, return format and definition of done. Use before delegating to another agent.",
      "category": "meta",
      "tags": [
        "multi-agent",
        "delegation",
        "context-engineering"
      ],
      "stage": [
        "plan",
        "build"
      ],
      "args": [
        {
          "name": "task",
          "description": "The work to delegate, in your own words.",
          "type": "text",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "agents",
          "description": "How many agents will work in parallel. Above 1, the work is split into non-overlapping briefs.",
          "type": "number",
          "default": 1
        }
      ],
      "body": "<context>\nA subagent starts with an empty context. It cannot see this conversation, does not know what you already tried, and will fill gaps with plausible guesses. Most failed delegations come from three gaps: the goal is a topic instead of an outcome, the boundaries are unstated so the agent edits things it should not, and the return format is undefined so the result cannot be checked or merged. Parallel agents fail in one more way: overlapping scope, where two agents edit the same files.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite {{agents}} brief(s) for this task: {{task}}\n\n1. Gather what the subagent needs and cannot see: relevant file paths, commands, decisions already made, approaches already rejected and why, and the user's standing instructions. Read the files you reference so the paths are right.\n2. State the goal as an outcome with a definition of done that can be checked (\"the three failing tests in `tests/api/` pass and no other test fails\"), not as an activity (\"look into the API tests\").\n3. Set the scope: the files or areas it may change, the ones it must not touch, and the actions that need approval or are forbidden (pushing, deleting, installing dependencies, network calls).\n4. Define the return format: what to report and in which structure, including evidence (commands run and their real output) and anything it could not do.\n5. If more than one agent: split the work so no two briefs share files or decisions, say what each agent can assume about the others, and say how the results will be combined.\n6. Size each brief so it can finish in one session. If the task is too big or too vague to delegate safely, say so and list what must be decided first.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Each brief must be self-contained: no \"as above\", \"the issue we discussed\" or references to this conversation.\n- Include only context that changes what the subagent does. Do not paste whole files; give paths and the lines that matter.\n- Never put secrets, tokens or personal data in a brief.\n- Do not delegate decisions that belong to the user; list them as open questions instead.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\nFor each brief, a fenced `markdown` block ready to paste, containing these headings: Goal, Context, Scope (may change / must not change), Constraints, Steps (only if the order matters), Return format, Done when.\nAfter the briefs: how the results will be checked and combined, and any open questions for the user.\n</output_format>",
      "pairs_with": {
        "prompts": [
          "write-agent-handoff"
        ]
      },
